ME helper for daemon-sensitive CLI tests #test #daemon #reflection\n  P3 @01KJTP69 Create wait-for-startup helper for process/daemon tests #test #daemon #reflection\n\n--- Recent Activity ---\n  ✓ @01KJS8B9 Add dispatch rule flags to kspec meta add agent / meta set (just now)\n    ├─ be840eb feat(meta): add agent dispatch-rule CLI flags (11 minutes ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ 7d94099 feat(meta): add agent dispatch-rule CLI flags (#669) (just now, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS7PD Add --text flag to kspec session log show to replay agent output (26 minutes ago)\n    ├─ b20e5ad feat(session-log): add --text replay for session log show (1 hour ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ 74b5e76 feat: add --text replay mode to session log show (#668) (26 minutes ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS702 CLI DX: accept tags as alias for tag in batch commands (1 hour ago)\n    ├─ c56b6f8 feat(batch): accept tags alias in batch args (1 hour ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ bfb55d1 feat(batch): accept tags alias for tag in batch commands (#667) (1 hour ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS6ZZ CLI DX: accept P1/P2/P3 format as alias for numeric priority in batch commands (1 hour ago)\n    ├─ 14af23c feat(batch): WIP accept P* priority aliases in batch (3 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    ├─ 1af5278 test(batch): assert priority alias results via task list (2 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ d561c38 feat(batch): accept P1/P2/P3 priority aliases in batch (#666) (1 hour ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS6ZP Handle EPIPE in ACP framing layer — src/acp/framing.ts send() throws uncaught EPIPE when child proce (2 hours ago)\n    ├─ f05177c fix(acp): checkpoint EPIPE handling work (5 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ 6607641 fix(acp): handle EPIPE in JSON-RPC framing output path (#665) (2 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS6ZX CLI DX: add --description flag to task set command (currently requires task patch --data) (2 hours ago)\n    ├─ 00fed9c feat(task): add --description support to task set (3 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ d0a95d0 feat(task): add --description support to task set (#661) (2 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS6ZW kspec batch --commands '' hangs instead of erroring — empty/missing args should fail fast with a cle (2 hours ago)\n    └─ bbce145 fix(batch): fail fast when batch stdin input is missing (#660) (2 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS701 CLI DX: accept spec_ref as alias for spec-ref in batch commands (2 hours ago) - No implementation delta required. Verified on 2026-03-03 that batch args accept spec_ref alias for --spec-ref in task ad...\n  ✓ @task-dispatch-in-progress-priority Implement in_progress-first dispatch scheduling (3 hours ago)\n    ├─ cb67865 feat(dispatch): prioritize in-progress task dispatch (3 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    ├─ f1ad9c3 fix(dispatch): keep needs_work ahead of pending (3 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ d80157c Implement in_progress-first dispatch scheduling (#663) (3 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ○ eab566a fix(agent): harden dispatch autonomy and prompt timeout handling (#662) (3 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS6ZT kspec validate --json appends human-readable summary text after JSON payload, causing invalid JSON f (3 hours ago) - Merged in PR #659. Implemented structured-output purity fix for validate/lint so --json/--yaml no longer appends human-r...\n\n--- Inbox (oldest first) ---\n  57 untriaged | 67 deferred | 154 total\n\n  @01KJDZBA (5 days ago by @claude) [testing, ralph, infra]\n    Add second mock adapter registration for ralph tests. Currently --adapter-cmd registers a single 'custom' adapter, making it impossible to test different adapter IDs in real (non-dry-run) runs. Options: (1) --worker-adapter-cmd / --reviewer-adapter-cmd flags, (2) env-based adapter registration (e.g. KSPEC_TEST_ADAPTERS JSON), (3) allow --adapter-cmd to accept an optional name prefix like --adapter-cmd 'worker:node mock.js'. This would enable proper integration tests for ac-2 (reviewer subagent uses reviewer adapter), ac-7 (env injection for both adapters with different harness targets), and ac-12 (different adapter IDs in session metadata). Currently these are covered by code-level AC annotations and dry-run output tests, but lack end-to-end verification with the mock ACP.\n  @01KJF69V (5 days ago by @claude) [cli, validation]\n    kspec validate should warn about items with identical titles in the same module — two items both named 'Generation Progress UX' with different ULIDs caused confusion during a fix session in imgen project\n  @01KJG5A7 (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [bug, ralph, codex, sessions]\n    Task budget false-positive outside ralph loop when stale KSPEC_SESSION_ID is present (observed in codex session). Repro: with KSPEC_SESSION_ID set to an old session that has budget.json started_this_cycle=max_per_cycle, 'kspec task start @ref' fails with 'Task budget exhausted ... let iteration end naturally' even when not running ralph and no in-progress tasks. Expected: budget guard should apply only during active ralph loop iteration context, or stale/non-ralph session IDs should be ignored.\n  @01KJGA18 (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [bug, infra, skills, setup]\n    Diagnose skill verify drift/no-hash for plugin-provided core skills.\n\nObserved behavior:\n- kspec skill verify reports codex statuses as drifted/no-hash for help, observations, triage, reflect, triage-inbox, writing-specs.\n- kspec skill diff reports plugin-provided (no local render to diff).\n- Rendered skill files exist under .agents/skills.\n\nInvestigate:\n- render-hash semantics in .kspec/skills for plugin-provided skills\n- plugin-provided status handling in skill verify/status/diff paths\n- whether this is expected behavior or a bug in drift detection\n  @01KJGAE2 (4 days ago by @claude) [plan-import, automation, friction]\n    derive_from_specs produces sparse tasks: derived tasks get null description, no tags, automation unassessed. Agents picking up these tasks lack context to work independently. Consider: auto-populate description from spec description, copy plan tags, or allow plan document to specify per-task fields.\n  @01KJGEEQ0 (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, cli, spec]\n    Reflection follow-up: add explicit parent/ancestor context to kspec item get output (human and --json) so hierarchy is unambiguous without separate tree queries.\n  @01KJGEEQ21 (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, cli, ux]\n    Reflection follow-up: add optional ancestor-chain display for kspec item list --tree --search so matched descendants show parent context and avoid false orphan interpretation.\n  @01KJGEEQ2Y (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, validation, traits]\n    Reflection follow-up: add validation check for trait/command-contract contradictions (e.g., command AC prohibits --refs while applied traits require batch refs semantics).\n  @01KJGFKJ (4 days ago by @claude) [cli, ux]\n    Unified kspec get @ref dispatch: currently plans require 'kspec plan get' while specs/tasks use 'kspec item get' / 'kspec task get'. A single 'kspec get @ref' that auto-dispatches to the right entity type would reduce friction for agents and humans who don't know the entity type upfront.\n  @01KJGW8Z (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, testing, cli]\n    Add stale-dist guard for CLI-based tests: detect outdated dist/cli build in tests/helpers/cli.ts and fail fast with actionable guidance (or optional auto-build) to avoid misleading failures.\n  @01KJHAFV (4 days ago by @claude) [reflection, cli, workflow]\n    Meta workflow editing gap: kspec meta set cannot update workflow steps. Agents must delete+recreate workflow to edit a step, which can churn workflow ULIDs and temporarily break references. Add safe step-level update support (e.g., --set-step <index> --content/--on-fail) or preserve ULID on replace to avoid reference drift.\n  @01KJJHAR (3 days ago by @claude) [infra, cli, reflection]\n    Worktree guard escape hatch: add env var KSPEC_GUARD_OVERRIDE_DONT_USE that bypasses the guard hook for intentional recovery operations (git reset, rebase in .kspec). Name deliberately long to discourage agent use — humans-only for recovery scenarios.\n  @01KJK3YZ (3 days ago by @claude) [reflection, git, automation]\n    Add commit-scope guard for automated task work: before committing, detect pre-existing staged files unrelated to the current task and prevent accidental inclusion (or require explicit acknowledgment). In this iteration, unrelated staged files were included because they were already in the index before git add on task files.\n  @01KJK5XG (3 days ago by @claude) [reflection, testing, ralph]\n    tests/ralph.test.ts helper runRalph splits args on whitespace, so quoted values (e.g., --focus \"keep scope narrow\") break and cause misleading unknown-command/option failures. Improve helper to accept argv arrays or robust shell-like parsing for test args.\n  @01KJK84Y (3 days ago by @claude) [reflection, cli, tasks]\n    Reflection: fix-cycle ergonomics mismatch — task-work guidance says 're-submit' needs_work tasks, but CLI rejects kspec task submit when status is needs_work and requires explicit kspec task start first. Consider allowing direct submit from needs_work (auto-transition to in_progress) or improving error/help text to show required two-step command sequence.\n  @01KJKDBK (3 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, testing, sessions]\n    tests/cli/session-note-limit.test.ts has reproducible timeout instability in current baseline. During this iteration: full npm test timed out at lines 34/70/104, and isolated run timed out at lines 34/104 despite rebuilding. This appears separate from ralph prompt changes and indicates session-start note-limit path/perf regression or test flake that should be stabilized.\n  @01KJKKCB (3 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, cli, tasks]\n    Reflection: add file-based payload input to task notes (e.g., kspec task note @ref --file <path> and/or --stdin). Current command only accepts positional message, which makes multiline/special-character notes brittle and conflicts with the convention to use file-based payloads for safe shell handling.\n  @01KJKY1H (3 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, bug, batch, shadow]\n    Reflection follow-up: kspec batch can fail during copy-back with ENOTEMPTY on .kspec/sessions/<session-id> (observed while batching task start+note). The batch reports all subcommands succeeded, but exits non-zero and leaves shadow worktree in a temporarily broken/deleted-file state until manual recovery (e.g., git -C .kspec restore .). Improve batch copy-back robustness for session blob directories and avoid failing the batch when payload writes succeeded.\n  @01KJN378 (2 days ago by @claude) [bug, testing]\n    enhanced-setup.test.ts: 'should render core skills to codex output when codex is detected' fails — .codex/config.toml not created. Pre-existing failure (confirmed by stash test). Likely related to completed task @01KJEWKG — may need test update to match new codex config behavior.\n  @01KJN5MB (2 days ago by @claude) [reflection]\n    Fix pre-existing test failure: tests/enhanced-setup.test.ts 'should render core skills to codex output when codex is detected' fails with ENOENT on .codex/config.toml — test expects setup to create codex config but it is not being created. Confirmed pre-existing (not introduced by recent changes). Investigate whether codex config generation is missing or test fixture setup is incomplete.\n  Use: kspec triage inbox to triage untriaged items\n\n--- Observations (unresolved) ---\n  [friction] @01KJW0NZ (2 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Creating PRs with gh --body inline markdown containing backticks triggers shell interpolation; use --body-file for reliable automation.\n  [friction] @01KJVT2Q (3 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    CLI integration tests run against dist binary, so editing command options requires a full build before vitest; failures initially look like unknown-option regressions.\n  [success] @01KJVP3S (5 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Context-switch hygiene worked: checkpointing dirty work to a dedicated branch plus task-note handoff preserved progress and prevented losing work when switching priority tasks.\n  [friction] @01KJVB7XH (8 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Behavior-level AC intent can be missed when tests only verify internals. Add endpoint/log effect assertions earlier in task implementation to reduce fix-cycle churn.\n  [success] @01KJVB7XD (8 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Worker/reviewer loop with explicit MUST-FIX feedback improved quality: first review caught behavior-level AC gaps, second pass merged cleanly with clear audit trail.\n  [success] @01KJV1PFJ (11 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Task lifecycle loop (task-work + PR + spawned pr-review) provided clean end-to-end closure with merged PR and task completion in one pass.\n  [friction] @01KJV1PFE (11 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    PR review automation attempted APPROVE on self-authored PR and GitHub rejected it; review event selection should adapt to author/reviewer identity.\n  [success] @01KJTZYE (11 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Concurrency fix pattern: isolate race to a narrow atomic helper, migrate only hot mutation paths first, verify with targeted race regressions before full-suite run, then close via worker+review-agent loop.\n  [friction] @01KJTFVY (16 hours ago by @claude)\n    Testing CLI-layer decisions (e.g. isJsonMode() → onUpdate suppression) requires knowing which abstraction level to test at. Direct runInvocation unit tests give false positives; CLI subprocess tests hit adapter limitations; ESM spy approach is unreliable cross-module. The reliable pattern: use setJsonMode(true) + isJsonMode() from output.ts to replicate the exact CLI decision in-process, then assert on the result. Document this as the preferred pattern for testing CLI mode-gating behavior.\n  [success] @01KJSA6A (1 day ago by @claude)\n    Session log analysis for agent failure diagnosis: Python one-liner over events.jsonl extracted the exact tool call sequence and identified the ACP permission request gap in 2 minutes. The structured event log format made root cause analysis fast — tool_call status:pending -> 5min gap -> agent.failed was immediately diagnostic.\n  [friction] @01KJSA69X (1 day ago by @claude)\n    meta set has --add-skill but no --remove-skill. To fix a wrong skill slug on an agent, had to edit .kspec/kynetic.meta.yaml directly and trigger commit via task note. This is the second time a missing remove/clear flag forced a YAML edit (dispatch rules also have no CLI). These gaps accumulate quickly when wiring up new agents.\n  [friction] @01KJSA69R (1 day ago by @claude)\n    Skill slug namespace confusion: .agents/skills/ uses kspec- prefix (kspec-task-work) for rendered output, but .kspec/skills/ uses bare slug (task-work) for runtime injection. Agent definitions must use the bare slug — but nothing in the CLI or docs makes this distinction clear. Easy to wire up agents with wrong slug and get silent skill injection failure (skills silently skipped when file not found).\n  [friction] @01KJS6ESZ (1 day ago by @claude)\n    check-unresolved-comments CI check goes stale when review threads are resolved after the check already ran for that push. Fix: gh run rerun <run-id>. Reminder: resolve threads BEFORE or immediately after pushing to avoid this.\n  [friction] @01KJS6ESV (1 day ago by @claude)\n    Daemon packages/daemon/src/server.ts cannot be imported directly in vitest — transitive Bun/Elysia imports break module resolution. Pattern: recreate the logic as a pure function in the test file (see daemon-server.test.ts and daemon-web-ui-bundle.test.ts). Worth documenting as a testing convention for daemon code.\n  [friction] @01KJS6ESQ (1 day ago by @claude)\n    shx cp -r <src> <dest> is non-idempotent when dest already exists — creates nested subdirectory on repeated runs. Build scripts must always shx rm -rf <dest> before copying. Add to build/ci convention.\n  [success] @01KJRSMRE (1 day ago by @claude)\n    GraphQL thread resolution + empty commit pattern worked for stale CI: (1) kspec task submit → (2) resolve threads via GraphQL mutation → (3) push code or empty commit → (4) CI re-runs with threads resolved. Resolving threads before push avoids needing the empty commit.\n  [friction] @01KJRSMRA (1 day ago by @claude)\n    check-unresolved-comments CI check is triggered by push. If review threads are resolved AFTER a push (not before), CI does not re-run automatically. Workaround: empty commit to trigger fresh run. Correct order: resolve threads FIRST, then push code.\n  [success] @01KJRR5DG (1 day ago by @claude)\n    Fix cycle search-before-capture pattern worked well: searched for existing tracking of both friction points before capturing observations, confirmed neither was already tracked, then captured a single consolidated observation. This prevented duplication and kept the reflection tight.\n  [friction] @01KJRR5DC (1 day ago by @claude)\n    Fix cycle had two SHOULD-FIX items from regenerated files not committed: (1) kspec agents generate was run locally but not committed to the PR branch — reviewer caught stale kspec-agents.md with ralph references; (2) kspec skill render was not run at all — .agents/skills/ rendered files still had ralph references. Both are easy to miss because the commands succeed but leave uncommitted artifacts. Consider adding these steps to the pre-PR local-review checklist or the implementation checklist notes when working on migrations that touch templates/skills.\n  [friction] @01KJRF87 (1 day ago by @claude)\n    batch-write-buffer wiring blocked by subprocess spawn model: WriteBuffer is a process-local singleton but batch-exec.ts runs each command via spawnSync as a separate process. Buffer writes in the parent won't be visible to spawned child processes. Need to evaluate: (a) switch to in-process command execution, or (b) use a temp file protocol where children write to a staging dir and parent collects on success.\n  [friction] @01KJR8J2Y (1 day ago by @claude)\n    parseIntOption from src/cli/validators.ts repeatedly missed when implementing new CLI commands — parseInt+isNaN pattern introduced 4 times in one PR. Needs more prominent discovery surface (skill or convention).\n  [success] @01KJR8J2K (1 day ago by @claude)\n    Iterative Codex PR review loop (fix → push → re-review) converged in 2 passes and caught layered bugs not visible in the first pass. Good pattern for PR polish cycles.\n  [success] @01KJR42M (1 day ago by @claude)\n    /codex pr covering both tasks in a single pass was efficient and caught a subtle bug (parseInt partial-match \"5abc\"->5) that manual review missed. Dual-spec single-pass review avoids duplicated context-gathering and produces tighter non-redundant findings.\n  [success] @01KJR42C (1 day ago by @claude)\n    Reading actual code before judging PR review threads saved significant work: 11 of 19 open threads on PR #634 were stale (issues already fixed in the latest commit). Manual thread-by-code matching took ~10 minutes but avoided implementing fixes that were already done.\n  [friction] @01KJR425 (1 day ago by @claude)\n    agent.ts (PR #634) reimplemented parseInt+isNaN validation instead of using parseIntOption from src/cli/validators.ts — reintroducing the partial-parse bug (parseInt(\"5abc\")===5) that parseIntOption was built to fix. The helper exists and conventions doc was added after @01KGWDPV, but agents implementing new CLI commands still miss it. Suggests validators.ts needs more prominent callout in AGENTS.md or task templates for new CLI commands.\n  [friction] @01KJQWQ0 (1 day ago by @claude)\n    git -C <path> pattern prevents CWD poisoning in recovery scenarios. Agents running git inside .kspec/ via cd poison the shell CWD for the rest of the session (Claude Code persists CWD changes). Always use git -C /path/to/.kspec/ instead of cd .kspec/ && git. Consider adding this to AGENTS.md development conventions.\n  [friction] @01KJPK6MR (2 days ago by @claude)\n    Stale PR base masquerades as scope problem: when a feature branch is based on a commit that predates another merged PR, the merged PR files appear in the diff. Review says out-of-scope changes. Fix is rebase onto main, not code changes. Two fix cycles consumed before root cause identified. Consider adding a check in PR workflow to detect stale bases.\n  [success] @01KJPK6MM (2 days ago by @claude)\n    PR scope verification: use gh pr view N --json baseRefOid,headRefOid then git diff <baseRefOid>..<headRefOid> --name-only to verify actual PR diff. This is more reliable than git diff origin/main..branch when the branch base predates recent merges to main.\n  [friction] @01KJPH4Z (2 days ago by @claude)\n    Trait AC annotations (@trait-json-output, @trait-shadow-commit) consistently missed in new test describe blocks. Two consecutive tasks (agent-definition-schema, session-model-evolution) both flagged in fix cycle for missing annotations. Pattern: workers add spec-specific ACs but forget inherited trait ACs. Fix: consider checklist or template reminder when adding new integration test blocks.\n  [friction] @01KJN7J5D (2 days ago by @claude)\n    Item status in JSON output is a nested object {maturity: string, implementation: string} not a plain string. Tests checking item.status === \"implemented\" fail silently — must use item.status?.implementation. This differs from task status which IS a plain string.\n  [friction] @01KJN7J5A (2 days ago by @claude)\n    item list --json returns {items:[...]} (wrapped) while tasks list --json returns a flat array. Inconsistent JSON shapes cause test type errors — kspecJson<any[]> typed as flat array silently fails when the actual shape is an object wrapper. Test authors need to know to use {items: Array<...>} for item list vs any[] for tasks list.\n  [success] @01KJN360K (2 days ago by @claude)\n    Test-first approach for regression bugs: wrote tests that failed (confirmed bug), then fixed source, then verified tests pass. Clean feedback loop — 2 failing tests before fix, 6 passing after. Recommend this pattern for all shadow commit gap tasks.\n  [friction] @01KJN360F (2 days ago by @claude)\n    Verifying pre-existing test failures requires stashing changes and re-running — slow feedback loop. A baseline test report or CI artifact would let agents identify pre-existing failures without manual stash/unstash.\n  [success] @01KJN09EX (2 days ago by @claude)\n    Progressive reproduction technique (incrementally adding middleware layers to a minimal Elysia server) isolated an Elysia framework interaction bug in minutes. More effective than reading framework source code.\n  [friction] @01KJN09ER (2 days ago by @claude)\n    E2E tests not in CI allowed a fundamental WebSocket upgrade bug to persist undetected. Previous task closed as fixed based on unit tests passing, but E2E would have shown the server never upgraded connections.\n  [success] @01KJKCF1 (3 days ago by @claude)\n    Agent investigation breadth vs accuracy tradeoff — Explore agents are valuable for surveying many files quickly but their conclusions need manual verification before acting. In this session, the agent reported event listener accumulation as CRITICAL (wrong — listeners registered once per spawn) while the actual bottleneck (getEventCount O(n^2)) wasn't in its top findings. Pattern: use agents for breadth, verify claims against actual code, then form your own root cause hypothesis.\n  [success] @01KJK7F4T (3 days ago by @claude)\n    Minimal floating-promise fix pattern: storing async function result in a variable and attaching .catch(() => {}) before Promise.race is the clean way to prevent Node.js 22 unhandled rejection crashes when a race-loser promise is later rejected by resource cleanup.\n  [friction] @01KJK7F4J (3 days ago by @claude)\n    Task budget guard blocks needs_work fix cycle transitions. kspec task start @ref fails with budget exhausted even when the task is in needs_work state (an existing fix cycle, not a new task start). needs_work → in_progress should be exempt from the budget guard since it is resuming prior work, not starting new work. Had to skip task state transitions and create PR without proper submit flow.\n  [success] @01KJJT51P (3 days ago by @claude)\n    Codex-then-fix cycle on PR #601 was tight: review posted, ac-9 gap caught, fixed in 1 commit, thread resolved, CI passed, merged. Total turnaround ~15 min including CI.\n  [friction] @01KJJT51H (3 days ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review cannot distinguish pre-existing test failures from regressions. Flags false positives (e.g. session-budget-integration flakiness on PR #590). Could pre-seed known-flaky test list or compare failure counts against main baseline.\n  [friction] @01KJJT519 (3 days ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review takes 10+ minutes because npm test subagent polling is inefficient. Consider instructing Codex to skip full suite and rely on CI, or use test shards instead of full npm test.\n  [friction] @01KJJHARM (3 days ago by @claude)\n    Merge driver silent data loss when --non-interactive flag missing from git config. No error, no warning — just hangs on stdin and leaves files unmerged. 12 task completions lost silently. Root cause: shadow.ts registration code path diverged from actual git config. Fix tracked in @01KJJGJ1.\n  [success] @01KJJHARH (3 days ago by @claude)\n    Isolated worktree + backup branch pattern for shadow branch recovery. Creating a backup branch before destructive ops, then using /tmp worktree for experimental rebase, then patching the result back as a normal commit avoided force-push entirely. This pattern should be documented for future shadow branch divergence scenarios.\n  [success] @01KJGW8ZF (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Using a focused validate mode matrix (schema/refs/alignment/completeness/staleness + combinations) quickly verified scoped semantics and prevented regressions while keeping turnaround fast.\n  [friction] @01KJGW8ZB (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    CLI tests that execute dist artifacts can produce misleading failures when dist is stale; this creates avoidable debugging churn before the real code behavior is evaluated.\n  [success] @01KJGQKP (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Targeted dry-run + focused test loop quickly exposed core-skill source-of-truth drift; package-distribution test caught stale plugin artifact path immediately.\n  [success] @01KJGQCA7 (4 days ago by @claude)\n    Iterative codex review loop (review → fix → re-review) converges well. Three rounds on PR #582 caught duplicate label, spec alignment drift, and missing test branch.\n  [friction] @01KJGQCA1 (4 days ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review threads require manual GraphQL resolution + CI re-trigger before merge. Multi-step dance every time codex posts inline comments.\n  [success] @01KJGFKJ (4 days ago by @claude)\n    Holistic plan review (plan + derived specs + tasks + traits together) before any code is written caught 6 spec gaps in @plan-auto-close-stale-active-sessions including missing targeting semantics, liveness guard, corrupt events handling, and task dependency ordering. Pattern: evaluate the full derivation tree, not just individual specs.\n  [friction] @01KJGAKM (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Core skill update UX gap: kspec skill update is version-gated, so same-version template edits are skipped and require kspec skill install-core. This is non-obvious during local template iteration. Improve guidance (update vs install-core) or adjust command behavior to detect local template changes.\n  [friction] @01KJGAE2A (4 days ago by @claude)\n    Plan-derived tasks need manual follow-up (batch set tags + note) to be automation-ready. The derive step should produce standalone tasks.\n  [success] @01KJGAE26 (4 days ago by @claude)\n    Parallel plan review (Plan agent + Codex) catches complementary issues: architectural concerns vs format/structural issues. High convergence on edge cases gives confidence. Worth standardizing as a dual-review step.\n  [success] @01KJEVMQ (5 days ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Portable {skill:<id>} tokens with renderer-side platform resolution reduced template duplication and kept Claude/Codex invocation guidance consistent across SKILL.md and supporting markdown.\n  [friction] @01KJEVM2 (5 days ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    When posting content containing $ and angle-bracket placeholders via shell-wrapped commands, interpolation can silently mutate task/PR notes; prefer literal-safe quoting or file-based payloads.\n  [success] @01KJDZV0A (5 days ago by @claude)\n    Plan-derived task descriptions with exact line numbers and numbered change steps make implementation fast and focused. The plan-to-task pipeline produces high-quality work items.\n  [success] @01KJDZV06 (5 days ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review as iterative second reviewer catches real bugs. Two-round pattern (review → fix → re-review) provides high-confidence quality gate for ralph adapter/env code.\n  [success] @01KJDXCBB (5 days ago by @claude)\n    MOCK_ACP_VERIFY_ARGS_FILE pattern extends the existing env var verification pattern cleanly — behavioral test infrastructure for ACP agent spawning is now solid.\n  [success] @01KJDXCB7 (5 days ago by @claude)\n    Well-decomposed task descriptions with exact files and verification criteria make implementation fast — the per-adapter-auto-approve task took one focused session from start to merged PR.\n  [success] @01KJDV6R (5 days ago by @claude)\n    Codex two-pass review pattern effective: implement → codex review → fix bug found → re-review clean. Caught missing previousValue in injectCodexEnv that would have broken Ralph cleanup.\n  [success] @01KJDR3S (5 days ago by @claude)\n    Triage record-then-act pattern with UI review works well at scale: batch-record 41 decisions, user reviews/overrides in web UI, batch-act on confirmed. Clean separation of agent proposals from user decisions. 12 overrides out of 41 shows useful agent-human collaboration.\n  [question] @01KJDQ14 (5 days ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] kspec meta set convention: add --update-rule and --remove-rule flags. Currently the only way to fix a rule is to delete the entire convention and recreate it. Surfaced during skill CLI lookups task when codex review caught a wording issue in one rule.\n\nReasoning: Convention --update-rule — DX improvement, workaround is delete+recreate\n  [question] @01KJDQ12 (5 days ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] @cmd-validate spec is stale — missing flags: --completeness, --staleness, --drift, --alignment, --skills, --conventions (all already implemented in code). Pre-existing drift, not introduced by trait AC task.\n\nReasoning: @cmd-validate spec is stale — missing 6 flags already in code. Spec drift.\n  [question] @01KJDQ119 (5 days ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] Triage API spec says ac-4 and ac-5 use PUT method but implementation uses POST /:ref/override and POST /:ref/act. Spec needs updating to match reality.\n\nReasoning: Triage API spec PUT vs POST mismatch — spec needs updating to match implementation\n  [question] @01KJDQ114 (5 days ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] Triage ac-5 says sets result_ref but delete action may not produce one. Verify behavior and update spec or add test assertion.\n\nReasoning: Triage ac-5 result_ref for delete — spec may be wrong, needs verification\n  [question] @01KJDQ0ZZ (5 days ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] Plan import: generate richer task descriptions from Implementation Notes section — include file paths, what to change, verification steps instead of generic 'See plan...' text\n\nReasoning: Plan import richer descriptions — improvement, not blocking\n  [question] @01KJDQ0ZT (5 days ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] Merge driver: auto-resolve .kspec-agents-hash file conflicts (deterministic hash, should always take latest)\n\nReasoning: Merge driver for hash file — low frequency conflict, manual resolution works\n  [success] @01KJDN04 (5 days ago by @claude)\n    Dual review pattern (plan agent + Codex) catches complementary issues. Plan agent validates design, Codex validates contracts and test coverage. Worth keeping as standard practice for non-trivial PRs.\n  [success] @01KJDFFJK (5 days ago by @claude)\n    Shadow branch merge driver handles complex YAML conflicts (project.tasks.yaml with 38 divergent commits) automatically during rebase — reliable for ralph parallel sessions.\n  [success] @01KJDFFJG (5 days ago by @claude)\n    Dual review pattern (internal plan review + /codex review-plan) catches complementary issues — format vs substance. Worth standardizing for all plan imports.\n  [friction] @01KJD933 (5 days ago by @claude)\n    PR merged by review subagent while worker agent still editing files on same branch — local branch switches to main automatically, all pending Edit tool changes silently lost. Caused significant rework this session (re-applying all triage.spec.ts changes on new branch fix/triage-ac6-session-close-error). The review subagent and worker agent operate concurrently on the same branch in a fix cycle, creating a race condition. Potential mitigations: (1) worker agent checks for branch changes before editing, (2) ralph serializes review subagent after worker fully stops, (3) worker agent uses a local patch/stash before any branch changes.\n  [friction] @01KJD6M8 (5 days ago by @claude)\n    When deleting test files that cover spec ACs, kspec validate does not warn that AC annotations are being removed. This means coverage regressions are only caught during Codex review after the PR is up — requiring an extra fix commit. A pre-delete validate or a local-review gate that checks 'these AC annotations would disappear' would catch this earlier. Observed during task-delete-static-analysis-tests (PR #558): deleted web-ui-triage.test.ts removed all @interactive-triage-ui ac-1..ac-8 coverage; Codex caught it, required creating triage.spec.ts to recover.\n  [success] @01KJD309R (5 days ago by @claude)\n    window.__testWs pattern for Playwright WebSocket E2E: Store native browser WebSocket in window.__testWs via page.evaluate() and access it across subsequent evaluate() calls. Browser context maintains the global between calls. Enables clean helper functions (connectWebSocket, sendAndWaitForAck, subscribeAndWaitForBroadcast) without needing Playwright WebSocket interception. Used successfully in packages/web-ui/tests/e2e/api-websocket.spec.ts.\n  [friction] @01KJD309N (5 days ago by @claude)\n    CI check-unresolved-comments timing race in review fix cycle: When Codex posts inline review comments on a PR, we push a fix commit which triggers CI immediately. The check-unresolved-comments job runs BEFORE we resolve the Codex threads via GraphQL, causing it to fail on the new commit. Then requires manual workflow re-run. Mitigation: always resolve all review threads BEFORE pushing any fix commits during a review cycle.\n  [friction] @01KJD309G (5 days ago by @claude)\n    JS date validation gotcha in E2E tests: new Date(\"invalid\") returns Invalid Date but does NOT throw. Tests using expect(() => new Date(x)).not.toThrow() or try/catch will always pass even with malformed timestamps - false positive. Always use isNaN(new Date(x).getTime()) for timestamp format validation. Caught this in api-websocket.spec.ts timestamp assertion (AC-29/@trait-websocket-protocol ac-3).\n  [success] @01KJCXMR (5 days ago by @claude)\n    Codex retrospective PR review workflow: /codex pr on merged PRs surfaces real issues. Found stale dist/ bug via retro review of PR #547 that live review missed.\n  [friction] @01KJCVXZ (5 days ago by @claude)\n    AC annotation verification gap: Tests annotated with wrong AC refs (e.g., Elysia 422 tests annotated as ac-23 when ac-23 requires 400+{error,details}; POST body validation annotated as ac-5/6/7 when those are for X-Kspec-Dir header). Fix: always run kspec item get @spec-ref and read AC text before annotating tests.\n  [friction] @01KJC3JS (6 days ago by @claude)\n    Trait N/A annotations placed inside JSDoc block comments are not machine-parseable — the '* // AC:' pattern (where * is the JSDoc line prefix) does not match the scanner's '// AC:' regex. Must use standalone // AC: line comments OUTSIDE any /** ... */ block. Occurred in api-meta.spec.ts E2E tests, caught by Codex review, required fix commit.\n  [friction] @01KJBW3RX (6 days ago by @claude)\n    scanTestCoverage() only scanned rootDir/tests/ — missed packages/web-ui/tests/e2e/ where Playwright E2E specs with AC annotations live. Fixed by extending scan to also cover the E2E directory and .spec.ts/.spec.js file extensions. Risk: any future E2E test directory additions would silently miss coverage until the scan list is updated. Consider making E2E scan paths configurable or convention-documented.\n  [success] @01KJBW3RS (6 days ago by @claude)\n    GitHub PR thread resolution requires GraphQL mutation (resolveReviewThread), not REST API. REST API only supports replies. Pattern: get thread node IDs via graphql query on pullRequest.reviewThreads, then loop resolveReviewThread mutations. Reliably resolved all 7 threads in one pass.\n  [friction] @01KJBTMZ (6 days ago by @claude)\n    Elysia body schema validation returns 422 (not 400) before the handler runs. When writing E2E tests for daemon endpoints that have body schema validation (t.Object(...)), missing/invalid body fields return 422. If the handler also has manual validation that returns 400, the schema check fires first. Account for this in test assertions — do not assume 400 for missing body fields on Elysia-validated routes.\n  [success] @01KJBJX0 (6 days ago by @claude)\n    Self-validating task pattern: created a P1 automation-eligible task whose acceptance criteria IS verifying the fix works. Ralph picks it up, the task checks its own session_id stamp, and the result documents whether the fix works end-to-end. Useful for testing harness-level integration that can't be verified from inside an active session.\n  [friction] @01KJB02B (6 days ago by @claude)\n    testUlid('PREFIX') returns identical values across calls without explicit sequence argument — deterministic with default sequence=0. Caused false-positive test pass in session-checkpoint-scoping tests where mySession and otherSession were identical. Fix options: auto-incrementing counter, require sequence param, or rename to testUlidDeterministic to make behavior obvious. testUlids() works correctly but is easy to overlook.\n  [success] @01KJAAGK (6 days ago by @claude)\n    Session test helper extraction (task @01KJ9ZDE) went smoothly. The all-fields-optional SessionContext pattern works well for tests that each need a different subset of fields — TypeScript structural typing handles it naturally. Reduced ~315 lines of duplication across 6 files into 2 shared modules. The inbox helpers needed a tempDir parameter change (closure → explicit arg) which was the main mechanical transformation.\n  [friction] @01KJA9BT (6 days ago by @claude)\n    Codex skill silently exits with no output when OPENAI_API_KEY is not set. The skill documents gh auth preflight but not API key validation. Agents waste time discovering this. Skill should check for API key and fail fast with clear message.\n  [success] @01KJA6SX (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Parallel audit agents pattern: spawning 4 specialized agents (code quality, test quality, non-test code, process quality) in parallel gives comprehensive coverage in ~2 min wall clock. Each agent needs clear scope boundaries and structured output format.\n  [friction] @01KJ8XQF (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Cross-cutting skill updates need checklist: When a concept spans multiple skills (e.g., spec context discovery in codex, pr-review, local-review, review template), updating one skill without checking all related ones leads to inconsistency. Codex review on PR #511 found 4 findings where the dig-deeper pattern wasn't applied uniformly. Consider: a way to tag related skills, or a pre-commit check that flags partial updates to skill groups.\n  [friction] @01KJ8X52 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Session checkpoint hook fires repeatedly on research-only sessions with pre-existing uncommitted changes from a different task. No way to signal this is a read-only investigation session.\n  [friction] @01KJ8R05 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Plan-derived tasks with depends_on sometimes become no-ops because the dependency task already implements all shared spec ACs. Seen in iteration 3: @update-setup-with-migration-cleanup (all 6 ACs done by @implement-native-worktree-guard) and @add-multi-harness-env-injection (all injection ACs done by @implement-session-create-command). Wastes budget starts and discovery time. Potential fix: plan import could warn when multiple tasks share a spec and one depends on the other, or task start could auto-detect 'AC already covered' and offer to close.\n  [success] @01KJ8QR2 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Pure function pattern for hook/guard commands: exporting the core logic as a testable pure function (e.g. evaluateWorktreeGuard) with stdin/process handling in the CLI layer enables fast unit testing (44 tests, no I/O) while keeping the CLI integration tests minimal (7 tests for protocol compliance). Replicate this pattern for future hook commands.\n  [success] @01KJ8NDT (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review caught exec()+unref() not actually preventing event loop hang (stdio pipes still buffered). Validated that /codex review-pr adds real value beyond rubber-stamping.\n  [friction] @01KJ8J44G (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Ralph CLI uses --max-loops but internal variables and log messages say iterations. Minor naming inconsistency that causes confusion when writing tests or debugging. Example: --max-loops 1 but logs Starting ralph loop (max 1 iterations).\n  [success] @01KJ8J44C (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Mock ACP agent pattern (tests/fixtures/mock-acp-agent.mjs) enables fast behavioral testing of ralph subprocess orchestration. Tests real env injection, budget cleanup, and signal handling in ~2s each. Key enabler: --adapter-cmd flag + spawnRalphUntil helper that watches output markers then kills the process.\n  [friction] @01KJ8EE7 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    setupTempFixtures() returns a flat dir where tempDir IS the specDir — no .kspec/ wrapper. Agents that assume nested structure (path.join(tempDir, \".kspec\")) get silent test failures. AGENTS.md test helpers table should note this.\n  [success] @01KJ82GP (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Parallel agent spawning for session log audit: 5 agents analyzing different log files simultaneously gave comprehensive coverage. Pattern worth replicating for any multi-file analysis task.\n  [success] @01KJ77DA (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Budget enforcement in task start integrates cleanly: check before transition, increment after save, resume case handled by existing early return. The library functions (checkBudget/incrementBudget) do the heavy lifting — CLI handler just wires them together. Good separation of concerns between store.ts and task.ts.\n  [success] @01KJ75RQ (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Dual plan review (internal agent + /codex review-plan) catches complementary issues: internal finds design gaps and terminology problems, codex finds code-level feasibility issues (missing APIs, wrong method names, breaking consumers). Convergent findings indicate high confidence. Used successfully for session-start-redesign plan.\n  [friction] @01KJ75K2 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    task set --depends-on replaces all deps rather than appending. When setting multiple deps in separate batch commands, only the last one survives. Must provide all deps in a single call. Consider adding --add-depends-on for append semantics (similar to --relates-to on item set which appends).\n  [friction] @01KJ73ME (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Ralph task-limit-guard hook blocks gh pr create even when finishing current task work. The guard treats all Bash commands equally — it can't distinguish 'creating a PR for completed work' from 'starting new task work'. Result: branch pushed but PR not created, requiring next iteration to finish. Consider whitelisting gh pr create or detecting PR creation context in the guard.\n  [success] @01KJ731A (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Codex review caught fail-open bug in getBudget() — corrupt budget.json was silently treated as 'no budget', effectively disabling enforcement. Changed to fail closed (only ENOENT returns null, parse/validation errors propagate). Pattern: for security/enforcement code, always default to the restrictive path. Codex consistently catches error-handling path issues that internal review misses.\n  [friction] @01KJ71CS (1 week ago by @claude)\n    @trait-semantic-exit-codes ACs hardcode exit code numbers (ac-2: 'exit code 1', ac-3: 'exit code 2', ac-4: 'exit code 3') that don't match kspec's actual EXIT_CODES scheme (ERROR=1, USAGE_ERROR=2, NOT_FOUND=3, VALIDATION_FAILED=4). Every command adding trait AC test coverage needs clarifying comments explaining the mapping. Fix: update trait ACs to use semantic descriptions ('non-zero exit code indicating validation failure') instead of hardcoded numbers, or update to match kspec's actual codes.\n  [success] @01KJ63RWA (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Dual review pattern (internal agent + Codex) for plan review catches different issue categories. Internal agent found consistency gaps; Codex found concurrency risks. Convergent findings give high confidence before implementation.\n  [success] @01KJ63RW7 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Parallel subagent audit pattern: launching 4+ agents in parallel for deep-dive review of ralph loop work (PR quality, functional verification, loop behavior, process adherence) produced comprehensive results in ~90 seconds. Effective for post-loop quality assessment.\n  [success] @01KJ5B3S (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review caught a real type drift bug (Convention.validation schema mismatch) during web-ui type deduplication PR. The shared-type approach works well: define in @kynetic-ai/shared, re-export from web-ui, enforce with static analysis test. Dual review (Codex + CI) catches complementary issues.\n  [friction] @01KJ51NB (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Post-merge branch state: When inheriting a session where a PR was already merged, the working directory may be on main. Always check branch (git branch --show-current) before committing, and create a feature branch first. Recovering from an accidental main commit requires creating a branch from HEAD and resetting main.\n  [friction] @01KJ50B8 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    settings.json written by 3 independent subsystems during setup (installClaudeCodeHooks, seedPermissions, enablePluginInProject via modifyJsonAtomic). Makes skip-when-unchanged optimization impossible without either a shared coordinator or consolidating all mutations into one function. Each writer reads, modifies, writes independently — interleaving prevents simple content comparison.\n  [success] @01KJ4VDK (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Pre-analyzed triage report (HTML artifact) combined with kspec batch made processing 107 inbox items efficient. Record-then-act pattern provided good audit trail. Pattern: analyze offline, port decisions via batch.\n  [question] @01KJ4SNF (1 week ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] Design section configuration for agents generate — allow projects to control which sections appear (skills table, conventions, workflows, static templates). Key questions: where does config live, include vs exclude list, defaults, custom sections, ordering, data vs template distinction, migration path. See plan doc for full requirements.\n\nReasoning: Must-have but needs spec design - section configuration for agents generate\n  [question] @01KJ4SNE (1 week ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] kspec validate @spec or @task — targeted validation that checks a single spec/task's AC coverage against codebase. Not full approval, just verifies each AC has at least one // AC: annotation in test files. Would make trait AC gaps immediately visible during task-work. Could be used by local-review skill as a concrete check instead of relying on agent diligence.\n\nReasoning: Must-have but needs spec design - targeted kspec validate for single item\n  [question] @01KJ4SJP5 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] Cross-feature requirement pattern: How to express that core features (search, validate, export) must support all entity types. When new entity type added, should automatically imply tasks for core features. Options: (1) applies_to field on feature specs, (2) trait on entity types like @trait-searchable, (3) validation warning for missing coverage, (4) parent spec that entity types must satisfy. Surfaced when realizing search doesn't cover inbox.\n\nReasoning: first item reasoning\n  [question] @01KJ4SJP2 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] Include triage records in static snapshot (kspec-snapshot.json). Currently the snapshot format (KspecSnapshot type) doesn't include triage records, so the triage page in static/GitHub Pages mode shows an empty state with a notice instead of displaying existing triage decisions read-only. AC-8 of @interactive-triage-ui expects read-only display. Needs: add triage records to export snapshot, update KspecSnapshot type, add fetchTriageRecordsStatic to read from snapshot.\n\nReasoning: There's a spec gap here\n  [friction] @01KJ3X7D (1 week ago by @claude)\n    CI timing race: when Codex posts a review comment after CI check-unresolved-comments has already run, the check fails because the new thread is unresolved. Requires manual re-run after resolving. Consider: (1) make check-unresolved-comments a required-last check, (2) have the review workflow auto-re-run the check after resolving threads, or (3) add a gh run rerun step to the codex review skill.\n  [idea] @01KJ3T9X (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Spec gap test\n\nReasoning: needs spec\n  [success] @01KJ3M13 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Zod superRefine pattern for status-dependent field requirements (e.g., triaged requires reasoning/decided_by, acted_on requires acted_at). Should be the default approach for any schema with status lifecycle — flat .optional() misses state invariants. First used in triage.ts, should apply to plan.ts and similar.\n  [success] @01KJ331N (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Parallel subagent research pattern: launching 3+ research agents simultaneously to investigate different aspects (code state, git history, task completions) builds comprehensive evidence in ~3 minutes vs 15+ sequentially. Critical for large triage sessions (95 items). Each agent gets a focused investigation scope and returns structured findings.\n  [friction] @01KJ1RW3 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Meta vs manifest confusion: skill render reads from meta (shadow branch), not template manifest. Changing manifest ID without updating meta has no effect. Need to delete+re-add skill via CLI.\n  [friction] @01KJ1A32 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Platform behavior assumption: PR #440 added namespace rendering (.claude/skills/kspec/<id>/) without verifying Claude Code actually discovers nested skill directories. It does not. Future platform integration work should verify discovery behavior (e.g. create a test skill, check if Claude Code lists it) before building rendering pipelines around assumed directory structures.\n  [friction] @01KHZHZA (1 week ago by @claude)\n    task get not showing descriptions meant had to grep raw YAML to verify task content after batch creation. Critical gap for agents working on tasks — they cannot read the description via CLI.\n  [success] @01KHZBEE (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Spec-before-implement pattern: adding ac-6 to @agent-instruction-gen before coding the noop behavior made test coverage natural and caught the right contract upfront.\n  [success] @01KHZ6H8 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Convention vs template mental model clarified: Templates carry kspec-generic content (shadow branch, task lifecycle, ralph loop — same for ALL kspec projects). Conventions carry project-specific rules (testing gotchas, dev setup, CI limitations — unique per project). When porting content from AGENTS.md, ask: does this apply to every kspec project? Template. Only this project? Convention.\n  [success] @01KHYTNN (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Dual review convergence: running both pr-review subagent and /codex review in parallel catches different issues and convergent findings give high confidence. Both flagged dead verifyTaskCompleted code independently.\n  [success] @01KHWVQP6 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Parallel review audit pattern: spawning 4 specialized subagents (worker process adherence, review process adherence, code quality, spec/AC coverage) to evaluate a ralph session. Each agent had a focused lens and found complementary issues. Total wall-clock time ~2 min for what would have been 15+ min of sequential analysis.\n  [success] @01KHWVQP3 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Dual-reviewer pattern (Claude agent + Codex) for PR review. Running both in parallel on PR #424 gave convergent findings on the critical branch-lookup bug (independent discovery by both). Codex also caught input validation gap that Claude missed. Different models find different issues — convergent findings give high confidence, divergent findings expand coverage.\n  [friction] @01KHWVQN (1 week ago by @claude)\n    GitHub blocks REQUEST_CHANGES on own PR. Ralph review subagents operate under the same GitHub credentials as the PR author, so they cannot post REQUEST_CHANGES reviews — only COMMENT. The pr-review skill instructs posting APPROVE or REQUEST_CHANGES, but REQUEST_CHANGES will always fail in self-authored PRs. Need either: (1) a separate GitHub bot account for review agents, or (2) the skill should use COMMENT event with structured review content instead of REQUEST_CHANGES.\n  [success] @01KHW3CCB (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Post-import verification agent catches data integrity issues that dry-run misses (null spec_ref, missing implementation notes). Should be part of standard plan import workflow.\n  [success] @01KHW3CC8 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Dual review pattern (internal Plan agent + Codex review-plan) catches complementary issues. Internal agent finds architectural concerns, Codex finds format/structure issues. Convergent findings give high confidence. Worth standardizing for all plan imports.\n  [success] @01KHVYHKH (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Parallel consumer project exploration: launching 3 agents to explore kynetic-bot, imgen, comfy-ecaj-nodes simultaneously produced convergent findings (same Quick Start pattern, same hooks, same skill drift) that were far more convincing than exploring one project. Use parallel exploration when researching cross-project patterns.\n  [success] @01KHVYHKE (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Dual review pattern before plan import: running both an internal plan review agent and Codex in parallel caught YAML quoting failures and substantive spec gaps (batch mode, worktree detection, remote type algorithm). Reviews converged on same must-fixes, giving high confidence. Always dual-review complex plan documents before importing.\n  [friction] @01KHVYHKA (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Invoked /spec-plan skill but wrote implementation guide instead of plan-import-format document. User had explicitly said /spec-plan. Lesson: invoking a skill is not enough — must follow its output format from the FIRST write. When /spec-plan is invoked for plan mode, write the plan file in kspec plan import format (# Title, ## Specs with YAML code block, ## Tasks, ## Implementation Notes) from the start.\n  [success] @01KHVE718 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Parallel agent reviews (Codex + /pr-review) found complementary issues: Codex caught test annotation gaps, review agent caught shared fixture regression. Multi-reviewer pattern is high-value for quality.\n  [friction] @01KHVE714 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Shared test fixture fragility: tests/fixtures/kynetic.meta.yaml is used by multiple test files. Tests that append YAML keys (like meta.test.ts appending conventions:) collide when the fixture gains new top-level keys. Caused real regression in PR #413. Consider per-test fixture isolation or fixture composition helpers.\n  [success] @01KHT79FA (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Running codex review in parallel with PR review agent caught a real security bypass (split-quote git \"reset\" --hard). The dual-reviewer pattern (code-level + quality-gate) provides meaningful coverage overlap.\n  [friction] @01KHT79F7 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Guard script bash code embedded in JS template literal makes quote escaping painful — single quotes inside tr/sed cause parse errors. Consider extracting guard scripts to standalone .sh files loaded at setup time instead of inline template literals.\n  [friction] @01KHSKZD (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Refactoring tasks without spec_ref have AC traceability gap — task-level ACs in notes aren't discoverable by standard // AC: @spec-ref ac-N annotation pattern. Codex review flagged this on PR #411. Options: (1) allow // AC: @task-ref ac-N, (2) require spec items even for refactoring, (3) teach reviewers to check task notes. Related: @task-skill-module-split had no spec_ref but defined 3 ACs in notes.\n  [success] @01KHSG0C (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review (gpt-5.3-codex) caught a real correctness bug in drift detection (hash-gating for sidecar changes) that the implementing agent missed. Codex reproduced the issue locally with a concrete script. High value for code review.\n  [success] @01KHRYZ5 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Parallel dual-reviewer strategy (Claude agent + Codex) is effective: Claude validates correctness, Codex catches spec-alignment gaps. Use both for PRs with spec ACs.\n  [success] @01KHRP78P (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Multi-model PR review (Claude + Codex in parallel) caught issues that single-model review missed. Codex found missing debugLog in outer catch and weak test assertions. Worth continuing this pattern for non-trivial PRs.\n  [friction] @01KHRP78K (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Running kspec setup during development creates kspec-agents.md and .claude/skills/ in project root. These are test artifacts for the kspec project itself (which uses AGENTS.md, not kspec-agents.md). They trigger repeated stop hook warnings about uncommitted changes. Need either: (a) test isolation so setup tests dont leak into project root, or (b) cleanup step in bootstrap/dev workflow.\n  [friction] @01KHQXGWQ (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review skill always demands // AC: @spec-ref ac-N annotations, even for infrastructure/cleanup tasks where ACs describe absence of code. Should skip AC annotation checks when task has no spec_ref or when spec type is not user-facing behavior.\n  [friction] @01KHQXGWG (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Background PR review agent can act on stale context: agent added 172-line test file for a spec that was deleted during review. When running review agents in background while making spec/task changes in foreground, the agent does not see those changes. Required a follow-up cleanup PR.\n  [friction] @01KHKTQNN (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Ralph loop PR-by-PR review process misses cross-PR duplication accumulation. PR #381 added rendering utils, #383 duplicated them in a new file, #394 consolidated render functions but missed the utilities, #401 added more dead code. Each PR passes review individually but the aggregate drift is significant (24 inbox items from one loop). Consider adding a consolidation/cleanup iteration every N PRs or at loop end.\n  [success] @01KHKTQNH (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Parallel subagent review of ralph loop: 5 agents reviewing different PR ranges (371-376, 377-384, 385-393, 394-402, test suite) completed in ~3min wall clock. Independent agents finding the same duplication issues confirms real problems vs noise. Effective pattern for post-loop quality review.\n  [friction] @01KHHR77E (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    kspec batch uses kebab-case args (depends-on) but schema fields use snake_case (depends_on). Inconsistency causes trial-and-error on first batch use.\n  [success] @01KHHR77C (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Running both internal Plan agent review AND Codex review-plan on the same plan catches complementary issues. Internal review finds code-level gaps; Codex finds spec quality issues.\n  [success] @01KHHR77A (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Structured interview (one question at a time with pro/con analysis) before planning leads to well-reasoned architecture decisions. User engagement is higher when they see trade-offs laid out clearly.\n  [friction] @01KHFPVS (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    CLI commands targeting main project files (like .claude/skills/) must use ctx.rootDir, not ctx.specDir or path.dirname(ctx.specDir). The specDir is the spec content location (.kspec/ in shadow mode), while rootDir is always the project root. Using dirname(specDir) fails in shadow mode because .kspec/ is a worktree at the project root level. Encountered in skill render command where files were written to /tmp/ instead of project root.\n  [success] @01KHFCMDE (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Iterative clarifying questions with ask tool: asking one question at a time with 3-4 concrete options (each with descriptions) before synthesizing research into a plan led to well-informed decisions. User could respond quickly because each question had enough context from the research. Better than presenting a full plan and asking for feedback.\n  [success] @01KHFCMDB (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Dual-reviewer pattern for plans: having both an internal holistic reviewer (Claude) and an external reviewer (Codex) independently review the same plan catches complementary issues. Internal finds code-level integration problems, external finds architectural concerns. Both agreed on the same critical issues (compound ACs, inline YAML). Three revision cycles (v1 reviewed v2 reviewed v3) produced significantly stronger output.\n  [success] @01KHFCMD8 (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Parallel subagent research pattern: launching 4+ research agents simultaneously on different aspects of the same question (cross-project audit, usage patterns, external ecosystem, install story) produced comprehensive non-overlapping findings in ~6 minutes. Much more effective than sequential investigation.\n  [friction] @01KHCYW7 (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Commander.js parent-action option routing: when parent and subcommands define the same options, Commander doesn't cleanly route them. Tried passThroughOptions (requires global enablePositionalOptions, breaks --json global option), ended up using allowUnknownOption + temporary Command re-parse. Pattern works but is non-idiomatic. If more command groups need default actions, consider extracting this into a shared utility.\n  [success] @01KHAAN8 (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Re-baseline research findings against current code before creating specs. In CLI agent-UX session, 7 of 21 recommendations from telemetry analysis were already implemented. Verifying each against the codebase with parallel explore agents before speccing saved significant overhead (would have created 7 unnecessary specs and 7 unnecessary tasks).\n  [friction] @01KGY73W8 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    GitHub PR review thread resolution requires GraphQL mutation (resolveReviewThread). REST reply endpoint posts replies but does not resolve threads. Had to discover thread node IDs via GraphQL query first.\n  [success] @01KGY73W6 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex as second reviewer on doc PRs caught a real inconsistency (scenario expected answer vs AGENTS.md rule) and a non-existent file path. Worth running on documentation PRs, not just code PRs.\n  [success] @01KGY73W3 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Stress testing with real codebase exploration is more effective than synthetic padding for finding doc gaps. Agent reads actual code (building 30k+ tokens of context), then answers recall questions. Found 2 real degradation points (PR workflow steps, scope expansion) that were reinforced.\n  [success] @01KGY73W0 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Test-first doc editing: building eval scenarios before trimming AGENTS.md prevented info loss. 15 scenarios + 8 stress test recall questions validated the trimmed doc (14/15 PASS, 6/8 PASS). Pattern: build test suite → make changes → validate → fix gaps.\n  [friction] @01KGXN2ZA (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    AGENTS.md task state transitions documented task complete as working from in_progress/pending, but CLI actually requires pending_review (or --skip-review). Pre-existing inaccuracy that propagated to README. Codex caught it via runtime spot-checks that I did not do.\n  [success] @01KGXN2Z5 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Running 4 parallel Explore/Bash subagents to research README structure, AGENTS.md structure, full CLI catalog, and recent git features simultaneously saved significant time and produced comprehensive input for the docs update.\n  [friction] @01KGXN2Y (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Documentation tasks that update README/AGENTS.md should also check skill files (.claude/skills/) for staleness on the same topics. Easy to miss because skills are a separate concern. A validate --skills --docs check could catch this.\n  [success] @01KGXHNR (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Using Codex (/codex pr) as an automated second reviewer caught a real bug (Commander parseInt pre-parse bypass) that manual review missed. The review-fix-re-review cycle completed in one session. Worth using for every non-trivial PR.\n  [friction] @01KGXHND (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Commander's .option() parse function (e.g. parseInt) silently pre-coerces values before the action handler runs. When replacing inline validation with shared helpers, the helper never sees the raw string. This caused a bug in PR #347 where derive/plan-derive still accepted '3abc' as priority 3. Fix: never use Commander parse functions for options validated by shared helpers.\n  [friction] @01KGWZ26W (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex review depth insufficient on first pass: 4 review rounds were needed to fully validate plan-import slug safety. Each fix introduced or revealed the next gap. Root cause: Codex reviews surface-level patterns first (wrong API check) before deeper ones (stale index across mutations). Need a way to prime Codex to reason about mutation-staleness and index invalidation upfront, possibly via a review checklist or system prompt addition to the codex skill.\n  [success] @01KGWZ26T (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex iterative review as convergence loop: codex review → fix → re-review converges on correctness. 4 rounds on PR #346 found progressively deeper cross-namespace slug collision gaps (duplicate_slug handling → plans missing from refIndex → stale index for import-created specs → spec creation not checking importReservedSlugs). Each round caught a real bug.\n  [success] @01KGWZ26Q (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Stale index mutation pattern: when mutating items in a loop with a pre-built refIndex, track created slugs in a parallel Set since refIndex won't see loop-created items. Pattern: importReservedSlugs Set alongside refIndex.isSlugAvailable(). Applied in plan-import to prevent cross-namespace slug collisions within a single import run.\n  [friction] @01KGWG8Y (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Session event data payloads are untyped — EventTypeSchema defines the type enum but data field is z.unknown(). Had to grep through real JSONL files to discover that Ralph records task completions as session.update events with sessionUpdate: 'tool_call' and rawInput.command. This caused a bug that was only caught by Codex reviewing against real data.\n  [success] @01KGWG8W (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review caught silent bug in session log list — tasks_completed metric searched for non-existent event type. Real task completions use session.update tool_call events with rawInput.command containing 'task complete'. Without review, feature would ship reporting 0 tasks for all real sessions.\n  [friction] @01KGVK9P (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Automation triage forces context switch to spec creation: when assessing eligibility, discovering a task needs a spec means switching from assessment mode to authoring mode (find module, find parent, create item, add ACs, link task). Consider a guided flow or --create-spec flag on task set --automation.\n  [success] @01KGVK9M (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Implementation notes at promotion time: adding concrete implementation notes during inbox promote (not after) makes tasks self-contained for future agents. Include: which files to modify, patterns to follow, approximate scope.\n  [success] @01KGVK9K (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Batch triage workflow: categorize inbox by theme first, quick-win deletions, then one-at-a-time promotion with AskUserQuestion showing What/Why/How context. Cross-reference against git log and completed tasks to catch already-done items. This pattern processed 61 items efficiently.\n  [success] @01KGVDDP (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Task @01KGVARW had excellent root cause analysis in its notes (specific file, line numbers, all call sites, exact reproduction). This made the fix trivial — under 10 minutes from start to merged PR. Detailed bug reports with root cause are high-leverage.\n  [success] @01KGVCEN (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Tracing ACP bugs through the chain (framing.ts → client.ts → spawner.ts → ralph.ts) was efficient because the codebase has clear layering and the override mechanism (methodTimeouts) was already in place. Minimal change (18 lines, 3 files) for the fix.\n  [friction] @01KGVCEF (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review on self-authored PRs creates review threads that trigger check-unresolved-comments CI gate. Requires manual GraphQL thread resolution + CI re-run before merging. Codex skill should either skip inline comments on own PRs or auto-resolve threads after posting.\n  [success] @01KGTNYA (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Parallel subagent research (two Explore agents) during spec-plan design phase efficiently gathered session infrastructure context in ~60s instead of sequential exploration.\n  [success] @01KGTNY9 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex plan review as pre-import quality gate caught trait ref format bug and compound AC issues. Worth making standard practice for plan imports with 3+ specs.\n  [success] @01KGTM42 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review as documentation quality gate: /codex pr caught real arg-name bugs in batch JSON examples that would have caused user failures. The review→fix→push cycle was fast. Worth using on all docs-heavy PRs.\n  [success] @01KGTH9G (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    GitHub PR review API with --input JSON file works reliably for inline comments. Pattern: write structured JSON to /tmp/codex-pr-review-body.json with {event, body, comments[{path, line, body}]}, then post via gh api repos/{o}/{r}/pulls/{n}/reviews --method POST --input file. Codex figured out the line vs position issue on its own (first attempt used line numbers which mapped to null, then corrected to diff positions).\n  [friction] @01KGTH99 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex exec review subcommand has different flag parsing than codex exec: -s must go before the review subcommand (codex exec -s danger-full-access review ...) not after (codex exec review -s ... fails). This flag ordering quirk isn't documented in codex --help and caused a runtime failure that required a fix commit.\n  [success] @01KGTH92 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex self-review loop is effective: had Codex review its own skill file, it found 4 MUST-FIX issues (invalid CLI flags, sandbox conflicts, malformed API payloads). Then had Codex review the PR with inline comments — it found 2 more issues (flag ordering, nested fences). Each round of review caught real bugs that would have caused runtime failures.\n  [success] @01KGTF5E (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Plan-driven implementation with spec-first ACs: having 22 acceptance criteria defined before coding made both implementation and testing systematic. Each test was annotated with its AC reference, and the review could verify coverage by checking annotations. The plan→spec→implement→test→review pipeline worked well for this medium-complexity feature.\n  [friction] @01KGTF57 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Atomic copy-back was a design gap caught by reviewers, not by tests. The plan described 'copy-back' as straightforward fs.cp, but both reviewers independently flagged that delete-then-copy is not safe under I/O failure. Future plans involving filesystem atomicity should explicitly call out failure modes in the design — the happy path isn't enough.\n  [success] @01KGS89M (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Subagent-reviewed AC drafts: Pattern of drafting ACs → subagent review → revise → apply caught 5+ real issues (--count+--json interaction, partial success gap, missing optional flag ACs, scope creep in AC-10, missing multi-trait remove symmetry). Prevented rework during implementation.\n  [success] @01KGS4Y7 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    WeakMap<Command, T> annotation pattern works cleanly for adding metadata to Commander commands. Avoids monkey-patching internals, is type-safe and GC-friendly, chains naturally with .command() calls (markMutating(cmd.command(...))), and integrates with extractCommandTree() by reading annotations during traversal. Reusable for future annotation types (e.g. requires-auth, idempotent). See src/cli/command-annotations.ts for reference implementation.\n  [success] @01KGRP6D (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Plan-import scoped notes fix: well-scoped plan with specific line numbers and code changes led to zero-iteration implementation (all tests passed first try). Good pattern for future fix plans.\n  [success] @01KGR5CV (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Precise plan with exact file paths, line numbers, and code diffs made the plan-import bug fix session trivially fast. Plan → implement → test → PR → merge in one smooth pass with zero rework.\n  [success] @01KGR0NM (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Subagent-driven holistic review for spec-plan design: Launching a general-purpose subagent with structured review criteria (completeness, consistency, spec coverage, edge cases) caught 2 blockers and 7 should-fix items before plan import. Pattern: write plan doc, then have subagent review against existing codebase patterns before committing.\n  [success] @01KGQYEC (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Parallel subagent mining pattern: spawning 3 specialized agents (notes, patterns, gaps) to analyze the same dataset from different angles. Each found complementary insights the others missed. Effective for pre-prune data extraction.\n  [friction] @01KGQY77 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    pr-review-merge consumes 36% of all workflow time (215 of 594 min). Step 1 (CI check) bimodal: 30 runs under 30s but 9 over 5 min. No fast-path for trivial PRs that pass all gates immediately. Consecutive chains of up to 14 suggest batch PR processing needs a dedicated workflow.\n  [idea] @01KGQY75 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Workflow run telemetry: Before pruning old runs, mine them for aggregate stats (abort rates, durations, sequencing patterns). Consider keeping aggregate statistics even when pruning individual records to enable trend analysis. First mining found: 9.9 hrs total, PR review 36%, reflect 46%, task-work-loop 6.5%.\n  [friction] @01KGQY72 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    session-reflect-loop runs too aggressively in ralph loops — 72 runs (40% of all runs), chains of up to 31 consecutive executions, vast majority capture zero new items. 113 min spent (19% of total workflow time) with questionable marginal value in consecutive chains. Should be conditional on whether preceding task-work-loop actually completed work.\n  [friction] @01KGQY70 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    task-work-loop has 69% abort rate (11/16 runs) — almost entirely because no eligible tasks existed when started. Workflow works fine when work exists (5/5 completed all 9 steps). Root cause: ralph orchestrator starts workflow without pre-checking kspec tasks ready --eligible.\n  [success] @01KGQY6Y (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Workflow run mining before prune: /reflect captured 99.4% of run content into durable artifacts. Out of 181 runs with ~100 meaningful notes, only 1 contained unique info not already tracked. Validates reflection-based knowledge capture approach.\n  [success] @01KGQVA9 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Subagent delegation pattern: using Explore agent for codebase survey + general-purpose agent for PR review kept main context clean and session efficient during daemon strict-mode fix\n  [friction] @01KGPHG8 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Committed directly to main forgetting branch protection requires PRs. Had to create branch after the fact and reset. Should always create feature branch first in this repo.\n  [success] @01KGPHG6 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Review agent (opus subagent) caught a real validation bug - missing required 'id' field in plan document AC example. The schema check against actual parser code was the key insight. Always verify examples against actual schema definitions.\n  [success] @01KGP40Y (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Tracing output flow (console.log → stdout.write chain) quickly revealed the doubled prefix bug. When debugging output duplication, mapping the full write path through all wrappers/interceptors identifies where multiple layers add the same content.\n  [friction] @01KGP40S (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Vitest replaces console.log with its own implementation that doesn't flow through process.stdout.write. Tests that capture stdout.write won't see console.log output. When testing code that wraps both console.log and stdout.write, spy on console.log directly (vi.spyOn) and test stdout.write capture separately.\n  [success] @01KGP3JJ (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Test-first implementation with AC annotations: Writing tests with AC annotations (// AC: @spec-ref ac-N) before implementation ensures complete coverage and creates clear traceability. Pattern used in help.test.ts covered all 5 ACs systematically and caught the --json flag issue early.\n  [friction] @01KGP3JB (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Commander.js optional argument pattern: When a command has an optional argument like .command('help [topic]'), the action handler signature must use (topic: string | undefined, options) NOT (topic?: string, options?). The optional TypeScript syntax breaks Commander's argument passing - options becomes an empty object. Pattern seen in task.ts commands works correctly.\n  [success] @01KGP16K (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Plan with exact file paths, line numbers, and 'nature of change' descriptions translates directly to precise edits. The plan for ralph loop fixes had a table mapping files to changes — each row became one Edit tool call with no ambiguity.\n  [friction] @01KGP16H (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    kspec validate output is noisy with pre-existing reference errors and alignment warnings (22 ref errors, 152 alignment warnings). Makes it hard to verify whether a specific change introduced new issues. A --diff or --new-only flag that shows only issues not present in the previous commit would help.\n  [friction] @01KGP14G (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Acceptance criteria schema requires 'id' field but this isn't obvious when writing test plans or spec YAML. Parser validation fails silently - spec just doesn't appear in parsed output. Better error would be: 'AC missing required field: id' at parse time. Or make id optional and auto-generate if missing.\n  [success] @01KGP14D (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Using createdSpecsMap to track specs during import solved parent reference resolution elegantly. Specs created in topological order could reference earlier-created specs by slug lookup in the map, working in both dry-run and real modes. Pattern: track created entities in a map keyed by slug for multi-step operations with interdependencies.\n  [friction] @01KGP14A (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Test fixtures use @test-core module but many example commands in docs/tests assume @cli exists. Caused global find/replace in tests when module reference didn't match. Consider: (1) standardizing fixture module names to match common examples, (2) documenting fixture module structure in test helpers, or (3) adding @cli as an alias/additional module in fixtures.\n  [success] @01KGMX3K (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Test-driven regex debugging proved effective: wrote comprehensive tests for plan document parser's markdown section extraction (## Specs, ## Tasks, ## Implementation Notes), which immediately caught regex pattern bugs (^## anchors failed to match). Fixed patterns before integration, avoiding late-stage debugging. Pattern: write tests first for parsing/extraction logic, especially when dealing with complex patterns or unfamiliar APIs.\n  [success] @01KGM1RX (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Pattern-following implementation: When implementing new CLI commands, examining similar existing commands (e.g., derive.ts for task creation patterns, plan.ts for command structure) and reusing their patterns leads to consistent, correct implementations. This approach worked well for plan derive - imports, error handling, slug generation, and bidirectional updates all followed established patterns, resulting in clean first implementation with no structural issues.\n  [success] @01KGM07T (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Validation ordering for better errors - When validating references, check type mismatch (isTask/isSpecItem) before checking existence. Gives 'Reference X is not a plan' instead of 'Plan reference not found' when user passes wrong type. Applied in task add and task set --plan-ref validation.\n  [success] @01KGM07Q (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Schema-first implementation pattern - Defining schemas (Plan, PlansFile) first, then parser functions (loadPlans, findPlanByRef), then CLI commands (plan add/get/set) created clean separation. Each layer had focused tests. Pattern worked well for both plan CLI (23 tests) and plan_ref field (8 tests).\n  [friction] @01KGM07N (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Test reference ambiguity with @01 prefix - When writing integration tests that create plans/tasks, using generic references like @01 fails when test fixtures already contain items with ULIDs starting with 01. Must use explicit slugs (--slug test-plan-1) for test isolation. Affected cli-plan.test.ts and task-plan-ref.test.ts - all references needed slugs added.\n  [success] @01KGKYGF (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Spec split for automation eligibility: Splitting a large spec into focused child specs made task-to-AC mapping clear and enabled granular automation eligibility assessment.\n  [friction] @01KGKYGD (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Parent relationship not persisted: item add with under flag creates items but parent field is null. Also spec says parent flag but CLI requires under flag.\n  [success] @01KGKVNV (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Spawning a Plan agent to review completed spec/task work against the original plan catches gaps (like missing traits) that the primary agent overlooked. Useful pattern for quality assurance on spec creation work.\n  [success] @01KGK93J (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Task notes with root cause analysis and specific file list made implementation straightforward - this pattern should be replicated when creating reflection-based tasks\n  [success] @01KGG9M7 (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    kspec bootstraps greenfield projects well: Initialized kynamo (Rust rewrite project) with kspec to track its own development. Shadow branch, spec items for 6 implementation phases, and inbox items all created smoothly. Demonstrates kspec's value beyond self-hosting - can bootstrap any new project with structured planning.\n  [success] @01KGG9KZ (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Multi-codebase exploration via parallel subagents: Spawning 4-6 explore/research agents simultaneously to analyze kspec and kynetic-bot for gap analysis, architecture comparison, and Rust migration planning was highly effective. Each agent focused on one domain (CLI stack, bot ecosystem, ACP, context mgmt, migration strategy) and the results synthesized into comprehensive documentation.\n  [success] @01KG95K2 (1/30/2026 by @claude)\n    kspec search command exists and works well - found it during reflection when checking coverage. Searched 'multi-ref' and got comprehensive results across specs, tasks, inbox. Should use this more proactively during work.\n  [success] @01KG8RB4 (1/30/2026 by @claude)\n    User edge-case questions ('what about Ctrl+C?', 'does it work on Windows?') led to better implementation. Don't rush to close - invite scrutiny.\n  [success] @01KG8RAY (1/30/2026 by @claude)\n    Review feedback loop (Greptile + Claude) caught real bugs: async race condition in signal handler and no-op test. Worth keeping mandatory.\n  [success] @01KG8PFD (1/30/2026 by @claude)\n    Following existing patterns (task-limit marker) made implementing new feature (end-iteration) straightforward. Same file paths, detection mechanism, and cleanup lifecycle.\n  [success] @01KG7ARF (1/30/2026 by @claude)\n    Test pattern: when creating multiple items in quick succession for batch operation tests, use JSON mode to capture full ULIDs instead of parsing 8-char prefixes from human output. ULID prefixes can collide when items are created within the same millisecond, causing test deduplication to produce unexpected results. Full ULIDs are always unique.\n  [success] @01KG6WJZ (1/29/2026 by @claude)\n    Observation triage session efficiently resolved 12 friction observations by: (1) batch retrieving all observations, (2) cross-referencing AGENTS.md documentation, (3) categorizing into resolvable/keep-open/historical before acting, (4) verifying obsolete items (like deleted test files) before resolution\n  [success] @01KG64RZ (1/29/2026 by @claude)\n    CLI --force flag pattern works well for agent/script use. inbox delete --force skips confirmation. Same pattern could apply to other commands needing non-interactive mode.\n  [success] @01KG5SGK (1/29/2026 by @claude)\n    Explore+Plan parallel subagent pattern for implementation tasks: Before implementing a task with existing spec/notes, launch two subagents in parallel - (1) Explore agent to gather codebase context (file locations, interfaces, test patterns) and (2) Plan agent to design the implementation approach. Both complete before coding begins, providing thorough context AND a concrete plan. Used successfully for ref normalization task - all tests passed first try.\n  [friction] @01KG4M2Y (1/29/2026 by @claude)\n    Skill documentation can become stale without detection: /e2e skill claimed getByRole('option') doesn't work with bits-ui when it actually does (bits-ui added proper ARIA roles). No mechanism flags skill docs as potentially outdated when dependencies change. Task @01KG1H91 was created based on incorrect assumption from stale docs.\n  [success] @01KG4M2R (1/29/2026 by @claude)\n    Throwaway inspection tests: Writing quick, disposable tests to inspect actual DOM/state is effective debugging. DOM inspection test revealed bits-ui elements have both role='option' AND data-slot attributes - concrete evidence in minutes.\n  [success] @01KG4M2H (1/29/2026 by @claude)\n    Investigation-first debugging: When tests pass but docs say they shouldn't, investigate WHY before closing. In this session, discovered skill documentation was outdated rather than tests being wrong - prevented false task closure and led to fixing actual issue (stale docs).\n  [success] @01KG2FM2 (1/28/2026 by @claude)\n    isStaticMode() guard pattern worked well for conditionally disabling features without major refactoring - single check at API layer propagates through entire app\n  [friction] @01KG17RD (1/27/2026 by @claude)\n    Session context is loaded from .kspec-session file (not kynetic.meta.yaml). This caused debugging time when E2E tests couldn't find session focus. The API (fetchSessionContext) and file location should be documented in /e2e skill or AGENTS.md.\n  [success] @01KG17R6 (1/27/2026 by @claude)\n    Using /e2e skill patterns immediately caught ULID validation issue (Crockford base32 excludes I, L, O, U). Invalid ULIDs in fixtures fail silently - the skill's warning helped debug quickly.\n  [success] @01KG0ZHY (1/27/2026 by @claude)\n    Dedicated E2E fixtures pattern - creating packages/web-ui/tests/fixtures/ separate from shared tests/fixtures/ cleanly isolates E2E test data from unit tests, preventing cross-contamination\n  [friction] @01KG0N4V (1/27/2026 by @claude)\n    Schema validation (safeParse) failures in extractItemsFromRaw are silent - items just don't appear in results with no error or warning. This caused significant debugging time when trait had invalid enum values and module had nested items in traits field. Consider adding validation error logging or a strict mode that throws on parse failures.\n  [success] @01KG0J4X (1/27/2026 by @claude)\n    Documenting blocking issues with specific symptoms, likely causes ranked by probability, and debug commands - makes next session pickup much faster\n  [success] @01KG0J4H (1/27/2026 by @claude)\n    Reading reference tests (tasks.spec.ts) before writing new tests - understanding existing patterns informed structure and avoided reinventing conventions\n  [success] @01KG031Y (1/27/2026 by @claude)\n    Console log capture in E2E tests revealed exact bits-ui callback values, making the array quirk immediately visible. Adding console.log to component handlers during debugging is highly effective for understanding Svelte 5 reactivity issues.\n  [success] @01KFZ708 (1/26/2026 by @claude)\n    Svelte 5 reactivity solution: Use $state object pattern + flushSync() for reactive conditional rendering. Separate $state variables may not trigger {#if} block re-renders; wrapping state in object (panel = $state({ open: false, task: null })) and using flushSync(() => { panel.open = true }) ensures synchronous DOM updates. Also documented in /svelte-5 skill. Resolves friction @01KFYAH9.\n  [success] @01KFZ3YN (1/26/2026 by @claude)\n    Using Explore subagent for deep analysis before making changes provided comprehensive understanding (file locations, line numbers, root cause, impact analysis) that enabled confident parallel fixes via multiple subagents.\n  [friction] @01KFZ3YG (1/26/2026 by @claude)\n    Task notes from loop mode sessions can become noisy/unreliable, requiring manual cleanup or user warning to ignore recent notes. Example: @01KFP84N had 26 noise notes removed and user warned remaining notes might be incorrect.\n  [success] @01KFYAH1 (1/26/2026 by @claude)\n    Debug element technique for Svelte reactivity: Adding conditional debug elements like `{#if state}<div data-testid=\"debug-...\">{state}</div>{/if}` quickly verifies whether state is actually true at runtime vs expected. Useful for diagnosing reactivity issues where console logs show updates but UI doesn't reflect them.\n  [success] @01KFXVKJ (1/26/2026 by @claude)\n    Comparing dist/ vs source when debugging daemon issues - immediately revealed the stale build problem when runtime debugging was going in circles\n  [success] @01KFXTAK (1/26/2026 by @claude)\n    Subagent plan review caught CLI command syntax issues (missing --as and --id flags) before execution. Using a reviewer subagent after writing a plan prevents wasted iterations.\n  [friction] @01KFXTAD (1/26/2026 by @claude)\n    Tasks created via 'kspec derive' have auto-generated notes that just copy spec content. For standalone tasks, needed to manually add structured sections: Background (why this matters), Reproduction steps, Concrete examples, Open design questions. This structure makes tasks actionable without conversation context.\n  [success] @01KFVXSQ (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop exit condition working correctly: Session 28 demonstrated clean exit behavior after @01KFV6CZ fix. Loop checked 'kspec tasks ready --eligible', found empty array (all 3 in_progress tasks were automation:needs_review), and exited immediately with zero redundant iterations. This resolves the overnight session issue where loops ran 30+ iterations with no progress. Exit condition now filters both ready AND in_progress tasks by automation:eligible status.\n  [success] @01KFVVHG (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop mode escalation discipline working as designed: Session 01KFVN3D ran 18 iterations with 3 in_progress tasks. All tasks marked automation:needs_review in iterations 7-8 after assessment. Loop correctly exited when kspec tasks ready --eligible returned empty array. No thrashing, no shortcuts attempted, no improper completion claims. Tasks stayed in_progress with comprehensive checkpoint notes providing human handoff context. This validates the automation system recognizing its limits and delegating complex/blocked work appropriately.\n  [success] @01KFVVB2 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Session checkpoint notes (iterations 7-17) consistently documented: task state, what's complete, what's blocked, required resolution steps, investigation files, automation status. This pattern provides excellent continuity for next session and clear audit trail of escalation reasoning.\n  [friction] @01KFVVAT (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph automation loop ran 10+ iterations (7-17) with zero implementation work after all tasks marked needs_review. Loop exit logic only checks task existence, not progress stagnation. Should detect: N consecutive iterations with no file changes, commits, or task status transitions → early exit with diagnostic.\n  [success] @01KFVTYW (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Task escalation with comprehensive rationale prevents false automation work. Pattern observed: when tasks blocked or require human decisions, automation loop adds multi-paragraph notes documenting: (1) WHAT blocks the work, (2) WHY it's not automatable, (3) WHAT human needs to do next. Tags task automation:needs_review. Result: zero false work attempts across 15 iterations, clear handoff context for human sessions. Quality over speed: better to escalate with context than attempt inappropriate work.\n  [success] @01KFVTBK (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Session checkpoint hook enforcement prevented task abandonment: Stop hook detected 3 in_progress tasks without recent meaningful progress and blocked session exit until checkpoint notes were added. All tasks received documentation of current state, blockers, and next steps. This pattern ensures tasks don't silently stall - they either get documented or completed. Hook runs automatically on session end with no manual intervention needed. Particularly valuable for automation loops where task state must be explicit for next iteration.\n  [friction] @01KFVTBA (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop iteration without progress: Loop ran 12 consecutive iterations with zero code changes or git commits. All iterations exited with 'no automation-eligible tasks' message. Tasks remained in_progress state throughout despite being marked automation:needs_review. Each iteration added near-identical checkpoint notes documenting 'no work attempted'. Pattern indicates need for circuit breaker that detects repeated no-progress iterations and either auto-escalates tasks or stops the loop. Related to @01KF554T circuit breaker concept but captures actual observed behavior rather than theoretical design.\n  [friction] @01KFVSKK (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop mode automation tag disconnect: Across 8 consecutive loop iterations, task notes documented 'marked automation:needs_review' but the actual command 'kspec task set @ref --automation needs_review' was never executed. This suggests a pattern in autonomous loops where intent is documented in notes but the corresponding state change command is not run. Root cause: loop documenting WHAT should happen without executing HOW to make it happen.\n  [success] @01KFVSBZ (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    E2E test infrastructure debugging pattern: When E2E tests fail, work backward through the stack systematically. Session 01KFVN3D example: test timeout → Vite proxy missing → API client hardcoded URLs → daemon fixture loading issue. At each layer, verify the fix (tests connect successfully) before moving to next layer. This methodical approach identified root cause (daemon ContextManager path handling) after fixing intermediate issues (Vite proxy + relative URLs in commit c24fd694).\n  [success] @01KFVRK6 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop iteration 5: Accountability in action - instead of immediately marking tasks as needs_review, actually debugged E2E infrastructure issues. Fixed real problems (Vite proxy configuration, API client URL handling) that were blocking all E2E tests. Committed working code (c24fd694) that moves the project forward. Loop failures should document genuine investigation and progress, not just 'this is hard, skipping.'\n  [friction] @01KFVQYR (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop mode error handling AC-5 not working: Tasks @01KFP84N, @01KFP84S, @01KFQAGY all have [LOOP-FAIL:3] notes but remain automation:eligible instead of being auto-escalated to automation:needs_review as specified in @loop-mode-error-handling AC-5. Implementation in PR #243 claims to handle this but actual behavior shows automation status not being updated after 3 failures. Verified in session 01KFVN3DDTKEQF1DAF8V3KV48V iteration 4.\n  [friction] @01KFVQKF (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Task DoD conflict with project conventions: Task @01KFQAF9 DoD said 'write real HTTP tests' and 'no documentary/stub tests remain' but AGENTS.md documents that daemon API routes use documentary tests (static analysis). Had to resolve conflict by checking existing patterns in daemon-api-*.test.ts files. Resolution: Created E2E tests in packages/web-ui/tests/e2e/ and updated documentary test to reference them. Friction point: When DoD contradicts documented patterns, unclear which is authoritative.\n  [success] @01KFVQKD (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    E2E tests via Playwright for daemon API endpoints: Using packages/web-ui/tests/e2e/*.spec.ts for real HTTP integration tests (not vitest mocks) aligns with project architecture documented in AGENTS.md. This session: created api-projects.spec.ts with 5 passing tests making real fetch() calls to running daemon. Pattern: Start daemon via test fixture, make HTTP requests, verify responses. More reliable than mocking and tests actual behavior.\n  [success] @01KFVPYB (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Reading actual source code to verify task claims proved more reliable than trusting notes. When @01KFP84N notes said 'UI incomplete', reading TaskList.svelte revealed all required test IDs were present. Verification approach: Read components -> check test IDs -> run tests (if possible) -> only then accept completion claims. This prevented false conclusions about missing implementation.\n  [friction] @01KFVPY3 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop mode task verification gap: Multiple iterations accepted task notes claiming 'already implemented' or 'tests exist' without independent verification (running tests, checking code). Iteration 1 concluded UI was incomplete based on test failures; iteration 2 found UI components actually exist with proper test IDs. Real issue was E2E infrastructure, not missing implementation. Task notes are context, not proof of completion.\n  [success] @01KFTTA3 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Structured loop iteration note template pattern: Used consistently across 30 automation iterations to document why progress wasn't possible. Template format: '## Loop Iteration N - [Status]' followed by Exit Reason, Task Status (with ✅/❌ checkboxes for completed/incomplete work), specific blockers, and clear next actions. Creates audit trail showing automation decision-making and prevents false 'why is nothing happening' concerns. Makes it obvious when automation is correctly identifying blockers vs stuck in broken logic.\n  [friction] @01KFTSWY (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop ran 28 iterations with identical outcome (no eligible tasks, same 3 tasks documented each time). After 2-3 consecutive iterations returning no eligible tasks, the loop should recognize the pattern and exit early. Current behavior wastes API calls and adds redundant notes. Need early termination logic: if last N iterations (N=2 or 3) all exited with 'no eligible tasks' and same task set, stop the loop.\n  [friction] @01KFTSR2 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Automation loop adds repetitive status notes to stalled tasks. After 27 iterations of ralph automation, same 3 in-progress tasks (@01KFP84N, @01KFP84S, @01KFQAGY) accumulated 27+ nearly-identical notes documenting 'Loop Iteration N - No Eligible Tasks'. While notes provide audit trail, they create significant noise obscuring substantive progress notes. Consider: batch note update mechanism, dedicated status/stall-reason field, or suppress iteration notes when status unchanged.\n  [friction] @01KFTSQQ (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Workflow system lacks early exit mechanism. When workflow determines no work is possible (e.g., no eligible tasks), must still step through all remaining workflow steps providing skip reasons. Attempted 'kspec workflow complete @ref' which does not exist. Would benefit from 'workflow exit' or 'workflow skip-remaining' command to cleanly terminate when continuation is impossible.\n  [success] @01KFTR8Z (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop iteration 21 efficiently completed pending_review task @01KFP85M in first action by checking task status, validating work was already merged, and immediately completing it. Demonstrates value of prioritizing pending_review tasks over in_progress tasks - they're usually closest to done and just need verification/completion. Pattern: pending_review > in_progress > ready.\n  [friction] @01KFTR8F (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Repetitive checkpoint notes across automation loops: Tasks stuck in automation:needs_review state get identical checkpoint notes added every iteration. Example: @01KFP84N received 36 notes, with notes 27-36 being nearly identical 'Loop Iteration N - Session End Checkpoint' messages. Creates noise without adding value. Possible solutions: (1) Skip checkpoint notes if last note is recent and task state unchanged, (2) Update most recent checkpoint note instead of appending, (3) Add checkpoint command that manages session-end state separately from progress notes.\n  [success] @01KFTQJ6 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop iteration 19 assessment: Correctly identified that all 4 eligible GitHub Pages export tasks (@01KFMMJ4, @01KFMMXK, @01KFMMXR, @01KFMMXW) exceed automation loop capacity. Marked all as automation:needs_review with clear reasoning. Tasks form a coordinated feature set requiring multi-hour focused implementation.\n  [friction] @01KFTPYV (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Zero-completion automation sessions: Loop iteration 17 completed with 0 tasks finished, all 3 in_progress tasks escalated to automation:needs_review. Distinct from prior successful Ralph loops. Root cause: Tasks marked automation:eligible but require daemon E2E testing (2 tasks) or multi-file coordinated implementation (1 task). Gap between 'eligible' tag and actual automation feasibility in single iteration. Affects task selection heuristics.\n  [success] @01KFTPYK (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Automation loop graceful exit: When tasks require daemon/multi-file coordination exceeding loop capacity, system correctly escalated 3 tasks to automation:needs_review with comprehensive handoff notes. Clean exit without attempting incomplete implementations. Session 01KFTDBQB3Z0PA5Y0GQD8T1RW3 iteration 17.\n  [friction] @01KFTPNH (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Task automation eligibility assessment happens too late in workflow. Tasks get marked automation:eligible without scope assessment. Automation loops then waste iterations doing [LOOP-FAIL:1], [LOOP-FAIL:2], [LOOP-FAIL:3] assessments before marking automation:needs_review. Example: task @01KFMMXF assessed in iterations 6, 7, 14 as too complex (~300-500 lines) but stayed in eligible pool causing repeated evaluation. Assessment should occur BEFORE tasks marked eligible, not after automation attempts them. Related to @task-automation-eligibility but focuses on pre-eligibility workflow gap.\n  [friction] @01KFTPN9 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Task-work-loop workflow lacks exit condition for 'eligible but unsuitable' scenario. When eligible tasks exist but all exceed single-iteration capacity (e.g., 5 P3 tasks requiring 300-500 lines each), workflow continues to step 4 (start task) with no clean exit path. Had to manually cancel workflow in iteration 16. Exit conditions defined (no eligible tasks, already done, work complete) but not 'all eligible tasks too complex'. Affects ralph when only complex work remains in eligible pool.\n  [friction] @01KFTP0G (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Automation loop cannot handle daemon-dependent E2E test verification. Pattern: Tasks get to ~80-85% complete (implementation done, test IDs added, tests enabled) but then block on daemon startup + iterative test debugging. Three tasks hit this in session 01KFTDBQB3Z0PA5Y0GQD8T1RW3 (iteration 14): @01KFP84N, @01KFP84S, @01KFQAGY. Root cause: Daemon requires manual 'npm run serve', E2E failures unpredictable, debugging cycle exceeds single-iteration capacity. Workaround: Mark automation:needs_review and exit loop. Impact: Web UI E2E testing tasks systematically blocked from automation completion. Potential solutions: (1) Automate daemon lifecycle in loop, (2) Split tasks into 'implementation' + 'E2E verification' phases, (3) Add automation:daemon-required tag for better categorization.\n  [success] @01KFTMKW (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Batching related E2E test tasks into a single PR branch: Completed 3 tasks (@01KFP85D observations panel, @01KFP85G error handling, @01KFP85M navigation) on feature branch feat/observations-panel-e2e-tests. All tasks involved E2E tests and data-testid attributes for web UI. Benefits: (1) Avoided multiple PR context switches and branch overhead, (2) Shared commits across tasks (e.g., error-message testid used by all 3), (3) Coherent scope for review. Pattern works when tasks are tightly related, share common files, and can be reviewed together. Session completed 4 tasks total efficiently using this approach.\n  [success] @01KFTKCK (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Task verification pattern: Before starting test-only task (01KFP854 - E2E tests), verified WebSocketManager implementation existed. Read manager.ts to confirm AC coverage (ac-28 to ac-32) before committing to E2E test creation. This prevented wasting iteration on tests for non-existent features. Pattern: For tasks scoped as 'write tests for X', verify X implementation exists first. Especially valuable in automation loops with limited iteration budget.\n  [success] @01KFTJNJ (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Task-work workflow (@task-work-loop) successfully enforced complete task lifecycle in automation loop: task selection from eligible list, verification before start, implementation with notes during work, DoD validation, commit with trailers, submit to pending_review, PR creation. Session 01KFTDBQB3Z0PA5Y0GQD8T1RW3 iteration 9 completed @01KFP850 (command palette E2E tests) from start to PR without friction. Workflow prevented common mistakes: starting without verification, skipping notes, missing DoD checks.\n  [friction] @01KFTJNA (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Automation loop cannot verify E2E tests that require daemon running. Pattern: Tasks add data-testid attributes and create E2E tests, but verification requires manual session to start daemon (npm run serve -w packages/web-ui) and run tests. Repeated across @01KFP84N (tasks view), @01KFP84S (items view), @01KFP84X (inbox view) in session 01KFTDBQB3Z0PA5Y0GQD8T1RW3. Tasks marked automation:needs_review and stall at ~70-80% complete. Potential solutions: headless daemon fixture for E2E tests, mock API layer, or split tasks (implementation + verification).\n  [success] @01KFTHKM (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Task-work-loop workflow provided effective structure for automation. Clear steps (filter eligible → select → verify → start → work → commit → submit → PR → exit) prevented skipped steps. Workflow state tracking enabled clean handoffs. Successfully completed inbox view implementation (01KFP84X) following all steps systematically.\n  [friction] @01KFTHKC (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Session checkpoint hook blocks exit when in_progress tasks have automation:needs_review status. Hook expects all in_progress tasks to either complete or have handoff notes. However, tasks marked automation:needs_review are intentionally paused for human review. Workaround: manually add comprehensive handoff notes before exit. Pattern suggests automation:needs_review should either auto-add handoff notes or be recognized as valid exit state.\n  [friction] @01KFTHK6 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    E2E test tasks hit systematic blocker in automation loops: Cannot run daemon (npm run serve) + E2E tests (npm run test:e2e) within loop constraints. Pattern affects all web UI E2E test tasks. Results in [LOOP-FAIL] notes and automation:needs_review escalation. Encountered on tasks 01KFP84N, 01KFP84S, 01KFP84X in iteration 8. Implementation completes successfully (test IDs + test files), but verification step requires human-driven session with long-running daemon.\n  [success] @01KFTH5G (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop error handling protocol (PR #243) executed correctly in iteration 7: [LOOP-FAIL:N] notes tracked failures per task, failure count visible across iterations, auto-escalation to automation:needs_review triggered at threshold 3. Pattern enables clear visibility into which tasks are struggling in automation and systematic escalation when human intervention needed. Tasks @01KFP84N and @01KFP84S hit 3rd failure (daemon requirement blocker) and were properly escalated.\n  [friction] @01KFTGRP (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop mode failure tracking not queryable: [LOOP-FAIL:N] notes exist but no CLI command to check task failure count. Had to manually grep task notes to determine if @01KFP84N and @01KFP84S hit 3-failure threshold (both at count 2). Needed for automation decision: should loop retry task or escalate to automation:needs_review? Suggest: kspec task get @ref --loop-failures or similar to return failure count.\n  [friction] @01KFTGKV (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop iteration 6 encountered systematic blockers: All eligible P2 tasks are web UI E2E tests requiring daemon startup + test verification, exceeding single iteration capacity. Both in-progress tasks (@01KFP84N, @01KFP84S) hit same blocker (daemon requirement) and now at failure count 2. Attempted @01KFMMXF (export command) but ~300-500 line implementation scope also exceeds capacity. Pattern suggests: (1) Web UI E2E tasks incorrectly marked automation:eligible - should be automation:needs_review until daemon automation exists, OR (2) Tasks should be split: 'Add test IDs' (automatable) + 'Verify E2E tests pass' (manual), OR (3) DoD should be relaxed to 'Tests enabled, pending verification'.\n  [success] @01KFTFX9 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Comprehensive session-end notes enable clean task handoffs. Final note on @01KFP84N documented: what's done, what remains, estimated effort, specific next steps, blockers, recommendations. This level of detail makes continuing work in next iteration frictionless - no context rebuilding needed. Pattern: when leaving task in_progress, include (1) completed work summary, (2) remaining work list, (3) effort estimate, (4) next concrete steps, (5) blockers/considerations. Especially valuable in automation loops where next iteration may be hours/days later.\n  [success] @01KFTFX1 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Partial progress commits valuable in automation loops. Iteration 4 on @01KFP84N: added test IDs to components (fd70b86d) even though DoD required fully enabled tests. Committing partial work provided value: (1) next iteration can continue from checkpoint, (2) incremental progress visible in git history, (3) work not lost if task abandoned. Better than all-or-nothing approach. Pattern: commit meaningful increments even if task incomplete, document remaining work clearly in notes and commit message.\n  [friction] @01KFTFWS (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Task Definition of Done (DoD) not visible during task selection. Task @01KFP84N had DoD requirement 'All E2E tests pass - no skips' in notes but wasn't surfaced during ready task selection. Workflow enforced DoD strictly at completion but constraint wasn't visible upfront. Created situation where task appeared eligible but became non-completable mid-work. DoD criteria are critical constraints that should influence task selection, not just exit validation. Suggest: extract DoD to structured field, show in task list, filter tasks by DoD compatibility.\n  [friction] @01KFTFWF (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Automation-eligible tasks lack time/scope estimates. All eligible tasks in iteration 4 were large-scope (10+ ACs, 30-60+ min work). Automation loops work best with 10-15 min tasks but selection mechanism doesn't filter by estimated effort. Existing eligibility system addresses complexity/clarity but not time dimension. Result: loops start work that can't complete in single iteration. Suggest: add estimated_effort field (small/medium/large) to tasks, --max-effort flag for automation.\n  [success] @01KFTESK (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Test-first development pattern: Writing comprehensive tests before integration implementation catches edge cases early and provides clear specification. Pattern used in loop-mode-error-handling task: (1) Created test file with 23 tests covering all 9 ACs, (2) Implemented utilities to make tests pass, (3) Integrated into ralph.ts. Benefits: TypeScript compilation errors caught immediately, edge cases identified before integration, tests serve as executable documentation. This approach is especially valuable for complex logic with multiple edge cases (failure tracking, progress detection, escalation thresholds).\n  [friction] @01KFT1QE (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Shadow branch divergence lacks guided resolution. When local and remote kspec-meta diverge significantly (61 vs 362 commits), kspec shadow sync just fails with no guidance. Manual git merge-tree diagnosis and conflict-by-conflict resolution required. @yaml-merge-driver would help but was cancelled.\n  [success] @01KFT1Q5 (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Subagent delegation for git history recovery: Pattern of spawn agent → search git log -p -S → extract lost content → report structured findings worked well for recovering 12+ lost notes from merge. Effective when content exists in history but was overwritten.\n  [friction] @01KFST52 (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Documentary test anti-pattern: Agents write test.skip() tests that pass CI but don't verify anything. Task framing as 'write tests' enables this. Root cause: tests treated as goal rather than validation mechanism.\n  [success] @01KFST50 (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Task reframing pattern: Change 'Write tests for X' to 'Implement X (AC-N to AC-M)' with implementation steps, required testIds, and 'No test.skip()' in Definition of Done. Prevents documentary test anti-pattern.\n  [success] @01KFST4Y (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Subagent audit pattern: Spawn agent to analyze multiple tasks → get structured findings → act on findings. Effective for task/test audits where parallel exploration needed.\n  [friction] @01KFS6P9 (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Workflow runs accumulate without lifecycle management. 34+ active runs exist with no clear way to complete/cancel old runs. Need: kspec workflow complete/cancel/cleanup commands to manage run lifecycle and prevent stale run accumulation.\n  [success] @01KFS6AX (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    E2E test creation workflow worked smoothly. Pattern established in project-selector.spec.ts provided clear template for documentary tests. Using test.skip() for pending implementation is effective for capturing expected behavior before UI exists. All 17 tests passed structure validation on first run.\n  [success] @01KFRCQH (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Using testUlid() helper for test fixtures prevents silent failures from invalid Crockford base32 chars (L, I, O, U). The helper auto-replaces these with valid chars. PR #210 adds documentation for this pattern.\n  [success] @01KFRAKV (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Incremental question-based requirements via AskUserQuestion: Asking one focused question at a time (task selection order, subagent model, skill invocation, error tracking) kept decisions clear and avoided ambiguity in spec design.\n  [success] @01KFRAKC (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Plan-Review-Revise cycle with subagents: Having a Plan agent design specs, then a second Plan agent review for gaps with full user constraint context, then fixing issues before presenting to user. Caught critical issues like ambiguous subagent spawn trigger.\n  [success] @01KFR0MB (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Subagent plan review before spec finalization - having a Plan subagent review the spec plan caught gaps (missing error cases, race conditions, path validation) that would have been harder to fix after writing the spec. Worth making standard practice for architecture specs.\n  [success] @01KFR0M0 (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Iterative question-based design with AskUserQuestion tool - asking one design question at a time during spec work (X-Kspec-Dir header choice, PID location, etc.) kept decisions focused and got clear answers. Better than presenting all options at once.\n  [success] @01KFQ8W7 (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    Capturing scope expansion immediately: When debugging revealed architectural issues beyond task scope, captured to inbox then promoted to proper task with full research context. Prevented context loss.\n  [success] @01KFQ8VZ (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    Subagent research pattern: Delegating architecture research to a subagent produced comprehensive findings with code examples, references, and concrete recommendations - more efficient than inline research\n  [success] @01KFQ59K (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    Same-origin architecture: daemon serving both web UI and API on single port simplifies CORS, WebSocket setup, and eliminates cross-origin complexity\n  [success] @01KFQ59D (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    E2E debugging: Playwright error-context.md files with page snapshots are invaluable for understanding what browser actually sees vs what code expects\n  [success] @01KFQ3AD (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    Detailed task implementation notes made E2E infrastructure setup straightforward - step-by-step guidance with verification checklist reduced ambiguity\n  [success] @01KFPH3Z (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    Documenting findings in task notes before implementing - created clear audit trail of decisions (daemon bundling approach) that future sessions can reference.\n  [success] @01KFPH3X (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    Investigation-first approach for bug fixes - reading task context, exploring code to understand root cause before proposing solutions. Found test failures were separate bug from the actual task issue.\n  [success] @01KFP9AN (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    AC-driven test design - Reading spec file directly and mapping tests to acceptance criteria ensured 100% coverage. Table format (AC | Test) made gaps immediately visible.\n  [success] @01KFP9AJ (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    Parallel subagent research for planning - Launching multiple explore agents simultaneously (one for best practices research, one for existing codebase patterns) provided comprehensive context quickly. Combined with Plan agent review, caught issues early before implementation.\n  [friction] @01KFNKFR (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    Interactive CLI tools block automation loops. Encountered with 'sv add tailwindcss' and 'shadcn-svelte init' - both require user input even with --yes flag. This forces either: (1) finding alternative non-interactive installation methods, (2) manual configuration, or (3) pre-scripted answers via expect/stdin. For automation-friendly workflows, prefer tools with true --yes/--no-interaction flags or configuration files over interactive prompts.\n  [success] @01KFNAT4 (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    PR merge workflow with review resolution: Successfully merged PR #189 using @pr-review-merge workflow. All CI checks passed (tests, claude-review). Greptile feedback was addressed in code but threads weren't programmatically resolvable - documented in PR comments instead. Workflow enforced systematic review before merge.\n  [success] @01KFMP73 (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Detailed task notes with code snippets, file paths, and AC mappings. Makes tasks self-contained for automation and future sessions without needing original context.\n  [success] @01KFMP6T (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Plan mode with subagent exploration before spec creation. Using Explore agent to gather context from related projects (kynetic) and Plan agent to review/refine plans caught gaps early.\n  [success] @01KFMH5S (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Trait-based approach for cross-cutting concerns - creating @trait-shadow-commit made implicit shadow commit behavior explicit, testable, and documented. Subagent exploration before planning caught 6 additional missing commits beyond initial 4. Spec-first implementation ensures AC annotations in code reference back to spec.\n  [success] @01KFM8HP (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Plan mode + subagent review workflow - Having a Plan agent review the plan before presenting to user caught several issues (exit code semantics, missing AC coverage, CLI signature mismatch) that would have caused rework. Worth replicating for complex specs.\n  [success] @01KFKSGW (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Hybrid skill-workflow model works well: skill as entrypoint providing context, workflow for structure and tracking. Reduced reflect skill from 215 to 152 lines while adding progress tracking.\n  [idea] @01KFKRBG (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Add step editing to workflow add command\n  [friction] @01KFKA4S (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Branch deletion without content verification: compared commit messages but didn't diff actual file contents. Should always run git diff <branch>:<file> <main>:<file> before concluding content is merged.\n  [success] @01KFKA4R (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop analysis: 5 parallel subagents (iterations, PR compliance, reflections, commits, review feedback) provided comprehensive audit coverage efficiently. Pattern: split analysis by concern, run in parallel, synthesize results.\n  [success] @01KFJHA3 (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Pattern for adding new validation types: Created separate validation module (validate-skills.ts), integrated into validate command with dedicated flag (--skills), added comprehensive tests in matching test file. This modular approach makes validation extensible without bloating the main validate.ts file.\n  [success] @01KFJGQE (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    E2E test pattern for multi-entity search worked well: tested each entity type individually first (inbox, observation, agent, workflow, convention), then attempted combined test. When combined test had issues, individual passing tests proved core functionality works. This approach provides both comprehensive coverage and clear failure isolation.\n  [success] @01KFJFZ1 (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop iteration completed two tasks efficiently: First reviewed and merged PR #164 (validation warning), then picked up next ready task (@01KFJ571 ACP type safety) and completed it with PR #165. The loop workflow (check PRs → merge if ready → pick next task) worked smoothly. Key pattern: Complete one task fully (including PR creation) before loop ends.\n  [success] @01KFJFVX (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Type safety improvements catch bugs at compile-time: Adding typed response methods (respondReadTextFile, respondWriteTextFile) to ACPClient prevents runtime bugs by enforcing correct response structures. The pattern of replacing 'unknown' with SDK types (ReadTextFileRequest, etc.) provides IDE autocomplete and catches errors early. This approach worked well - build caught all type mismatches immediately.\n  [success] @01KFJF6V (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    PR workflow from main branch with unpushed commits: create feature branch, reset main to origin/main, push feature branch. This cleanly separates completed work from main without losing commits.\n  [success] @01KFJF6N (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Implementing validation warnings follows a clear pattern: 1) Add warning type to interface, 2) Add check logic in validation function, 3) Add formatting in display function, 4) Add E2E test. The existing staleness checks provided a good template to follow.\n  [success] @01KFJENS (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Successfully executed ralph loop PR review workflow: spawned review subagent → identified MUST-FIX issue (missing tests) → added test coverage → pushed to PR branch → waited for CI → verified all checks pass → merged with --merge flag to preserve trailers. Review agent correctly identified the gap between stated goal (catch format bugs) and reality (no tests exercising the code).\n  [success] @01KFJENJ (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review workflow caught missing test coverage - the automated review correctly identified that new mock functionality had zero tests, blocking merge until tests were added. Added 6 comprehensive tests by spawning the mock subprocess and using ACPClient to validate the full JSON-RPC protocol flow. The review requirement to test through the actual mock process (not just unit testing the logic) ensured we validated the complete integration.\n  [success] @01KFJE2D (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Task @01KFJ52W completed smoothly using proper workflow: read real implementation first (src/cli/commands/ralph.ts), understood the protocol format, implemented matching behavior in mock. Reading the production code before implementing the test mock ensured format compatibility.\n  [success] @01KFJDE8 (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop PR workflow is now well-established: create PR for pending_review tasks, wait for CI, review comments, merge if clean, then complete task. This iteration demonstrated the full cycle twice (PR #161 merged, PR #162 created).\n  [success] @01KFJDBD (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Biome initialization process worked smoothly - the auto-init command detected the repo structure and configured appropriate excludes (dist/) and VCS integration automatically. Only manual change needed was adjusting indentation style from tabs to spaces to match existing code.\n  [success] @01KFJCZB (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Test-first verification for infrastructure changes: after making the helper change, ran full test suite to verify no regressions. The failing tests revealed a subtle stdin handling bug (empty string vs undefined) that would have been hard to catch otherwise. Full test coverage paid off immediately.\n  [success] @01KFJCZ4 (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Using spawnSync instead of execSync for test helpers provides better control over stdio streams. Key insight: execSync only returns stdout on success - stderr is lost. spawnSync captures both stdout/stderr in separate properties regardless of exit code. This pattern is more predictable for testing CLI tools that write warnings to stderr.\n  [success] @01KFJCFN (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Review subagent pattern from RALPH_LOOP_INSTRUCTIONS.md worked exceptionally well. Single Task tool invocation with focused prompt ('check code quality, test coverage, alignment with spec, be strict about AC coverage') produced comprehensive review in table format covering: AC coverage (100%), code quality (excellent), test quality (E2E, no fluff), CI status (passing), files changed summary, and clear merge decision. The strict AC coverage requirement caught that all 4 direct ACs + 6 inherited trait ACs were properly tested. This automated review quality rivals human PR reviews.\n  [success] @01KFJCFD (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Complete iteration 12 workflow executed successfully: Found pending_review task → Created PR #160 with /pr skill → Spawned review subagent → Review approved with 100% AC coverage → Merged PR with --merge strategy → Pulled merged changes to local main. The automated review subagent provided excellent quality checks (AC coverage, test quality, code patterns). Total time from pending_review to merged: ~8 minutes. Key learning: Task should stay in pending_review until AFTER merge (I completed it prematurely after PR creation).\n  [success] @01KFJC2A (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Task state transition worked well: pending_review status held the completed work until PR was created, then completing the task after PR creation provides clean separation. This matches the RALPH_LOOP_INSTRUCTIONS.md pattern: task submit → PR creation → task complete after merge. However, I completed the task immediately after PR creation rather than after merge - should verify if this is the intended workflow or if completion should wait for merge.\n  [success] @01KFJC23 (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    The /pr skill workflow (auto-generate branch name from context, move commits from main to feature branch, create PR) worked seamlessly for shipping completed work. The skill correctly derived 'feat/workflow-advanced-features' from the task reference, handled the branch creation and main reset cleanly, and created a comprehensive PR. This pattern is efficient for the ralph loop where work completes on main and needs to be shipped via PR.\n  [success] @01KFJBZJ (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Workflow advanced features implementation completed smoothly. Key success factors: (1) Read existing workflow implementation first to understand patterns, (2) Extended schema before implementing CLI commands, (3) Followed existing test patterns for comprehensive coverage, (4) All 13 new tests passed on first run without fixes needed. The incremental approach (schema → commands → tests) worked well for this feature.\n  [success] @01KFJBNX (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Automated PR review (Greptile) caught critical bug where entry_confirmed was never recorded - only exit_confirmed was working. The review identified that entry_confirmed remained undefined in all cases. This demonstrates the value of automated code review in catching semantic bugs that pass tests. The fix involved creating stub step results when advancing to the next step to capture entry_confirmed, then updating those stubs when the step completes.\n  [success] @01KFJAVE (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Test-driven workflow implementation pattern worked well: wrote comprehensive E2E tests covering all ACs before refining implementation. The tests caught a subtle ordering issue (exit criteria must be checked before entry criteria) and a logical issue (skipping should bypass entry criteria for next step). Having 15 tests covering edge cases (strict/advisory modes, skip/confirm/force combinations) gave confidence that the implementation was correct. All 915 tests passing confirms no regressions.\n  [success] @01KFJA3P (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Iteration 8 completed: Merged PR #157 (workflow step navigation), then completed task @01KFJ4VT (shadow hook tests). Created PR #158. Workflow: reviewed and merged open PR first (per RALPH_LOOP_INSTRUCTIONS), then picked highest priority ready task. Task had clear 3-case scope from notes. Added tests to existing shadow.test.ts file, all 61 tests pass. PR created with /pr skill - auto-generated branch name test/shadow-hook-tests from commit message and task context.\n  [success] @01KFJA21 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Added comprehensive E2E tests for shadow hook functionality. Tests verify: 1) Hook installation during init copies from hooks/pre-commit to .git/hooks/pre-commit with executable permissions, 2) Hook blocks unauthorized commits (verifies commit count stays at 1), 3) Hook allows commits with KSPEC_SHADOW_COMMIT=1 env var (verifies commit count increases and message is correct). Used real hook file for tests 2 and 3 to ensure accurate behavior. All tests run in isolated temp directories. Task notes captured implementation approach before coding.\n  [success] @01KFJ9YY (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Workflow step navigation implementation demonstrates excellent code-to-spec traceability. AC annotations used throughout both implementation and tests. Run reference inference (auto-detect single active run) provides great UX. Error messages include actionable guidance with exact commands. Summary output on completion (duration, step counts) matches spec requirements exactly. This is a model implementation for future workflow features.\n  [success] @01KFJ9YR (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review and merge workflow executed successfully. Used Task agent for comprehensive review (AC coverage, test quality, E2E strategy, test isolation all verified). All CI checks passed, automated reviews found no issues. Task completed cleanly with proper trailers and spec sync. Total time from PR creation to merge: ~4 minutes. The structured review process caught no issues because implementation quality was excellent from the start - comprehensive E2E test coverage, proper AC annotations, perfect test isolation.\n  [success] @01KFJ9NF (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Workflow step navigation implementation went smoothly. The existing workflow foundation (AC tests, error patterns, helper functions) made adding the next command straightforward. Test-first approach caught edge cases early (e.g., inferring run when exactly one active). Pattern of checking spec ACs against test coverage helped ensure nothing was missed.\n  [success] @01KFJ97V (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review workflow is effective: spawned review subagent found 3 incorrect spec references that would have been missed. The strict review caught that annotations referenced @shadow-init instead of @shadow-init-remote for remote detection code. Fixed before merge, maintaining proper traceability.\n  [success] @01KFJ8JB (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review-merge flow worked smoothly: spawned subagent for comprehensive review, verified CI checks passed, checked for review comments, merged with --merge to preserve trailers, then completed task. The strict review requirements (AC coverage, test quality, isolation) caught in advance prevent merge issues. Subagent review before PR creation + automated CI review + manual merge checks = robust quality gate.\n  [success] @01KFJ8J5 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Systematic approach to code cleanup - created comprehensive mapping document before making changes, built automated sed script to handle bulk replacements, then manually verified and fixed edge cases. This pattern of 'map-script-verify-fix' worked well for 72 annotations across 9 files with zero regressions (all 874 tests pass). Script-first approach with manual verification is effective for large-scale mechanical refactoring.\n  [success] @01KFJ7SA (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    When fixing bugs with AC coverage: First read the code to understand the implementation, then identify the exact function/logic that needs updating, then add tests that validate the AC before implementing. This workflow ensures the fix is correct and properly validated. For AC-15, updating getParentTaskRef() to filter cancelled tasks was straightforward once the problem was identified.\n  [success] @01KFJ7AP (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Tests should use non-shadow mode setup (files in project root) unless specifically testing shadow functionality. The .kspec/ directory is ONLY used when shadow branch is properly configured. This keeps test setup simple and matches how kspec actually discovers files in non-shadow environments. Pattern: create kynetic.yaml, kynetic.meta.yaml, project.tasks.yaml directly in temp root directory.\n  [success] @01KFJ7AG (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Test fixture ULID validation issue was quickly identified through systematic debugging. The root cause (invalid ULID format in test fixtures) was found by examining validation failures and understanding that ULIDs must match proper regex format. Fix was straightforward: import ulid() and generate valid ULIDs. This pattern of using valid test data that matches production validation rules is important for reliable tests.\n  [success] @01KFJ636 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Workflow run foundation implementation demonstrates good schema-first development pattern. Defined all Zod schemas first, then storage operations, then CLI commands, then tests. This ordering prevented type errors and made the implementation flow smoothly. The type safety from Zod schemas caught issues early in compilation rather than at runtime.\n  [success] @01KFHYJ9 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    When debugging opaque CI/auth errors, search GitHub issues early. A quick search for 'npm trusted publishers OIDC' would have found the npm version bug immediately, saving significant iteration time.\n  [success] @01KFHJP6 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    PR automated review catching AC test coverage gaps - the Claude reviewer on PR #141 identified two missing test assertions (--clear-deps and --automation author validation) that I'd missed. The strict AC coverage requirement in CLAUDE.md combined with automated review provides good safety net.\n  [success] @01KFGRVJ (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Spec-first with traits: Creating spec, adding traits, then deriving task immediately surfaced what tests were needed (trait ACs). Applied to --clear-deps feature.\n  [success] @01KFG1JN (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Task @extract-test-helpers was already completed before being picked up. The workflow correctly handled this by verifying implementation (tests/helpers/cli.ts exists with all helpers, no duplicates in test files, 841 tests pass) and documenting findings in task notes before marking complete. This demonstrates good verification practices when tasks may have been completed out of band.\n  [success] @01KFG1CX (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Task reset command workflow: Reset cancelled task to pending, then start/submit/complete with proper documentation. Fixed @task-yaml-formatting status efficiently using task reset flow.\n  [success] @01KFG13F (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review and merge workflow is efficient - spawned parallel review subagents for both PRs, got clear recommendations, merged both immediately, completed tasks, pulled changes, and started new work. The review agents provided comprehensive analysis including local test verification and CI status context.\n  [success] @01KFG0GW (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Vitest v1 to v4 major version upgrade required only minimal changes: single async/await fix in test file. Breaking changes were well-contained. The vitest v4 warning message ('Promise returned by expect(actual).rejects.toThrow(expected) was not awaited') was clear and pointed directly to the issue, making the fix trivial. Major version upgrades don't always require extensive refactoring if the codebase follows best practices.\n  [success] @01KFFZSM (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Tech debt cleanup pattern: When finding duplicate logic, check if the duplication serves a purpose (e.g., specialized behavior) or is truly redundant. In this case, inbox.ts had a local shortUlid() helper that was functionally identical to .slice(0,8) used elsewhere. The 'smart' ReferenceIndex.shortUlid() method wasn't needed for ephemeral inbox items without slugs. Solution: remove helper, use direct string slicing consistently. This makes code more maintainable and easier to understand.\n  [success] @01KFFZG3 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop PR review workflow successfully prevented corrupted code from being merged. The iteration followed RALPH_LOOP_INSTRUCTIONS.md: (1) Checked for open PRs, (2) Spawned review subagent, (3) Fixed blocking issues found, (4) Re-verified tests locally, (5) Merged with --merge to preserve trailers, (6) Completed task with proper documentation. This systematic approach caught and fixed a critical issue that manual review might have missed.\n  [success] @01KFFZFH (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Interactive rebase with automated editor worked well for fixing historical commits. Using GIT_EDITOR with sed to automatically mark commits for editing eliminated manual intervention. Pattern: GIT_EDITOR='sed -i \"s/^pick <hash>/edit <hash>/\"' git rebase -i <base>. This allowed scripted fixes to commit history while maintaining full control.\n  [success] @01KFFZFC (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review subagent workflow is effective. Spawning a dedicated subagent with strict review criteria caught the critical file corruption issue before merge. The subagent provided detailed analysis including: blocking issues prioritized, test coverage gaps identified, code quality assessment, and specific fix commands. This caught a problem that would have been difficult to spot in manual review.\n  [friction] @01KFFZF7 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Git handling of files with newlines in filenames is extremely difficult. Standard commands like 'git rm' with shell quoting don't work. Even null-terminated operations struggle with escaping. Solution: Python script with subprocess.run() successfully handled the special characters where shell commands failed. This type of git corruption should be prevented at commit time if possible.\n  [success] @01KFFZ0F (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Test helper API documentation was critical for fixing tests. The kspec() helper signature (string args, not array) wasn't obvious from test file alone. Reading tests/helpers/cli.ts revealed correct usage pattern. Future: consider adding JSDoc examples in helper file showing common patterns.\n  [success] @01KFFYC3 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    findChildItems already existed in items.ts with correct implementation - avoided duplicate code by discovering existing helper. Good pattern: search for existing helpers before implementing new ones.\n  [success] @01KFFXV3 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Test error message checking pattern: When commands fail, error messages can be in either stdout or stderr. Using 'const output = result.stdout + result.stderr' and checking that combined output is more robust than checking just stdout. This worked for all 4 initially failing tests.\n  [success] @01KFFXTY (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    KSPEC_TEST_TTY pattern works well for testing interactive prompts. By adding an env var check alongside process.stdin.isTTY, we can simulate TTY in tests while preserving real TTY detection in production. This pattern can be reused for other interactive commands.\n  [success] @01KFFX1B (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Iteration 16: Successfully completed full PR workflow - reviewed and merged PR #122 (task set batch), blocked unclear task @01KFBDR7 (Result types), completed and shipped @01KFBJFE (dedupe formatRelativeTime) via PR #123. Pattern: PR review -> merge -> pick new task -> implement -> commit -> PR. Task selection improved: skipped manual_only task, blocked ambiguous task, picked clear tech-debt task. Total: 2 PRs processed (1 merged, 1 created).\n  [friction] @01KFFWV2 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Task @01KFBDR7 'Improve Result type enforcement' has unclear scope. Initial investigation shows current Result pattern (discriminated unions with 'ok' field) is well-implemented and consistently used. Most code properly checks .ok before accessing success fields. Task note suggests multiple improvements (branded types, lint rules, never throw library) but unclear which is actually needed or would provide value. Without concrete examples of bugs or near-misses from this pattern, hard to prioritize improvements. Recommendation: Block task pending concrete evidence of problems, or pivot to documentation task.\n  [success] @01KFFWR2 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop PR review workflow successful: spawned review subagent for pending_review task, agent performed comprehensive AC coverage check (13 ACs validated), test quality assessment (E2E strategy, isolation), and edge case verification. Review found no issues. Waited for CI, then merged with --merge flag to preserve trailers. Pattern: pending_review task -> spawn review agent -> wait for CI green -> merge -> complete task. This workflow ensures systematic quality checks without human intervention.\n  [success] @01KFFWAJ (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Test-first implementation worked well for task set batch. Writing all 15 tests first (based on spec ACs) provided clear targets and caught issues early. The 'write tests → implement → fix → repeat' cycle was faster than trying to implement and test simultaneously.\n  [success] @01KFFW9Q (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Batch operation pattern in batch.ts worked well for task set implementation. executeBatchOperation() handled all the complexity of partial failures, ref deduplication, and output formatting. Helper function extraction (setTaskFields) allowed clean code reuse between single and batch modes.\n  [success] @01KFFVG0 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Stop hook successfully caught incomplete session cleanup. Ralph loop should always checkpoint in-progress tasks before ending iteration, even if not completing them. The hook reminder ensures continuity for next session.\n  [success] @01KFFVEK (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop iteration 14: Successfully merged PR #121 (JSON output consistency), researched drift detection task (found it under-specified), and started well-defined task-set-batch task. Pattern: When a ready task lacks concrete examples or clear scope, checkpoint it with observations and move to a better-defined task rather than spinning on requirements gathering.\n  [friction] @01KFFVC7 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Task @01KFBDQE 'Add spec-schema drift detection' needs clearer scoping. The original note mentions three issues but #2 appears to be a misunderstanding (already handled by --staleness check), and #3 (fixture conflicts) lacks concrete examples. Tasks derived from retrospective observations should include specific examples or reproduction steps, not just abstract descriptions of problems.\n  [success] @01KFFTSZ (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop iteration 13 completed successfully: Reviewed and merged PR #120, then completed task @01KFBDM8 (JSON output consistency audit). The PR review workflow worked smoothly with automated CI checks. The audit task was well-scoped with clear notes about what to investigate. Created PR #121 for the fix. Key efficiency: Using TodoWrite to track sub-tasks within the larger audit task helped maintain focus.\n  [success] @01KFFTRJ (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    JSON output audit revealed mostly good patterns - only one inconsistency found (clone-for-testing had its own --json flag). The systematic approach of searching for JSON.stringify, checking stderr handling, and documenting envelope format conventions was effective. Key insight: envelope format variation is intentional and serves different use cases (raw data vs success confirmations vs errors vs hook-specific formats).\n  [success] @01KFFT48 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    PR creation workflow smooth: auto-generated branch name from task slug, created branch, reset main, pushed, created PR with comprehensive summary including AC coverage checklist. The /pr skill handled the complexity well.\n  [success] @01KFFT1J (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Test helper pattern (kspecWithStatus vs kspecError) worked well once I found the right function. The cli.ts helper exports are well-structured for different test needs.\n  [success] @01KFFT1E (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    State enforcement implementation went smoothly - clear spec with detailed ACs made implementation straightforward. Breaking existing tests was expected and fixing them reinforced the new workflow.\n  [success] @01KFFSGA (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Spec status audit revealed that implementation often outpaces spec updates. ULID shortening, cascade updates, and YAML conventions were all implemented but marked not_started. The cascade feature in item set updated 18 child items automatically, demonstrating the value of hierarchical status propagation. Shadow branch separation meant no main branch PR was needed for metadata updates.\n  [success] @01KFFS6V (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Successfully completed full PR review cycle in single iteration: reviewed 2 PRs in parallel, merged 1 immediately (PR #118), fixed failing test in other (PR #119), waited for CI to confirm fix, then merged. Both tasks (@01KFBPQA, @01KFC3AR) completed. The --watch flag on gh pr checks was useful for waiting on CI completion.\n  [success] @01KFFS1P (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    RALPH_LOOP_INSTRUCTIONS.md PR review workflow is well-designed: check for PRs first, spawn review subagents, use decision tree (pass→merge, fail→fix). Following this process caught issues before merge and ensured quality. The --merge flag (not --squash) preserves kspec trailers which is critical for kspec log @ref functionality.\n  [friction] @01KFFS1G (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Shell escaping in tests is tricky across environments. The $'...' syntax worked locally but failed in CI. Using printf with double-escaped newlines ($(printf 'Line 1\\\\nLine 2\\\\nLine 3')) is more portable and explicit. Tests should prefer printf over $'...' for multiline string handling in bash commands.\n  [success] @01KFFS1A (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review subagents worked excellently in parallel. Spawned two review agents simultaneously - both produced comprehensive, strict reviews identifying issues (PR #119 had failing test, PR #118 was clean). The parallel approach saved time and both reviews were thorough with proper AC coverage validation.\n  [success] @01KFFRDE (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Iteration 8 complete - accomplished multiple tasks:\n\n1. Reviewed PR #118 (log empty repo fix) - comprehensive review passed\n2. Implemented @01KFC3AR (--description flag) with full AC coverage\n3. Created PR #119 for --description implementation\n4. Fixed failing CI test in PR #118 (missing git config)\n5. Captured observations (success + friction patterns)\n\nKey patterns:\n- PR review workflow with subagent worked well\n- Implementation -> tests -> PR flow is smooth\n- CI failures often reveal env config issues (git user.name/email)\n- Test patterns: always configure git after init in temp dirs\n\nBoth PRs now ready for merge once CI completes.\n  [friction] @01KFFRD1 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    CI test failures can occur when tests don't configure git user.name/email before running git commit in temp dirs. Pattern to follow: Always add 'git config user.email/user.name' commands after 'git init' in tests that create fresh repos and commit. Found in PR #118 - test passed locally but failed in CI.\n  [success] @01KFFR8H (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Iteration 8: Successfully implemented @01KFC3AR (--description flag for task add) from start to PR creation. Pattern that worked well:\n1. Read spec to understand all ACs\n2. Check existing schema (description field already existed)\n3. Add CLI option and wire to TaskInput\n4. Write comprehensive tests FIRST with AC annotations\n5. Fix shell escaping issues in tests (not implementation bugs)\n6. All 792 tests pass on first implementation try\n7. Created PR with full context\n\nTotal time: ~15 minutes for feature + tests + PR. Clean handoff to next iteration.\n  [success] @01KFFQSB (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    PR creation workflow smooth - auto-generated branch name from task slug, moved commit from main to feature branch, created comprehensive PR body from task/spec context. Task notes provided all context needed for PR summary.\n  [success] @01KFFQQA (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Test fixture management pattern: Using setupTempFixtures() + removing .git + git init creates clean test environment with kspec structure but no commits. More reliable than manually constructing YAML. Pattern used successfully in AC-2 and AC-5 tests.\n  [success] @01KFFPY6 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Skipped test was actually ready to run - validation infrastructure improvements had resolved the original issue. Worth periodically reviewing skipped tests to see if they can be unskipped. Test passed immediately when .skip was removed.\n  [success] @01KFFPY1 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review workflow working well: Claude review posted actionable feedback (null check for comment.author), fix was straightforward (optional chaining), all checks passed after fix, and merge proceeded smoothly. The automated review catch prevented a potential production bug.\n  [success] @01KFFPWF (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop PR workflow is working well: started iteration by checking for open PRs, reviewed and merged PR #115 (fixing spec trailers), then picked next priority task. PR review → merge → task selection creates natural workflow cadence.\n  [success] @01KFFPWA (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    CI workflow implementation was already partially complete from previous session - checking git status and existing files before starting work prevented duplicate effort. The workflow uses GitHub GraphQL API to query review thread resolution status, which is more robust than REST API approach for checking unresolved comments.\n  [success] @01KFFP5D (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    CI workflow implementation pattern: GitHub Actions with github-script action provides clean GraphQL API integration without custom tooling. For review thread resolution checks, the isResolved and isOutdated fields are exactly what's needed - filtering for \\!isResolved && \\!isOutdated gives accurate results. Excluding outdated threads prevents false positives from force-pushed commits.\n  [success] @01KFFN83 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Test improvement strategy: AC-5 test was fluff (admitted in comments it didn't test the scenario). Rewrote to actually set up dependency scenario and validate via tasks ready command. Understanding how dependency checking works (at query time, not start time) was key to writing correct test.\n  [success] @01KFFN7T (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review workflow validated: spawned subagent for strict review before merge, identified blocking issues (spec field name mismatch, fluff test), fixed them, then merged. Review agent correctly flagged AC-5 test as not validating its criterion.\n  [success] @01KFFMS3 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    PR CI waiting pattern - when CI is pending on a PR, better to move to next task rather than blocking iteration. The PR will be picked up in a future iteration once CI completes.\n  [success] @01KFFMRZ (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Task scoping from retrospective notes works well - the task notes from the ralph loop retrospective provided clear, actionable improvements that translated directly into implementation steps.\n  [success] @01KFFM7N (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Task reset implementation went smoothly. Following existing patterns from task.ts (cancel, complete, block) made implementation straightforward. Comprehensive test coverage (15 tests for 6 ACs) caught no issues. Shadow commit verification simplified by trusting commitIfShadow success rather than parsing git internals. All 783 tests pass on first try.\n  [success] @01KFEZ86 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Spec-plan session validated guided workflow design - the manual process of creating the guided-workflow-execution spec followed exactly the pattern the feature would automate: requirements → exploration → design → review → spec creation → derive. Dogfooding opportunity once implemented.\n  [success] @01KFEYT8 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Breaking monolithic feature into requirements before deriving tasks produces self-contained tasks with proper dependencies. One feature with 14 ACs became 4 requirements with 4-6 ACs each.\n  [success] @01KFEYT3 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Subagent review of specs before implementation caught 4 missing ACs and 2 missing flag behaviors. Worth doing for non-trivial specs.\n  [success] @01KFE1BE (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Fixed spec-implementation mismatch by updating the spec to match working implementation. Key insight: When spec and implementation diverge, evaluate which is more useful before blindly 'fixing' code to match spec. In this case, task dependency checking was more actionable than spec hierarchy checking, so updating the spec was the right choice. The tests validated that the implementation was correct for what it does.\n  [success] @01KFE13K (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Spec review task series completed efficiently. Breaking down large spec updates into 7 focused tasks (trait creation, AC addition, trait application by command category) enabled systematic progress tracking and validation at each step. TodoWrite tool helped track progress within each task.\n  [success] @01KFE0RJ (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Shadow branch auto-commit workflow: File changes made via Edit tool are automatically committed when kspec commands (like task note) run. No manual git add/commit needed for shadow-tracked files. This is the expected behavior and works well.\n  [success] @01KFE0EM (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Trait application pattern for CLI commands: (1) All commands get json-output, semantic-exit-codes, error-guidance. (2) Batch operations add multi-ref-batch. (3) Destructive operations add confirmation-prompt. Applied consistently to 7 task lifecycle commands. Pattern is clear and repeatable for remaining commands.\n  [success] @01KFE0E7 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Shadow branch auto-commit pattern works seamlessly for spec changes. Modified cli.yaml (spec file) and all changes were automatically committed to shadow branch via kspec commands (task note, task complete). No manual git operations needed. This confirms the design - spec changes live in shadow branch, auto-committed by CLI.\n  [success] @01KFE03D (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Systematic AC addition workflow worked efficiently: grouped items by category (schema types, task types, relationships, YAML), added 2 AC per item following given/when/then format. Pattern: each AC should test a specific aspect of the item's behavior. For type definitions, one AC validates structure and one validates usage/purpose.\n  [success] @01KFDZVT (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Debugging approach for YAML loading issues:\n1. Used node scripts to test individual components (initContext, extractItemsFromRaw, SpecItemSchema)\n2. Isolated the failure point (ULID validation regex)\n3. This incremental debugging approach was much faster than adding console.logs to source code\n\nPattern to remember: when kspec commands don't work as expected, test the parser/schema layers directly with minimal scripts.\n  [friction] @01KFDZVK (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Manually editing .kspec/kynetic.yaml to add traits requires:\n1. Understanding ULID format constraints (no I, L, O, U chars)\n2. Manually generating valid ULIDs\n3. Working around shadow branch auto-commit restrictions\n\nThe kspec trait add command would have handled all this, but for bulk creation of 8 traits with detailed AC, manual YAML editing was more practical. Future improvement: support adding AC during trait creation, or bulk trait import from YAML file.\n  [success] @01KFDZGT (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    E2E validation workflow effective for confirming system readiness. Created test data, verified behavior, cleaned up - all within single iteration. Trait system confirmed production-ready without needing to create permanent test fixtures.\n  [success] @01KFDZBX (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Completed first task in spec review sequence (@01KFDYAW). Simple cleanup task (removing non-existent file reference) was straightforward. Shadow branch auto-commit worked correctly - no manual commit needed for .kspec/ changes.\n  [success] @01KFDPM7 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph Loop iteration completed successfully: (1) Checked for open PRs per instructions (2) Found PR #106 with failing tests (7/8) (3) Launched review subagent to identify issues (4) Fixed all 3 blocking issues (worktree metadata cleanup, test setup, helper usage) (5) Verified locally (8/8 tests + full suite 707 passing) (6) Pushed and waited for CI (all green) (7) Merged PR and completed task @01KFBBTT (8) Captured 4 observations. Full workflow from PR review to merge completion in single iteration.\n  [friction] @01KFDPKZ (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Shadow branch worktree gets removed when checking out branches. After merging PR and checking out main, .kspec/ worktree was removed. Attempting to recreate with 'git worktree add .kspec kspec-meta' created nested .kspec/.kspec/ worktree breaking kspec. Fix required absolute paths and manual cleanup. Root cause: git worktree add interprets paths relative to CWD which may be inside .kspec. Consider: (1) kspec commands that detect missing worktree and auto-repair (2) fix spawn error in 'kspec shadow repair' (3) prevent nested worktree creation.\n  [success] @01KFD2YY (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Systematic debugging approach worked well: (1) Verified git clone works standalone, (2) Added debug output to trace actual parameters, (3) Discovered output was empty not error, (4) Checked test helper return types, (5) Found pattern in working tests. Each step narrowed the problem space until root cause was clear.\n  [friction] @01KFD2YS (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Optional positional parameter order in CLI commands can create usability issues. [source] [dest] signature made it impossible to provide dest and default source. Changed to [dest] [source] which better matches common usage patterns. Consider: use named options (--source, --dest) for commands where both parameters are truly optional and order-independent.\n  [success] @01KFD2YK (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Test helper pattern clarity - integration.test.ts showed the correct pattern of importing kspecOutput as kspec for string output. When tests fail due to type mismatches (expecting string, getting object), check imports against working test files.\n  [success] @01KFD1TC (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Bug investigation pattern: When validation passes unexpectedly, check item counts in output. 'Items checked: 3' vs expected 5 revealed traits weren't being loaded. Count mismatches are powerful debugging signals for data loading issues.\n  [success] @01KFD0VX (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Merged PR #104 (trait CLI commands) successfully. Review subagent found 0 blocking issues, 100% AC coverage (8/8), all tests passing. Production-quality implementation with proper isolation and AC annotations.\n  [success] @01KFD0DW (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review feedback loop worked well: (1) Subagent identified test quality issues with clear MUST-FIX verdict, (2) Systematic debugging traced root causes (YAML structure, slug conflicts, missing deps), (3) Fixed all 12 test failures, (4) All 695 tests pass locally, (5) PR updated and CI re-running. The strict review criteria (E2E tests, temp dirs, AC coverage, no fluff) ensured high quality implementation.\n  [success] @01KFD0A8 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Systematic test debugging approach worked well: (1) Run tests to see failures, (2) Isolate root cause through manual reproduction, (3) Fix fixtures to match actual kspec requirements, (4) Verify all tests pass. Using manual CLI invocations in temp dirs helped identify structural issues that weren't obvious from test code alone.\n  [success] @01KFCZ4S (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Used dedicated review subagent to identify critical schema bug in PR #104. The subagent's strict review caught that ManifestSchema was missing the traits field, causing Zod to strip traits during parsing. This was a blocking bug that made all functionality fail. Fixed the issue and verified through extensive manual testing. Pattern: Always use review subagents before merge, they catch bugs that aren't obvious from code inspection alone.\n  [success] @01KFCYH1 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Trait CLI implementation followed clean separation: parser infrastructure (TYPE_TO_CHILD_FIELD, NESTED_ITEM_FIELDS) before CLI commands. This pattern worked well - infrastructure changes enabled CLI to use existing patterns (addChildItem, buildIndexes) without special cases. E2E tests caught integration issues early (traits not loading from manifest).\n  [friction] @01KFCXYZ (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    CI checks can take 3-4 minutes to complete, which can consume a significant portion of an iteration when waiting for merge approval. Consider: (1) Starting CI early if possible, (2) Using wait time productively for reflection/documentation, (3) Maybe implementing a timeout strategy in Ralph Loop for when CI takes too long.\n  [success] @01KFCXY2 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Full PR review → fix → merge cycle completed successfully in one iteration. The Ralph Loop workflow of dedicating review subagent, fixing issues, waiting for CI, and merging is working smoothly. Key: strict review criteria and blocking on CI checks before merge.\n  [success] @01KFCXQH (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review workflow caught test quality issues before merge - the automated subagent review identified fluff tests and missing documentation that would have degraded test quality patterns in the codebase. The strict review criteria (MUST-FIX vs suggestions) worked well.\n  [success] @01KFCXGB (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Trait AC coverage validation implementation went smoothly by building on existing infrastructure. The TraitIndex and checkCompleteness() architecture made it straightforward to add inherited AC checking - just scan trait ACs the same way we scan direct ACs. The existing test coverage scanning and ref validation already handled most of the heavy lifting. Clean separation of concerns paid off.\n  [success] @01KFCX5D (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Systematic debugging approach: When implementation code looks correct but tests fail, investigate test infrastructure first. Created isolated debug scripts to replicate exact test scenario, which revealed YAML parser only reading first document. This pattern of creating minimal reproduction scripts in scratchpad is valuable for debugging test failures.\n  [success] @01KFCVFM (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Trait display implementation completed efficiently by reusing TraitIndex foundation. Integrating into buildIndexes() made trait data available throughout CLI. Text and JSON output handled cleanly with separate formatting logic. Test fixtures required valid ULIDs - good validation caught this early.\n  [success] @01KFCTR3 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Following established code patterns (ReferenceIndex, AlignmentIndex) made TraitIndex implementation straightforward. The bidirectional mapping pattern and constructor-based indexing approach worked well for trait-spec relationships. Test structure with AC annotations provides clear traceability.\n  [success] @01KFCTE1 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop PR review workflow proved effective: dedicated review subagent correctly identified missing E2E tests as BLOCKING issue, prompted fix, re-review confirmed resolution, and merge completed successfully. The strict review criteria (AC coverage, E2E priority, test isolation) caught important gaps before merge.\n  [success] @01KFCSQF (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Traits field validation implementation followed clean pattern from existing ref validation. Adding to REF_FIELDS array and extending validateRefs() was straightforward. The test pattern from parser.test.ts made writing comprehensive tests easy. All 5 ACs covered in 6 tests.\n  [success] @01KFCS35 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Test-first debugging workflow effective: Added debug output to failing tests to identify root cause (invalid ULID format), then fixed and removed debug code. Faster than guessing.\n  [success] @01KFCR87 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    YAML migration task was straightforward - clear scope, no dependencies, simple find-replace pattern. This type of refactoring task is ideal for automation: well-defined changes, easy to verify with tests, low risk.\n  [success] @01KFCQZK (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review workflow with dedicated subagent worked well - caught missing test coverage before merge. Strict review guidelines (AC coverage required, no fluff tests, E2E preferred) ensure quality standards are maintained across iterations.\n  [success] @01KFCQ0S (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    PR workflow (#96 → #97) demonstrates smooth iteration loop: review existing PR → merge → pick next task → implement → submit → create PR. The pending_review state effectively gates work and ensures each PR gets proper review before merge. Task @01KFC1GKG scope was well-defined (foundation only) which made it easy to complete in one iteration.\n  [success] @01KFCPF1 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Exit code fixes completed efficiently using TodoWrite to track all 6 individual changes. The todo list helped ensure no fixes were missed and provided clear progress visibility through the task.\n  [success] @01KFC3C2 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Task notes template for standalone implementation context worked well: Scope (what this task covers), Prerequisites (dependencies), Files (to modify/create), Patterns (code examples), Tests (where to add). Prevents 'see parent for context' band-aids.\n  [success] @01KFC3BV (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Subagent review of spec caught 3 blocking issues (AC collision, undefined JSON structure, display contradiction) that would have caused implementation problems. Pattern: review specs with critical eye before finalizing, not just for completeness but for internal consistency and edge cases.\n  [friction] @01KFC3BM (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Plan mode required multiple back-and-forth cycles. Missing upfront: output type (spec vs code), standalone requirements check, display/UX needs. Review subagent ran AFTER trying to exit plan mode instead of as a step before finalizing. Suggests plan mode needs structured phases or checklist.\n\n--- Session Metadata ---\n  Last updated: just now\n\n--- Quick Commands ---\n  kspec task start @01KHF2BK  # begin work\n  kspec triage inbox  # triage untriaged inbox items\n\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"ℹ Synced shadow branch from remote\n\n=== Session Context ===\nBranch: main | Generated: just now\nTasks: 0 active, 35 ready, 0 blocked, 738/797 completed | Inbox: 154\n\n--- Ready to Pick Up ---\n  P2 @01KHF2BK Revisit ralph wrap-up agent design and test coverage\n  P2 @01KHF2BN Create spec for validate --drift feature\n  P2 @01KHF34K Add code-level reflect gating in ralph based on iteration delta\n  P2 @01KHVMWP Write getting-started tutorial and README for external users\n  P2 @task-redesign-web-ui Redesign web UI holistically — interactive spec/design session #web-ui #design #spike\n  P2 @task-reconcile-validate-alignment-warning-baseline Reconcile validate alignment warning baseline (status mismatches + orphans) #validation #alignment #meta\n  P2 @task-triage-automation-eligible-missing-spec-ref Triage automation-eligible tasks missing spec_ref #validation #automation #tasks\n  P2 @01KJNPVV Design agent runtime architecture — from ralph loop to reactive agent service #design #ralph #agents #architecture\n  P2 @01KJS8BA Deprecate kspec ralph — show migration message pointing to kspec agent #cli #agent #deprecation\n  P2 @01KJS8BC kspec setup seeds built-in worker and reviewer agent definitions #cli #agent #setup\n  P2 @01KJS8V7 Evaluate and improve dispatch prompt for worker and reviewer agents #agent #dx #dispatch\n  P2 @01KJSATP Fix pre-existing test failure: enhanced-setup codex config.toml not created #bug #setup #codex #testing\n  P2 @task-concurrent-mutation-regression-suite Add concurrent mutation regression tests for write operations #testing #concurrency #parser\n  P2 @01KJVD7H Add explicit dispatch watch subscribe-handshake failure behavior #cli #websocket\n  P2 @task-dispatch-watch-transcript-fixtures Add dispatch watch transcript fixtures for adapter boundary patterns #cli #agent #websocket #tests\n  P3 @ralph-permission-prompt Implement permission prompting for ralph non-yolo mode #ralph\n  P3 @task-guided-workflow-execution Implement: Guided Workflow Execution #meta #workflows\n  P3 @01KGGXCH Create @trait-verbose-output for consistent verbose behavior #trait #cli #dx\n  P3 @01KGVGSH Auto-derive task dependencies from spec depends_on relationships #cli #derive #dx\n  P3 @01KGVGY4 Support ephemeral workflow runs without persistent state #workflow #dx\n  P3 @01KGVGZH Add kspec worktree command to spawn isolated agent workspaces\n  P3 @01KHASRB Add kspec merge-driver configure command #cli #dx #merge\n  P3 @01KJ4SM7 kspec item add needs --root flag for adding traits to project-level kynetic.yaml without --under. Cu\n  P3 @task-ci-e2e-tests Add E2E test job to CI #ci #infra\n  P3 @task-plan-completeness-warning-debt-reduction Create phased plan for completeness warning debt reduction #validation #completeness #quality\n  P3 @task-ralph-migration-guide Write migration guide from ralph to agent runtime #docs #migration\n  P3 @01KJS8BD Replace 06-ralph-loop.md template with agent dispatch documentation #docs #agent #templates\n  P3 @01KJS8BE Migrate ralph tests to agent runtime equivalents #testing #agent #ralph\n  P3 @01KJS8BF Update workflows to remove ralph references and use agent dispatch concepts #docs #workflow #ralph\n  P3 @01KJS8BG Update skills to reference agent runtime instead of ralph #docs #skills #ralph\n  P3 @01KJTHA4 Fix AC-10 test flakiness in cli-agent-commands #cli #testing #bug\n  P3 @01KJTHAB Fix core-skill-install test flakiness in shard 3 #testing #bug\n  P3 @01KJTHAC Fix cli-serve test flakiness in shard 3 #testing #bug #cli\n  P3 @01KJTP68 Add isolated HOME helper for daemon-sensitive CLI tests #test #daemon #reflection\n  P3 @01KJTP69 Create wait-for-startup helper for process/daemon tests #test #daemon #reflection\n\n--- Recent Activity ---\n  ✓ @01KJS8B9 Add dispatch rule flags to kspec meta add agent / meta set (just now)\n    ├─ be840eb feat(meta): add agent dispatch-rule CLI flags (11 minutes ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ 7d94099 feat(meta): add agent dispatch-rule CLI flags (#669) (just now, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS7PD Add --text flag to kspec session log show to replay agent output (26 minutes ago)\n    ├─ b20e5ad feat(session-log): add --text replay for session log show (1 hour ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ 74b5e76 feat: add --text replay mode to session log show (#668) (26 minutes ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS702 CLI DX: accept tags as alias for tag in batch commands (1 hour ago)\n    ├─ c56b6f8 feat(batch): accept tags alias in batch args (1 hour ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ bfb55d1 feat(batch): accept tags alias for tag in batch commands (#667) (1 hour ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS6ZZ CLI DX: accept P1/P2/P3 format as alias for numeric priority in batch commands (1 hour ago)\n    ├─ 14af23c feat(batch): WIP accept P* priority aliases in batch (3 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    ├─ 1af5278 test(batch): assert priority alias results via task list (2 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ d561c38 feat(batch): accept P1/P2/P3 priority aliases in batch (#666) (1 hour ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS6ZP Handle EPIPE in ACP framing layer — src/acp/framing.ts send() throws uncaught EPIPE when child proce (2 hours ago)\n    ├─ f05177c fix(acp): checkpoint EPIPE handling work (5 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ 6607641 fix(acp): handle EPIPE in JSON-RPC framing output path (#665) (2 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS6ZX CLI DX: add --description flag to task set command (currently requires task patch --data) (2 hours ago)\n    ├─ 00fed9c feat(task): add --description support to task set (3 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ d0a95d0 feat(task): add --description support to task set (#661) (2 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS6ZW kspec batch --commands '' hangs instead of erroring — empty/missing args should fail fast with a cle (2 hours ago)\n    └─ bbce145 fix(batch): fail fast when batch stdin input is missing (#660) (2 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS701 CLI DX: accept spec_ref as alias for spec-ref in batch commands (2 hours ago) - No implementation delta required. Verified on 2026-03-03 that batch args accept spec_ref alias for --spec-ref in task ad...\n  ✓ @task-dispatch-in-progress-priority Implement in_progress-first dispatch scheduling (3 hours ago)\n    ├─ cb67865 feat(dispatch): prioritize in-progress task dispatch (3 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    ├─ f1ad9c3 fix(dispatch): keep needs_work ahead of pending (3 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ d80157c Implement in_progress-first dispatch scheduling (#663) (3 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ○ eab566a fix(agent): harden dispatch autonomy and prompt timeout handling (#662) (3 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS6ZT kspec validate --json appends human-readable summary text after JSON payload, causing invalid JSON f (3 hours ago) - Merged in PR #659. Implemented structured-output purity fix for validate/lint so --json/--yaml no longer appends human-r...\n\n--- Inbox (oldest first) ---\n  57 untriaged | 67 deferred | 154 total\n\n  @01KJDZBA (5 days ago by @claude) [testing, ralph, infra]\n    Add second mock adapter registration for ralph tests. Currently --adapter-cmd registers a single 'custom' adapter, making it impossible to test different adapter IDs in real (non-dry-run) runs. Options: (1) --worker-adapter-cmd / --reviewer-adapter-cmd flags, (2) env-based adapter registration (e.g. KSPEC_TEST_ADAPTERS JSON), (3) allow --adapter-cmd to accept an optional name prefix like --adapter-cmd 'worker:node mock.js'. This would enable proper integration tests for ac-2 (reviewer subagent uses reviewer adapter), ac-7 (env injection for both adapters with different harness targets), and ac-12 (different adapter IDs in session metadata). Currently these are covered by code-level AC annotations and dry-run output tests, but lack end-to-end verification with the mock ACP.\n  @01KJF69V (5 days ago by @claude) [cli, validation]\n    kspec validate should warn about items with identical titles in the same module — two items both named 'Generation Progress UX' with different ULIDs caused confusion during a fix session in imgen project\n  @01KJG5A7 (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [bug, ralph, codex, sessions]\n    Task budget false-positive outside ralph loop when stale KSPEC_SESSION_ID is present (observed in codex session). Repro: with KSPEC_SESSION_ID set to an old session that has budget.json started_this_cycle=max_per_cycle, 'kspec task start @ref' fails with 'Task budget exhausted ... let iteration end naturally' even when not running ralph and no in-progress tasks. Expected: budget guard should apply only during active ralph loop iteration context, or stale/non-ralph session IDs should be ignored.\n  @01KJGA18 (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [bug, infra, skills, setup]\n    Diagnose skill verify drift/no-hash for plugin-provided core skills.\n\nObserved behavior:\n- kspec skill verify reports codex statuses as drifted/no-hash for help, observations, triage, reflect, triage-inbox, writing-specs.\n- kspec skill diff reports plugin-provided (no local render to diff).\n- Rendered skill files exist under .agents/skills.\n\nInvestigate:\n- render-hash semantics in .kspec/skills for plugin-provided skills\n- plugin-provided status handling in skill verify/status/diff paths\n- whether this is expected behavior or a bug in drift detection\n  @01KJGAE2 (4 days ago by @claude) [plan-import, automation, friction]\n    derive_from_specs produces sparse tasks: derived tasks get null description, no tags, automation unassessed. Agents picking up these tasks lack context to work independently. Consider: auto-populate description from spec description, copy plan tags, or allow plan document to specify per-task fields.\n  @01KJGEEQ0 (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, cli, spec]\n    Reflection follow-up: add explicit parent/ancestor context to kspec item get output (human and --json) so hierarchy is unambiguous without separate tree queries.\n  @01KJGEEQ21 (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, cli, ux]\n    Reflection follow-up: add optional ancestor-chain display for kspec item list --tree --search so matched descendants show parent context and avoid false orphan interpretation.\n  @01KJGEEQ2Y (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, validation, traits]\n    Reflection follow-up: add validation check for trait/command-contract contradictions (e.g., command AC prohibits --refs while applied traits require batch refs semantics).\n  @01KJGFKJ (4 days ago by @claude) [cli, ux]\n    Unified kspec get @ref dispatch: currently plans require 'kspec plan get' while specs/tasks use 'kspec item get' / 'kspec task get'. A single 'kspec get @ref' that auto-dispatches to the right entity type would reduce friction for agents and humans who don't know the entity type upfront.\n  @01KJGW8Z (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, testing, cli]\n    Add stale-dist guard for CLI-based tests: detect outdated dist/cli build in tests/helpers/cli.ts and fail fast with actionable guidance (or optional auto-build) to avoid misleading failures.\n  @01KJHAFV (4 days ago by @claude) [reflection, cli, workflow]\n    Meta workflow editing gap: kspec meta set cannot update workflow steps. Agents must delete+recreate workflow to edit a step, which can churn workflow ULIDs and temporarily break references. Add safe step-level update support (e.g., --set-step <index> --content/--on-fail) or preserve ULID on replace to avoid reference drift.\n  @01KJJHAR (3 days ago by @claude) [infra, cli, reflection]\n    Worktree guard escape hatch: add env var KSPEC_GUARD_OVERRIDE_DONT_USE that bypasses the guard hook for intentional recovery operations (git reset, rebase in .kspec). Name deliberately long to discourage agent use — humans-only for recovery scenarios.\n  @01KJK3YZ (3 days ago by @claude) [reflection, git, automation]\n    Add commit-scope guard for automated task work: before committing, detect pre-existing staged files unrelated to the current task and prevent accidental inclusion (or require explicit acknowledgment). In this iteration, unrelated staged files were included because they were already in the index before git add on task files.\n  @01KJK5XG (3 days ago by @claude) [reflection, testing, ralph]\n    tests/ralph.test.ts helper runRalph splits args on whitespace, so quoted values (e.g., --focus \"keep scope narrow\") break and cause misleading unknown-command/option failures. Improve helper to accept argv arrays or robust shell-like parsing for test args.\n  @01KJK84Y (3 days ago by @claude) [reflection, cli, tasks]\n    Reflection: fix-cycle ergonomics mismatch — task-work guidance says 're-submit' needs_work tasks, but CLI rejects kspec task submit when status is needs_work and requires explicit kspec task start first. Consider allowing direct submit from needs_work (auto-transition to in_progress) or improving error/help text to show required two-step command sequence.\n  @01KJKDBK (3 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, testing, sessions]\n    tests/cli/session-note-limit.test.ts has reproducible timeout instability in current baseline. During this iteration: full npm test timed out at lines 34/70/104, and isolated run timed out at lines 34/104 despite rebuilding. This appears separate from ralph prompt changes and indicates session-start note-limit path/perf regression or test flake that should be stabilized.\n  @01KJKKCB (3 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, cli, tasks]\n    Reflection: add file-based payload input to task notes (e.g., kspec task note @ref --file <path> and/or --stdin). Current command only accepts positional message, which makes multiline/special-character notes brittle and conflicts with the convention to use file-based payloads for safe shell handling.\n  @01KJKY1H (3 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, bug, batch, shadow]\n    Reflection follow-up: kspec batch can fail during copy-back with ENOTEMPTY on .kspec/sessions/<session-id> (observed while batching task start+note). The batch reports all subcommands succeeded, but exits non-zero and leaves shadow worktree in a temporarily broken/deleted-file state until manual recovery (e.g., git -C .kspec restore .). Improve batch copy-back robustness for session blob directories and avoid failing the batch when payload writes succeeded.\n  @01KJN378 (2 days ago by @claude) [bug, testing]\n    enhanced-setup.test.ts: 'should render core skills to codex output when codex is detected' fails — .codex/config.toml not created. Pre-existing failure (confirmed by stash test). Likely related to completed task @01KJEWKG — may need test update to match new codex config behavior.\n  @01KJN5MB (2 days ago by @claude) [reflection]\n    Fix pre-existing test failure: tests/enhanced-setup.test.ts 'should render core skills to codex output when codex is detected' fails with ENOENT on .codex/config.toml — test expects setup to create codex config but it is not being created. Confirmed pre-existing (not introduced by recent changes). Investigate whether codex config generation is missing or test fixture setup is incomplete.\n  Use: kspec triage inbox to triage untriaged items\n\n--- Observations (unresolved) ---\n  [friction] @01KJW0NZ (2 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Creating PRs with gh --body inline markdown containing backticks triggers shell interpolation; use --body-file for reliable automation.\n  [friction] @01KJVT2Q (3 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    CLI integration tests run against dist binary, so editing command options requires a full build before vitest; failures initially look like unknown-option regressions.\n  [success] @01KJVP3S (5 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Context-switch hygiene worked: checkpointing dirty work to a dedicated branch plus task-note handoff preserved progress and prevented losing work when switching priority tasks.\n  [friction] @01KJVB7XH (8 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Behavior-level AC intent can be missed when tests only verify internals. Add endpoint/log effect assertions earlier in task implementation to reduce fix-cycle churn.\n  [success] @01KJVB7XD (8 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Worker/reviewer loop with explicit MUST-FIX feedback improved quality: first review caught behavior-level AC gaps, second pass merged cleanly with clear audit trail.\n  [success] @01KJV1PFJ (11 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Task lifecycle loop (task-work + PR + spawned pr-review) provided clean end-to-end closure with merged PR and task completion in one pass.\n  [friction] @01KJV1PFE (11 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    PR review automation attempted APPROVE on self-authored PR and GitHub rejected it; review event selection should adapt to author/reviewer identity.\n  [success] @01KJTZYE (11 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Concurrency fix pattern: isolate race to a narrow atomic helper, migrate only hot mutation paths first, verify with targeted race regressions before full-suite run, then close via worker+review-agent loop.\n  [friction] @01KJTFVY (16 hours ago by @claude)\n    Testing CLI-layer decisions (e.g. isJsonMode() → onUpdate suppression) requires knowing which abstraction level to test at. Direct runInvocation unit tests give false positives; CLI subprocess tests hit adapter limitations; ESM spy approach is unreliable cross-module. The reliable pattern: use setJsonMode(true) + isJsonMode() from output.ts to replicate the exact CLI decision in-process, then assert on the result. Document this as the preferred pattern for testing CLI mode-gating behavior.\n  [success] @01KJSA6A (1 day ago by @claude)\n    Session log analysis for agent failure diagnosis: Python one-liner over events.jsonl extracted the exact tool call sequence and identified the ACP permission request gap in 2 minutes. The structured event log format made root cause analysis fast — tool_call status:pending -> 5min gap -> agent.failed was immediately diagnostic.\n  [friction] @01KJSA69X (1 day ago by @claude)\n    meta set has --add-skill but no --remove-skill. To fix a wrong skill slug on an agent, had to edit .kspec/kynetic.meta.yaml directly and trigger commit via task note. This is the second time a missing remove/clear flag forced a YAML edit (dispatch rules also have no CLI). These gaps accumulate quickly when wiring up new agents.\n  [friction] @01KJSA69R (1 day ago by @claude)\n    Skill slug namespace confusion: .agents/skills/ uses kspec- prefix (kspec-task-work) for rendered output, but .kspec/skills/ uses bare slug (task-work) for runtime injection. Agent definitions must use the bare slug — but nothing in the CLI or docs makes this distinction clear. Easy to wire up agents with wrong slug and get silent skill injection failure (skills silently skipped when file not found).\n  [friction] @01KJS6ESZ (1 day ago by @claude)\n    check-unresolved-comments CI check goes stale when review threads are resolved after the check already ran for that push. Fix: gh run rerun <run-id>. Reminder: resolve threads BEFORE or immediately after pushing to avoid this.\n  [friction] @01KJS6ESV (1 day ago by @claude)\n    Daemon packages/daemon/src/server.ts cannot be imported directly in vitest — transitive Bun/Elysia imports break module resolution. Pattern: recreate the logic as a pure function in the test file (see daemon-server.test.ts and daemon-web-ui-bundle.test.ts). Worth documenting as a testing convention for daemon code.\n  [friction] @01KJS6ESQ (1 day ago by @claude)\n    shx cp -r <src> <dest> is non-idempotent when dest already exists — creates nested subdirectory on repeated runs. Build scripts must always shx rm -rf <dest> before copying. Add to build/ci convention.\n  [success] @01KJRSMRE (1 day ago by @claude)\n    GraphQL thread resolution + empty commit pattern worked for stale CI: (1) kspec task submit → (2) resolve threads via GraphQL mutation → (3) push code or empty commit → (4) CI re-runs with threads resolved. Resolving threads before push avoids needing the empty commit.\n  [friction] @01KJRSMRA (1 day ago by @claude)\n    check-unresolved-comments CI check is triggered by push. If review threads are resolved AFTER a push (not before), CI does not re-run automatically. Workaround: empty commit to trigger fresh run. Correct order: resolve threads FIRST, then push code.\n  [success] @01KJRR5DG (1 day ago by @claude)\n    Fix cycle search-before-capture pattern worked well: searched for existing tracking of both friction points before capturing observations, confirmed neither was already tracked, then captured a single consolidated observation. This prevented duplication and kept the reflection tight.\n  [friction] @01KJRR5DC (1 day ago by @claude)\n    Fix cycle had two SHOULD-FIX items from regenerated files not committed: (1) kspec agents generate was run locally but not committed to the PR branch — reviewer caught stale kspec-agents.md with ralph references; (2) kspec skill render was not run at all — .agents/skills/ rendered files still had ralph references. Both are easy to miss because the commands succeed but leave uncommitted artifacts. Consider adding these steps to the pre-PR local-review checklist or the implementation checklist notes when working on migrations that touch templates/skills.\n  [friction] @01KJRF87 (1 day ago by @claude)\n    batch-write-buffer wiring blocked by subprocess spawn model: WriteBuffer is a process-local singleton but batch-exec.ts runs each command via spawnSync as a separate process. Buffer writes in the parent won't be visible to spawned child processes. Need to evaluate: (a) switch to in-process command execution, or (b) use a temp file protocol where children write to a staging dir and parent collects on success.\n  [friction] @01KJR8J2Y (1 day ago by @claude)\n    parseIntOption from src/cli/validators.ts repeatedly missed when implementing new CLI commands — parseInt+isNaN pattern introduced 4 times in one PR. Needs more prominent discovery surface (skill or convention).\n  [success] @01KJR8J2K (1 day ago by @claude)\n    Iterative Codex PR review loop (fix → push → re-review) converged in 2 passes and caught layered bugs not visible in the first pass. Good pattern for PR polish cycles.\n  [success] @01KJR42M (1 day ago by @claude)\n    /codex pr covering both tasks in a single pass was efficient and caught a subtle bug (parseInt partial-match \"5abc\"->5) that manual review missed. Dual-spec single-pass review avoids duplicated context-gathering and produces tighter non-redundant findings.\n  [success] @01KJR42C (1 day ago by @claude)\n    Reading actual code before judging PR review threads saved significant work: 11 of 19 open threads on PR #634 were stale (issues already fixed in the latest commit). Manual thread-by-code matching took ~10 minutes but avoided implementing fixes that were already done.\n  [friction] @01KJR425 (1 day ago by @claude)\n    agent.ts (PR #634) reimplemented parseInt+isNaN validation instead of using parseIntOption from src/cli/validators.ts — reintroducing the partial-parse bug (parseInt(\"5abc\")===5) that parseIntOption was built to fix. The helper exists and conventions doc was added after @01KGWDPV, but agents implementing new CLI commands still miss it. Suggests validators.ts needs more prominent callout in AGENTS.md or task templates for new CLI commands.\n  [friction] @01KJQWQ0 (1 day ago by @claude)\n    git -C <path> pattern prevents CWD poisoning in recovery scenarios. Agents running git inside .kspec/ via cd poison the shell CWD for the rest of the session (Claude Code persists CWD changes). Always use git -C /path/to/.kspec/ instead of cd .kspec/ && git. Consider adding this to AGENTS.md development conventions.\n  [friction] @01KJPK6MR (2 days ago by @claude)\n    Stale PR base masquerades as scope problem: when a feature branch is based on a commit that predates another merged PR, the merged PR files appear in the diff. Review says out-of-scope changes. Fix is rebase onto main, not code changes. Two fix cycles consumed before root cause identified. Consider adding a check in PR workflow to detect stale bases.\n  [success] @01KJPK6MM (2 days ago by @claude)\n    PR scope verification: use gh pr view N --json baseRefOid,headRefOid then git diff <baseRefOid>..<headRefOid> --name-only to verify actual PR diff. This is more reliable than git diff origin/main..branch when the branch base predates recent merges to main.\n  [friction] @01KJPH4Z (2 days ago by @claude)\n    Trait AC annotations (@trait-json-output, @trait-shadow-commit) consistently missed in new test describe blocks. Two consecutive tasks (agent-definition-schema, session-model-evolution) both flagged in fix cycle for missing annotations. Pattern: workers add spec-specific ACs but forget inherited trait ACs. Fix: consider checklist or template reminder when adding new integration test blocks.\n  [friction] @01KJN7J5D (2 days ago by @claude)\n    Item status in JSON output is a nested object {maturity: string, implementation: string} not a plain string. Tests checking item.status === \"implemented\" fail silently — must use item.status?.implementation. This differs from task status which IS a plain string.\n  [friction] @01KJN7J5A (2 days ago by @claude)\n    item list --json returns {items:[...]} (wrapped) while tasks list --json returns a flat array. Inconsistent JSON shapes cause test type errors — kspecJson<any[]> typed as flat array silently fails when the actual shape is an object wrapper. Test authors need to know to use {items: Array<...>} for item list vs any[] for tasks list.\n  [success] @01KJN360K (2 days ago by @claude)\n    Test-first approach for regression bugs: wrote tests that failed (confirmed bug), then fixed source, then verified tests pass. Clean feedback loop — 2 failing tests before fix, 6 passing after. Recommend this pattern for all shadow commit gap tasks.\n  [friction] @01KJN360F (2 days ago by @claude)\n    Verifying pre-existing test failures requires stashing changes and re-running — slow feedback loop. A baseline test report or CI artifact would let agents identify pre-existing failures without manual stash/unstash.\n  [success] @01KJN09EX (2 days ago by @claude)\n    Progressive reproduction technique (incrementally adding middleware layers to a minimal Elysia server) isolated an Elysia framework interaction bug in minutes. More effective than reading framework source code.\n  [friction] @01KJN09ER (2 days ago by @claude)\n    E2E tests not in CI allowed a fundamental WebSocket upgrade bug to persist undetected. Previous task closed as fixed based on unit tests passing, but E2E would have shown the server never upgraded connections.\n  [success] @01KJKCF1 (3 days ago by @claude)\n    Agent investigation breadth vs accuracy tradeoff — Explore agents are valuable for surveying many files quickly but their conclusions need manual verification before acting. In this session, the agent reported event listener accumulation as CRITICAL (wrong — listeners registered once per spawn) while the actual bottleneck (getEventCount O(n^2)) wasn't in its top findings. Pattern: use agents for breadth, verify claims against actual code, then form your own root cause hypothesis.\n  [success] @01KJK7F4T (3 days ago by @claude)\n    Minimal floating-promise fix pattern: storing async function result in a variable and attaching .catch(() => {}) before Promise.race is the clean way to prevent Node.js 22 unhandled rejection crashes when a race-loser promise is later rejected by resource cleanup.\n  [friction] @01KJK7F4J (3 days ago by @claude)\n    Task budget guard blocks needs_work fix cycle transitions. kspec task start @ref fails with budget exhausted even when the task is in needs_work state (an existing fix cycle, not a new task start). needs_work → in_progress should be exempt from the budget guard since it is resuming prior work, not starting new work. Had to skip task state transitions and create PR without proper submit flow.\n  [success] @01KJJT51P (3 days ago by @claude)\n    Codex-then-fix cycle on PR #601 was tight: review posted, ac-9 gap caught, fixed in 1 commit, thread resolved, CI passed, merged. Total turnaround ~15 min including CI.\n  [friction] @01KJJT51H (3 days ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review cannot distinguish pre-existing test failures from regressions. Flags false positives (e.g. session-budget-integration flakiness on PR #590). Could pre-seed known-flaky test list or compare failure counts against main baseline.\n  [friction] @01KJJT519 (3 days ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review takes 10+ minutes because npm test subagent polling is inefficient. Consider instructing Codex to skip full suite and rely on CI, or use test shards instead of full npm test.\n  [friction] @01KJJHARM (3 days ago by @claude)\n    Merge driver silent data loss when --non-interactive flag missing from git config. No error, no warning — just hangs on stdin and leaves files unmerged. 12 task completions lost silently. Root cause: shadow.ts registration code path diverged from actual git config. Fix tracked in @01KJJGJ1.\n  [success] @01KJJHARH (3 days ago by @claude)\n    Isolated worktree + backup branch pattern for shadow branch recovery. Creating a backup branch before destructive ops, then using /tmp worktree for experimental rebase, then patching the result back as a normal commit avoided force-push entirely. This pattern should be documented for future shadow branch divergence scenarios.\n  [success] @01KJGW8ZF (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Using a focused validate mode matrix (schema/refs/alignment/completeness/staleness + combinations) quickly verified scoped semantics and prevented regressions while keeping turnaround fast.\n  [friction] @01KJGW8ZB (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    CLI tests that execute dist artifacts can produce misleading failures when dist is stale; this creates avoidable debugging churn before the real code behavior is evaluated.\n  [success] @01KJGQKP (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Targeted dry-run + focused test loop quickly exposed core-skill source-of-truth drift; package-distribution test caught stale plugin artifact path immediately.\n  [success] @01KJGQCA7 (4 days ago by @claude)\n    Iterative codex review loop (review → fix → re-review) converges well. Three rounds on PR #582 caught duplicate label, spec alignment drift, and missing test branch.\n  [friction] @01KJGQCA1 (4 days ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review threads require manual GraphQL resolution + CI re-trigger before merge. Multi-step dance every time codex posts inline comments.\n  [success] @01KJGFKJ (4 days ago by @claude)\n    Holistic plan review (plan + derived specs + tasks + traits together) before any code is written caught 6 spec gaps in @plan-auto-close-stale-active-sessions including missing targeting semantics, liveness guard, corrupt events handling, and task dependency ordering. Pattern: evaluate the full derivation tree, not just individual specs.\n  [friction] @01KJGAKM (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Core skill update UX gap: kspec skill update is version-gated, so same-version template edits are skipped and require kspec skill install-core. This is non-obvious during local template iteration. Improve guidance (update vs install-core) or adjust command behavior to detect local template changes.\n  [friction] @01KJGAE2A (4 days ago by @claude)\n    Plan-derived tasks need manual follow-up (batch set tags + note) to be automation-ready. The derive step should produce standalone tasks.\n  [success] @01KJGAE26 (4 days ago by @claude)\n    Parallel plan review (Plan agent + Codex) catches complementary issues: architectural concerns vs format/structural issues. High convergence on edge cases gives confidence. Worth standardizing as a dual-review step.\n  [success] @01KJEVMQ (5 days ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Portable {skill:<id>} tokens with renderer-side platform resolution reduced template duplication and kept Claude/Codex invocation guidance consistent across SKILL.md and supporting markdown.\n  [friction] @01KJEVM2 (5 days ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    When posting content containing $ and angle-bracket placeholders via shell-wrapped commands, interpolation can silently mutate task/PR notes; prefer literal-safe quoting or file-based payloads.\n  [success] @01KJDZV0A (5 days ago by @claude)\n    Plan-derived task descriptions with exact line numbers and numbered change steps make implementation fast and focused. The plan-to-task pipeline produces high-quality work items.\n  [success] @01KJDZV06 (5 days ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review as iterative second reviewer catches real bugs. Two-round pattern (review → fix → re-review) provides high-confidence quality gate for ralph adapter/env code.\n  [success] @01KJDXCBB (5 days ago by @claude)\n    MOCK_ACP_VERIFY_ARGS_FILE pattern extends the existing env var verification pattern cleanly — behavioral test infrastructure for ACP agent spawning is now solid.\n  [success] @01KJDXCB7 (5 days ago by @claude)\n    Well-decomposed task descriptions with exact files and verification criteria make implementation fast — the per-adapter-auto-approve task took one focused session from start to merged PR.\n  [success] @01KJDV6R (5 days ago by @claude)\n    Codex two-pass review pattern effective: implement → codex review → fix bug found → re-review clean. Caught missing previousValue in injectCodexEnv that would have broken Ralph cleanup.\n  [success] @01KJDR3S (5 days ago by @claude)\n    Triage record-then-act pattern with UI review works well at scale: batch-record 41 decisions, user reviews/overrides in web UI, batch-act on confirmed. Clean separation of agent proposals from user decisions. 12 overrides out of 41 shows useful agent-human collaboration.\n  [question] @01KJDQ14 (5 days ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] kspec meta set convention: add --update-rule and --remove-rule flags. Currently the only way to fix a rule is to delete the entire convention and recreate it. Surfaced during skill CLI lookups task when codex review caught a wording issue in one rule.\n\nReasoning: Convention --update-rule — DX improvement, workaround is delete+recreate\n  [question] @01KJDQ12 (5 days ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] @cmd-validate spec is stale — missing flags: --completeness, --staleness, --drift, --alignment, --skills, --conventions (all already implemented in code). Pre-existing drift, not introduced by trait AC task.\n\nReasoning: @cmd-validate spec is stale — missing 6 flags already in code. Spec drift.\n  [question] @01KJDQ119 (5 days ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] Triage API spec says ac-4 and ac-5 use PUT method but implementation uses POST /:ref/override and POST /:ref/act. Spec needs updating to match reality.\n\nReasoning: Triage API spec PUT vs POST mismatch — spec needs updating to match implementation\n  [question] @01KJDQ114 (5 days ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] Triage ac-5 says sets result_ref but delete action may not produce one. Verify behavior and update spec or add test assertion.\n\nReasoning: Triage ac-5 result_ref for delete — spec may be wrong, needs verification\n  [question] @01KJDQ0ZZ (5 days ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] Plan import: generate richer task descriptions from Implementation Notes section — include file paths, what to change, verification steps instead of generic 'See plan...' text\n\nReasoning: Plan import richer descriptions — improvement, not blocking\n  [question] @01KJDQ0ZT (5 days ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] Merge driver: auto-resolve .kspec-agents-hash file conflicts (deterministic hash, should always take latest)\n\nReasoning: Merge driver for hash file — low frequency conflict, manual resolution works\n  [success] @01KJDN04 (5 days ago by @claude)\n    Dual review pattern (plan agent + Codex) catches complementary issues. Plan agent validates design, Codex validates contracts and test coverage. Worth keeping as standard practice for non-trivial PRs.\n  [success] @01KJDFFJK (5 days ago by @claude)\n    Shadow branch merge driver handles complex YAML conflicts (project.tasks.yaml with 38 divergent commits) automatically during rebase — reliable for ralph parallel sessions.\n  [success] @01KJDFFJG (5 days ago by @claude)\n    Dual review pattern (internal plan review + /codex review-plan) catches complementary issues — format vs substance. Worth standardizing for all plan imports.\n  [friction] @01KJD933 (5 days ago by @claude)\n    PR merged by review subagent while worker agent still editing files on same branch — local branch switches to main automatically, all pending Edit tool changes silently lost. Caused significant rework this session (re-applying all triage.spec.ts changes on new branch fix/triage-ac6-session-close-error). The review subagent and worker agent operate concurrently on the same branch in a fix cycle, creating a race condition. Potential mitigations: (1) worker agent checks for branch changes before editing, (2) ralph serializes review subagent after worker fully stops, (3) worker agent uses a local patch/stash before any branch changes.\n  [friction] @01KJD6M8 (5 days ago by @claude)\n    When deleting test files that cover spec ACs, kspec validate does not warn that AC annotations are being removed. This means coverage regressions are only caught during Codex review after the PR is up — requiring an extra fix commit. A pre-delete validate or a local-review gate that checks 'these AC annotations would disappear' would catch this earlier. Observed during task-delete-static-analysis-tests (PR #558): deleted web-ui-triage.test.ts removed all @interactive-triage-ui ac-1..ac-8 coverage; Codex caught it, required creating triage.spec.ts to recover.\n  [success] @01KJD309R (5 days ago by @claude)\n    window.__testWs pattern for Playwright WebSocket E2E: Store native browser WebSocket in window.__testWs via page.evaluate() and access it across subsequent evaluate() calls. Browser context maintains the global between calls. Enables clean helper functions (connectWebSocket, sendAndWaitForAck, subscribeAndWaitForBroadcast) without needing Playwright WebSocket interception. Used successfully in packages/web-ui/tests/e2e/api-websocket.spec.ts.\n  [friction] @01KJD309N (5 days ago by @claude)\n    CI check-unresolved-comments timing race in review fix cycle: When Codex posts inline review comments on a PR, we push a fix commit which triggers CI immediately. The check-unresolved-comments job runs BEFORE we resolve the Codex threads via GraphQL, causing it to fail on the new commit. Then requires manual workflow re-run. Mitigation: always resolve all review threads BEFORE pushing any fix commits during a review cycle.\n  [friction] @01KJD309G (5 days ago by @claude)\n    JS date validation gotcha in E2E tests: new Date(\"invalid\") returns Invalid Date but does NOT throw. Tests using expect(() => new Date(x)).not.toThrow() or try/catch will always pass even with malformed timestamps - false positive. Always use isNaN(new Date(x).getTime()) for timestamp format validation. Caught this in api-websocket.spec.ts timestamp assertion (AC-29/@trait-websocket-protocol ac-3).\n  [success] @01KJCXMR (5 days ago by @claude)\n    Codex retrospective PR review workflow: /codex pr on merged PRs surfaces real issues. Found stale dist/ bug via retro review of PR #547 that live review missed.\n  [friction] @01KJCVXZ (5 days ago by @claude)\n    AC annotation verification gap: Tests annotated with wrong AC refs (e.g., Elysia 422 tests annotated as ac-23 when ac-23 requires 400+{error,details}; POST body validation annotated as ac-5/6/7 when those are for X-Kspec-Dir header). Fix: always run kspec item get @spec-ref and read AC text before annotating tests.\n  [friction] @01KJC3JS (6 days ago by @claude)\n    Trait N/A annotations placed inside JSDoc block comments are not machine-parseable — the '* // AC:' pattern (where * is the JSDoc line prefix) does not match the scanner's '// AC:' regex. Must use standalone // AC: line comments OUTSIDE any /** ... */ block. Occurred in api-meta.spec.ts E2E tests, caught by Codex review, required fix commit.\n  [friction] @01KJBW3RX (6 days ago by @claude)\n    scanTestCoverage() only scanned rootDir/tests/ — missed packages/web-ui/tests/e2e/ where Playwright E2E specs with AC annotations live. Fixed by extending scan to also cover the E2E directory and .spec.ts/.spec.js file extensions. Risk: any future E2E test directory additions would silently miss coverage until the scan list is updated. Consider making E2E scan paths configurable or convention-documented.\n  [success] @01KJBW3RS (6 days ago by @claude)\n    GitHub PR thread resolution requires GraphQL mutation (resolveReviewThread), not REST API. REST API only supports replies. Pattern: get thread node IDs via graphql query on pullRequest.reviewThreads, then loop resolveReviewThread mutations. Reliably resolved all 7 threads in one pass.\n  [friction] @01KJBTMZ (6 days ago by @claude)\n    Elysia body schema validation returns 422 (not 400) before the handler runs. When writing E2E tests for daemon endpoints that have body schema validation (t.Object(...)), missing/invalid body fields return 422. If the handler also has manual validation that returns 400, the schema check fires first. Account for this in test assertions — do not assume 400 for missing body fields on Elysia-validated routes.\n  [success] @01KJBJX0 (6 days ago by @claude)\n    Self-validating task pattern: created a P1 automation-eligible task whose acceptance criteria IS verifying the fix works. Ralph picks it up, the task checks its own session_id stamp, and the result documents whether the fix works end-to-end. Useful for testing harness-level integration that can't be verified from inside an active session.\n  [friction] @01KJB02B (6 days ago by @claude)\n    testUlid('PREFIX') returns identical values across calls without explicit sequence argument — deterministic with default sequence=0. Caused false-positive test pass in session-checkpoint-scoping tests where mySession and otherSession were identical. Fix options: auto-incrementing counter, require sequence param, or rename to testUlidDeterministic to make behavior obvious. testUlids() works correctly but is easy to overlook.\n  [success] @01KJAAGK (6 days ago by @claude)\n    Session test helper extraction (task @01KJ9ZDE) went smoothly. The all-fields-optional SessionContext pattern works well for tests that each need a different subset of fields — TypeScript structural typing handles it naturally. Reduced ~315 lines of duplication across 6 files into 2 shared modules. The inbox helpers needed a tempDir parameter change (closure → explicit arg) which was the main mechanical transformation.\n  [friction] @01KJA9BT (6 days ago by @claude)\n    Codex skill silently exits with no output when OPENAI_API_KEY is not set. The skill documents gh auth preflight but not API key validation. Agents waste time discovering this. Skill should check for API key and fail fast with clear message.\n  [success] @01KJA6SX (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Parallel audit agents pattern: spawning 4 specialized agents (code quality, test quality, non-test code, process quality) in parallel gives comprehensive coverage in ~2 min wall clock. Each agent needs clear scope boundaries and structured output format.\n  [friction] @01KJ8XQF (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Cross-cutting skill updates need checklist: When a concept spans multiple skills (e.g., spec context discovery in codex, pr-review, local-review, review template), updating one skill without checking all related ones leads to inconsistency. Codex review on PR #511 found 4 findings where the dig-deeper pattern wasn't applied uniformly. Consider: a way to tag related skills, or a pre-commit check that flags partial updates to skill groups.\n  [friction] @01KJ8X52 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Session checkpoint hook fires repeatedly on research-only sessions with pre-existing uncommitted changes from a different task. No way to signal this is a read-only investigation session.\n  [friction] @01KJ8R05 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Plan-derived tasks with depends_on sometimes become no-ops because the dependency task already implements all shared spec ACs. Seen in iteration 3: @update-setup-with-migration-cleanup (all 6 ACs done by @implement-native-worktree-guard) and @add-multi-harness-env-injection (all injection ACs done by @implement-session-create-command). Wastes budget starts and discovery time. Potential fix: plan import could warn when multiple tasks share a spec and one depends on the other, or task start could auto-detect 'AC already covered' and offer to close.\n  [success] @01KJ8QR2 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Pure function pattern for hook/guard commands: exporting the core logic as a testable pure function (e.g. evaluateWorktreeGuard) with stdin/process handling in the CLI layer enables fast unit testing (44 tests, no I/O) while keeping the CLI integration tests minimal (7 tests for protocol compliance). Replicate this pattern for future hook commands.\n  [success] @01KJ8NDT (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review caught exec()+unref() not actually preventing event loop hang (stdio pipes still buffered). Validated that /codex review-pr adds real value beyond rubber-stamping.\n  [friction] @01KJ8J44G (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Ralph CLI uses --max-loops but internal variables and log messages say iterations. Minor naming inconsistency that causes confusion when writing tests or debugging. Example: --max-loops 1 but logs Starting ralph loop (max 1 iterations).\n  [success] @01KJ8J44C (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Mock ACP agent pattern (tests/fixtures/mock-acp-agent.mjs) enables fast behavioral testing of ralph subprocess orchestration. Tests real env injection, budget cleanup, and signal handling in ~2s each. Key enabler: --adapter-cmd flag + spawnRalphUntil helper that watches output markers then kills the process.\n  [friction] @01KJ8EE7 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    setupTempFixtures() returns a flat dir where tempDir IS the specDir — no .kspec/ wrapper. Agents that assume nested structure (path.join(tempDir, \".kspec\")) get silent test failures. AGENTS.md test helpers table should note this.\n  [success] @01KJ82GP (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Parallel agent spawning for session log audit: 5 agents analyzing different log files simultaneously gave comprehensive coverage. Pattern worth replicating for any multi-file analysis task.\n  [success] @01KJ77DA (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Budget enforcement in task start integrates cleanly: check before transition, increment after save, resume case handled by existing early return. The library functions (checkBudget/incrementBudget) do the heavy lifting — CLI handler just wires them together. Good separation of concerns between store.ts and task.ts.\n  [success] @01KJ75RQ (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Dual plan review (internal agent + /codex review-plan) catches complementary issues: internal finds design gaps and terminology problems, codex finds code-level feasibility issues (missing APIs, wrong method names, breaking consumers). Convergent findings indicate high confidence. Used successfully for session-start-redesign plan.\n  [friction] @01KJ75K2 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    task set --depends-on replaces all deps rather than appending. When setting multiple deps in separate batch commands, only the last one survives. Must provide all deps in a single call. Consider adding --add-depends-on for append semantics (similar to --relates-to on item set which appends).\n  [friction] @01KJ73ME (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Ralph task-limit-guard hook blocks gh pr create even when finishing current task work. The guard treats all Bash commands equally — it can't distinguish 'creating a PR for completed work' from 'starting new task work'. Result: branch pushed but PR not created, requiring next iteration to finish. Consider whitelisting gh pr create or detecting PR creation context in the guard.\n  [success] @01KJ731A (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Codex review caught fail-open bug in getBudget() — corrupt budget.json was silently treated as 'no budget', effectively disabling enforcement. Changed to fail closed (only ENOENT returns null, parse/validation errors propagate). Pattern: for security/enforcement code, always default to the restrictive path. Codex consistently catches error-handling path issues that internal review misses.\n  [friction] @01KJ71CS (1 week ago by @claude)\n    @trait-semantic-exit-codes ACs hardcode exit code numbers (ac-2: 'exit code 1', ac-3: 'exit code 2', ac-4: 'exit code 3') that don't match kspec's actual EXIT_CODES scheme (ERROR=1, USAGE_ERROR=2, NOT_FOUND=3, VALIDATION_FAILED=4). Every command adding trait AC test coverage needs clarifying comments explaining the mapping. Fix: update trait ACs to use semantic descriptions ('non-zero exit code indicating validation failure') instead of hardcoded numbers, or update to match kspec's actual codes.\n  [success] @01KJ63RWA (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Dual review pattern (internal agent + Codex) for plan review catches different issue categories. Internal agent found consistency gaps; Codex found concurrency risks. Convergent findings give high confidence before implementation.\n  [success] @01KJ63RW7 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Parallel subagent audit pattern: launching 4+ agents in parallel for deep-dive review of ralph loop work (PR quality, functional verification, loop behavior, process adherence) produced comprehensive results in ~90 seconds. Effective for post-loop quality assessment.\n  [success] @01KJ5B3S (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review caught a real type drift bug (Convention.validation schema mismatch) during web-ui type deduplication PR. The shared-type approach works well: define in @kynetic-ai/shared, re-export from web-ui, enforce with static analysis test. Dual review (Codex + CI) catches complementary issues.\n  [friction] @01KJ51NB (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Post-merge branch state: When inheriting a session where a PR was already merged, the working directory may be on main. Always check branch (git branch --show-current) before committing, and create a feature branch first. Recovering from an accidental main commit requires creating a branch from HEAD and resetting main.\n  [friction] @01KJ50B8 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    settings.json written by 3 independent subsystems during setup (installClaudeCodeHooks, seedPermissions, enablePluginInProject via modifyJsonAtomic). Makes skip-when-unchanged optimization impossible without either a shared coordinator or consolidating all mutations into one function. Each writer reads, modifies, writes independently — interleaving prevents simple content comparison.\n  [success] @01KJ4VDK (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Pre-analyzed triage report (HTML artifact) combined with kspec batch made processing 107 inbox items efficient. Record-then-act pattern provided good audit trail. Pattern: analyze offline, port decisions via batch.\n  [question] @01KJ4SNF (1 week ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] Design section configuration for agents generate — allow projects to control which sections appear (skills table, conventions, workflows, static templates). Key questions: where does config live, include vs exclude list, defaults, custom sections, ordering, data vs template distinction, migration path. See plan doc for full requirements.\n\nReasoning: Must-have but needs spec design - section configuration for agents generate\n  [question] @01KJ4SNE (1 week ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] kspec validate @spec or @task — targeted validation that checks a single spec/task's AC coverage against codebase. Not full approval, just verifies each AC has at least one // AC: annotation in test files. Would make trait AC gaps immediately visible during task-work. Could be used by local-review skill as a concrete check instead of relying on agent diligence.\n\nReasoning: Must-have but needs spec design - targeted kspec validate for single item\n  [question] @01KJ4SJP5 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] Cross-feature requirement pattern: How to express that core features (search, validate, export) must support all entity types. When new entity type added, should automatically imply tasks for core features. Options: (1) applies_to field on feature specs, (2) trait on entity types like @trait-searchable, (3) validation warning for missing coverage, (4) parent spec that entity types must satisfy. Surfaced when realizing search doesn't cover inbox.\n\nReasoning: first item reasoning\n  [question] @01KJ4SJP2 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    [spec-gap] Include triage records in static snapshot (kspec-snapshot.json). Currently the snapshot format (KspecSnapshot type) doesn't include triage records, so the triage page in static/GitHub Pages mode shows an empty state with a notice instead of displaying existing triage decisions read-only. AC-8 of @interactive-triage-ui expects read-only display. Needs: add triage records to export snapshot, update KspecSnapshot type, add fetchTriageRecordsStatic to read from snapshot.\n\nReasoning: There's a spec gap here\n  [friction] @01KJ3X7D (1 week ago by @claude)\n    CI timing race: when Codex posts a review comment after CI check-unresolved-comments has already run, the check fails because the new thread is unresolved. Requires manual re-run after resolving. Consider: (1) make check-unresolved-comments a required-last check, (2) have the review workflow auto-re-run the check after resolving threads, or (3) add a gh run rerun step to the codex review skill.\n  [idea] @01KJ3T9X (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Spec gap test\n\nReasoning: needs spec\n  [success] @01KJ3M13 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Zod superRefine pattern for status-dependent field requirements (e.g., triaged requires reasoning/decided_by, acted_on requires acted_at). Should be the default approach for any schema with status lifecycle — flat .optional() misses state invariants. First used in triage.ts, should apply to plan.ts and similar.\n  [success] @01KJ331N (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Parallel subagent research pattern: launching 3+ research agents simultaneously to investigate different aspects (code state, git history, task completions) builds comprehensive evidence in ~3 minutes vs 15+ sequentially. Critical for large triage sessions (95 items). Each agent gets a focused investigation scope and returns structured findings.\n  [friction] @01KJ1RW3 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Meta vs manifest confusion: skill render reads from meta (shadow branch), not template manifest. Changing manifest ID without updating meta has no effect. Need to delete+re-add skill via CLI.\n  [friction] @01KJ1A32 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Platform behavior assumption: PR #440 added namespace rendering (.claude/skills/kspec/<id>/) without verifying Claude Code actually discovers nested skill directories. It does not. Future platform integration work should verify discovery behavior (e.g. create a test skill, check if Claude Code lists it) before building rendering pipelines around assumed directory structures.\n  [friction] @01KHZHZA (1 week ago by @claude)\n    task get not showing descriptions meant had to grep raw YAML to verify task content after batch creation. Critical gap for agents working on tasks — they cannot read the description via CLI.\n  [success] @01KHZBEE (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Spec-before-implement pattern: adding ac-6 to @agent-instruction-gen before coding the noop behavior made test coverage natural and caught the right contract upfront.\n  [success] @01KHZ6H8 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Convention vs template mental model clarified: Templates carry kspec-generic content (shadow branch, task lifecycle, ralph loop — same for ALL kspec projects). Conventions carry project-specific rules (testing gotchas, dev setup, CI limitations — unique per project). When porting content from AGENTS.md, ask: does this apply to every kspec project? Template. Only this project? Convention.\n  [success] @01KHYTNN (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Dual review convergence: running both pr-review subagent and /codex review in parallel catches different issues and convergent findings give high confidence. Both flagged dead verifyTaskCompleted code independently.\n  [success] @01KHWVQP6 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Parallel review audit pattern: spawning 4 specialized subagents (worker process adherence, review process adherence, code quality, spec/AC coverage) to evaluate a ralph session. Each agent had a focused lens and found complementary issues. Total wall-clock time ~2 min for what would have been 15+ min of sequential analysis.\n  [success] @01KHWVQP3 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Dual-reviewer pattern (Claude agent + Codex) for PR review. Running both in parallel on PR #424 gave convergent findings on the critical branch-lookup bug (independent discovery by both). Codex also caught input validation gap that Claude missed. Different models find different issues — convergent findings give high confidence, divergent findings expand coverage.\n  [friction] @01KHWVQN (1 week ago by @claude)\n    GitHub blocks REQUEST_CHANGES on own PR. Ralph review subagents operate under the same GitHub credentials as the PR author, so they cannot post REQUEST_CHANGES reviews — only COMMENT. The pr-review skill instructs posting APPROVE or REQUEST_CHANGES, but REQUEST_CHANGES will always fail in self-authored PRs. Need either: (1) a separate GitHub bot account for review agents, or (2) the skill should use COMMENT event with structured review content instead of REQUEST_CHANGES.\n  [success] @01KHW3CCB (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Post-import verification agent catches data integrity issues that dry-run misses (null spec_ref, missing implementation notes). Should be part of standard plan import workflow.\n  [success] @01KHW3CC8 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Dual review pattern (internal Plan agent + Codex review-plan) catches complementary issues. Internal agent finds architectural concerns, Codex finds format/structure issues. Convergent findings give high confidence. Worth standardizing for all plan imports.\n  [success] @01KHVYHKH (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Parallel consumer project exploration: launching 3 agents to explore kynetic-bot, imgen, comfy-ecaj-nodes simultaneously produced convergent findings (same Quick Start pattern, same hooks, same skill drift) that were far more convincing than exploring one project. Use parallel exploration when researching cross-project patterns.\n  [success] @01KHVYHKE (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Dual review pattern before plan import: running both an internal plan review agent and Codex in parallel caught YAML quoting failures and substantive spec gaps (batch mode, worktree detection, remote type algorithm). Reviews converged on same must-fixes, giving high confidence. Always dual-review complex plan documents before importing.\n  [friction] @01KHVYHKA (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Invoked /spec-plan skill but wrote implementation guide instead of plan-import-format document. User had explicitly said /spec-plan. Lesson: invoking a skill is not enough — must follow its output format from the FIRST write. When /spec-plan is invoked for plan mode, write the plan file in kspec plan import format (# Title, ## Specs with YAML code block, ## Tasks, ## Implementation Notes) from the start.\n  [success] @01KHVE718 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Parallel agent reviews (Codex + /pr-review) found complementary issues: Codex caught test annotation gaps, review agent caught shared fixture regression. Multi-reviewer pattern is high-value for quality.\n  [friction] @01KHVE714 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Shared test fixture fragility: tests/fixtures/kynetic.meta.yaml is used by multiple test files. Tests that append YAML keys (like meta.test.ts appending conventions:) collide when the fixture gains new top-level keys. Caused real regression in PR #413. Consider per-test fixture isolation or fixture composition helpers.\n  [success] @01KHT79FA (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Running codex review in parallel with PR review agent caught a real security bypass (split-quote git \"reset\" --hard). The dual-reviewer pattern (code-level + quality-gate) provides meaningful coverage overlap.\n  [friction] @01KHT79F7 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Guard script bash code embedded in JS template literal makes quote escaping painful — single quotes inside tr/sed cause parse errors. Consider extracting guard scripts to standalone .sh files loaded at setup time instead of inline template literals.\n  [friction] @01KHSKZD (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Refactoring tasks without spec_ref have AC traceability gap — task-level ACs in notes aren't discoverable by standard // AC: @spec-ref ac-N annotation pattern. Codex review flagged this on PR #411. Options: (1) allow // AC: @task-ref ac-N, (2) require spec items even for refactoring, (3) teach reviewers to check task notes. Related: @task-skill-module-split had no spec_ref but defined 3 ACs in notes.\n  [success] @01KHSG0C (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review (gpt-5.3-codex) caught a real correctness bug in drift detection (hash-gating for sidecar changes) that the implementing agent missed. Codex reproduced the issue locally with a concrete script. High value for code review.\n  [success] @01KHRYZ5 (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Parallel dual-reviewer strategy (Claude agent + Codex) is effective: Claude validates correctness, Codex catches spec-alignment gaps. Use both for PRs with spec ACs.\n  [success] @01KHRP78P (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Multi-model PR review (Claude + Codex in parallel) caught issues that single-model review missed. Codex found missing debugLog in outer catch and weak test assertions. Worth continuing this pattern for non-trivial PRs.\n  [friction] @01KHRP78K (1 week ago by @claude)\n    Running kspec setup during development creates kspec-agents.md and .claude/skills/ in project root. These are test artifacts for the kspec project itself (which uses AGENTS.md, not kspec-agents.md). They trigger repeated stop hook warnings about uncommitted changes. Need either: (a) test isolation so setup tests dont leak into project root, or (b) cleanup step in bootstrap/dev workflow.\n  [friction] @01KHQXGWQ (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review skill always demands // AC: @spec-ref ac-N annotations, even for infrastructure/cleanup tasks where ACs describe absence of code. Should skip AC annotation checks when task has no spec_ref or when spec type is not user-facing behavior.\n  [friction] @01KHQXGWG (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Background PR review agent can act on stale context: agent added 172-line test file for a spec that was deleted during review. When running review agents in background while making spec/task changes in foreground, the agent does not see those changes. Required a follow-up cleanup PR.\n  [friction] @01KHKTQNN (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Ralph loop PR-by-PR review process misses cross-PR duplication accumulation. PR #381 added rendering utils, #383 duplicated them in a new file, #394 consolidated render functions but missed the utilities, #401 added more dead code. Each PR passes review individually but the aggregate drift is significant (24 inbox items from one loop). Consider adding a consolidation/cleanup iteration every N PRs or at loop end.\n  [success] @01KHKTQNH (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Parallel subagent review of ralph loop: 5 agents reviewing different PR ranges (371-376, 377-384, 385-393, 394-402, test suite) completed in ~3min wall clock. Independent agents finding the same duplication issues confirms real problems vs noise. Effective pattern for post-loop quality review.\n  [friction] @01KHHR77E (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    kspec batch uses kebab-case args (depends-on) but schema fields use snake_case (depends_on). Inconsistency causes trial-and-error on first batch use.\n  [success] @01KHHR77C (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Running both internal Plan agent review AND Codex review-plan on the same plan catches complementary issues. Internal review finds code-level gaps; Codex finds spec quality issues.\n  [success] @01KHHR77A (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Structured interview (one question at a time with pro/con analysis) before planning leads to well-reasoned architecture decisions. User engagement is higher when they see trade-offs laid out clearly.\n  [friction] @01KHFPVS (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    CLI commands targeting main project files (like .claude/skills/) must use ctx.rootDir, not ctx.specDir or path.dirname(ctx.specDir). The specDir is the spec content location (.kspec/ in shadow mode), while rootDir is always the project root. Using dirname(specDir) fails in shadow mode because .kspec/ is a worktree at the project root level. Encountered in skill render command where files were written to /tmp/ instead of project root.\n  [success] @01KHFCMDE (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Iterative clarifying questions with ask tool: asking one question at a time with 3-4 concrete options (each with descriptions) before synthesizing research into a plan led to well-informed decisions. User could respond quickly because each question had enough context from the research. Better than presenting a full plan and asking for feedback.\n  [success] @01KHFCMDB (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Dual-reviewer pattern for plans: having both an internal holistic reviewer (Claude) and an external reviewer (Codex) independently review the same plan catches complementary issues. Internal finds code-level integration problems, external finds architectural concerns. Both agreed on the same critical issues (compound ACs, inline YAML). Three revision cycles (v1 reviewed v2 reviewed v3) produced significantly stronger output.\n  [success] @01KHFCMD8 (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Parallel subagent research pattern: launching 4+ research agents simultaneously on different aspects of the same question (cross-project audit, usage patterns, external ecosystem, install story) produced comprehensive non-overlapping findings in ~6 minutes. Much more effective than sequential investigation.\n  [friction] @01KHCYW7 (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Commander.js parent-action option routing: when parent and subcommands define the same options, Commander doesn't cleanly route them. Tried passThroughOptions (requires global enablePositionalOptions, breaks --json global option), ended up using allowUnknownOption + temporary Command re-parse. Pattern works but is non-idiomatic. If more command groups need default actions, consider extracting this into a shared utility.\n  [success] @01KHAAN8 (2 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Re-baseline research findings against current code before creating specs. In CLI agent-UX session, 7 of 21 recommendations from telemetry analysis were already implemented. Verifying each against the codebase with parallel explore agents before speccing saved significant overhead (would have created 7 unnecessary specs and 7 unnecessary tasks).\n  [friction] @01KGY73W8 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    GitHub PR review thread resolution requires GraphQL mutation (resolveReviewThread). REST reply endpoint posts replies but does not resolve threads. Had to discover thread node IDs via GraphQL query first.\n  [success] @01KGY73W6 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex as second reviewer on doc PRs caught a real inconsistency (scenario expected answer vs AGENTS.md rule) and a non-existent file path. Worth running on documentation PRs, not just code PRs.\n  [success] @01KGY73W3 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Stress testing with real codebase exploration is more effective than synthetic padding for finding doc gaps. Agent reads actual code (building 30k+ tokens of context), then answers recall questions. Found 2 real degradation points (PR workflow steps, scope expansion) that were reinforced.\n  [success] @01KGY73W0 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Test-first doc editing: building eval scenarios before trimming AGENTS.md prevented info loss. 15 scenarios + 8 stress test recall questions validated the trimmed doc (14/15 PASS, 6/8 PASS). Pattern: build test suite → make changes → validate → fix gaps.\n  [friction] @01KGXN2ZA (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    AGENTS.md task state transitions documented task complete as working from in_progress/pending, but CLI actually requires pending_review (or --skip-review). Pre-existing inaccuracy that propagated to README. Codex caught it via runtime spot-checks that I did not do.\n  [success] @01KGXN2Z5 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Running 4 parallel Explore/Bash subagents to research README structure, AGENTS.md structure, full CLI catalog, and recent git features simultaneously saved significant time and produced comprehensive input for the docs update.\n  [friction] @01KGXN2Y (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Documentation tasks that update README/AGENTS.md should also check skill files (.claude/skills/) for staleness on the same topics. Easy to miss because skills are a separate concern. A validate --skills --docs check could catch this.\n  [success] @01KGXHNR (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Using Codex (/codex pr) as an automated second reviewer caught a real bug (Commander parseInt pre-parse bypass) that manual review missed. The review-fix-re-review cycle completed in one session. Worth using for every non-trivial PR.\n  [friction] @01KGXHND (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Commander's .option() parse function (e.g. parseInt) silently pre-coerces values before the action handler runs. When replacing inline validation with shared helpers, the helper never sees the raw string. This caused a bug in PR #347 where derive/plan-derive still accepted '3abc' as priority 3. Fix: never use Commander parse functions for options validated by shared helpers.\n  [friction] @01KGWZ26W (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex review depth insufficient on first pass: 4 review rounds were needed to fully validate plan-import slug safety. Each fix introduced or revealed the next gap. Root cause: Codex reviews surface-level patterns first (wrong API check) before deeper ones (stale index across mutations). Need a way to prime Codex to reason about mutation-staleness and index invalidation upfront, possibly via a review checklist or system prompt addition to the codex skill.\n  [success] @01KGWZ26T (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex iterative review as convergence loop: codex review → fix → re-review converges on correctness. 4 rounds on PR #346 found progressively deeper cross-namespace slug collision gaps (duplicate_slug handling → plans missing from refIndex → stale index for import-created specs → spec creation not checking importReservedSlugs). Each round caught a real bug.\n  [success] @01KGWZ26Q (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Stale index mutation pattern: when mutating items in a loop with a pre-built refIndex, track created slugs in a parallel Set since refIndex won't see loop-created items. Pattern: importReservedSlugs Set alongside refIndex.isSlugAvailable(). Applied in plan-import to prevent cross-namespace slug collisions within a single import run.\n  [friction] @01KGWG8Y (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Session event data payloads are untyped — EventTypeSchema defines the type enum but data field is z.unknown(). Had to grep through real JSONL files to discover that Ralph records task completions as session.update events with sessionUpdate: 'tool_call' and rawInput.command. This caused a bug that was only caught by Codex reviewing against real data.\n  [success] @01KGWG8W (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review caught silent bug in session log list — tasks_completed metric searched for non-existent event type. Real task completions use session.update tool_call events with rawInput.command containing 'task complete'. Without review, feature would ship reporting 0 tasks for all real sessions.\n  [friction] @01KGVK9P (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Automation triage forces context switch to spec creation: when assessing eligibility, discovering a task needs a spec means switching from assessment mode to authoring mode (find module, find parent, create item, add ACs, link task). Consider a guided flow or --create-spec flag on task set --automation.\n  [success] @01KGVK9M (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Implementation notes at promotion time: adding concrete implementation notes during inbox promote (not after) makes tasks self-contained for future agents. Include: which files to modify, patterns to follow, approximate scope.\n  [success] @01KGVK9K (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Batch triage workflow: categorize inbox by theme first, quick-win deletions, then one-at-a-time promotion with AskUserQuestion showing What/Why/How context. Cross-reference against git log and completed tasks to catch already-done items. This pattern processed 61 items efficiently.\n  [success] @01KGVDDP (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Task @01KGVARW had excellent root cause analysis in its notes (specific file, line numbers, all call sites, exact reproduction). This made the fix trivial — under 10 minutes from start to merged PR. Detailed bug reports with root cause are high-leverage.\n  [success] @01KGVCEN (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Tracing ACP bugs through the chain (framing.ts → client.ts → spawner.ts → ralph.ts) was efficient because the codebase has clear layering and the override mechanism (methodTimeouts) was already in place. Minimal change (18 lines, 3 files) for the fix.\n  [friction] @01KGVCEF (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review on self-authored PRs creates review threads that trigger check-unresolved-comments CI gate. Requires manual GraphQL thread resolution + CI re-run before merging. Codex skill should either skip inline comments on own PRs or auto-resolve threads after posting.\n  [success] @01KGTNYA (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Parallel subagent research (two Explore agents) during spec-plan design phase efficiently gathered session infrastructure context in ~60s instead of sequential exploration.\n  [success] @01KGTNY9 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex plan review as pre-import quality gate caught trait ref format bug and compound AC issues. Worth making standard practice for plan imports with 3+ specs.\n  [success] @01KGTM42 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex PR review as documentation quality gate: /codex pr caught real arg-name bugs in batch JSON examples that would have caused user failures. The review→fix→push cycle was fast. Worth using on all docs-heavy PRs.\n  [success] @01KGTH9G (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    GitHub PR review API with --input JSON file works reliably for inline comments. Pattern: write structured JSON to /tmp/codex-pr-review-body.json with {event, body, comments[{path, line, body}]}, then post via gh api repos/{o}/{r}/pulls/{n}/reviews --method POST --input file. Codex figured out the line vs position issue on its own (first attempt used line numbers which mapped to null, then corrected to diff positions).\n  [friction] @01KGTH99 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex exec review subcommand has different flag parsing than codex exec: -s must go before the review subcommand (codex exec -s danger-full-access review ...) not after (codex exec review -s ... fails). This flag ordering quirk isn't documented in codex --help and caused a runtime failure that required a fix commit.\n  [success] @01KGTH92 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Codex self-review loop is effective: had Codex review its own skill file, it found 4 MUST-FIX issues (invalid CLI flags, sandbox conflicts, malformed API payloads). Then had Codex review the PR with inline comments — it found 2 more issues (flag ordering, nested fences). Each round of review caught real bugs that would have caused runtime failures.\n  [success] @01KGTF5E (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Plan-driven implementation with spec-first ACs: having 22 acceptance criteria defined before coding made both implementation and testing systematic. Each test was annotated with its AC reference, and the review could verify coverage by checking annotations. The plan→spec→implement→test→review pipeline worked well for this medium-complexity feature.\n  [friction] @01KGTF57 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Atomic copy-back was a design gap caught by reviewers, not by tests. The plan described 'copy-back' as straightforward fs.cp, but both reviewers independently flagged that delete-then-copy is not safe under I/O failure. Future plans involving filesystem atomicity should explicitly call out failure modes in the design — the happy path isn't enough.\n  [success] @01KGS89M (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Subagent-reviewed AC drafts: Pattern of drafting ACs → subagent review → revise → apply caught 5+ real issues (--count+--json interaction, partial success gap, missing optional flag ACs, scope creep in AC-10, missing multi-trait remove symmetry). Prevented rework during implementation.\n  [success] @01KGS4Y7 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    WeakMap<Command, T> annotation pattern works cleanly for adding metadata to Commander commands. Avoids monkey-patching internals, is type-safe and GC-friendly, chains naturally with .command() calls (markMutating(cmd.command(...))), and integrates with extractCommandTree() by reading annotations during traversal. Reusable for future annotation types (e.g. requires-auth, idempotent). See src/cli/command-annotations.ts for reference implementation.\n  [success] @01KGRP6D (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Plan-import scoped notes fix: well-scoped plan with specific line numbers and code changes led to zero-iteration implementation (all tests passed first try). Good pattern for future fix plans.\n  [success] @01KGR5CV (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Precise plan with exact file paths, line numbers, and code diffs made the plan-import bug fix session trivially fast. Plan → implement → test → PR → merge in one smooth pass with zero rework.\n  [success] @01KGR0NM (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Subagent-driven holistic review for spec-plan design: Launching a general-purpose subagent with structured review criteria (completeness, consistency, spec coverage, edge cases) caught 2 blockers and 7 should-fix items before plan import. Pattern: write plan doc, then have subagent review against existing codebase patterns before committing.\n  [success] @01KGQYEC (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Parallel subagent mining pattern: spawning 3 specialized agents (notes, patterns, gaps) to analyze the same dataset from different angles. Each found complementary insights the others missed. Effective for pre-prune data extraction.\n  [friction] @01KGQY77 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    pr-review-merge consumes 36% of all workflow time (215 of 594 min). Step 1 (CI check) bimodal: 30 runs under 30s but 9 over 5 min. No fast-path for trivial PRs that pass all gates immediately. Consecutive chains of up to 14 suggest batch PR processing needs a dedicated workflow.\n  [idea] @01KGQY75 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Workflow run telemetry: Before pruning old runs, mine them for aggregate stats (abort rates, durations, sequencing patterns). Consider keeping aggregate statistics even when pruning individual records to enable trend analysis. First mining found: 9.9 hrs total, PR review 36%, reflect 46%, task-work-loop 6.5%.\n  [friction] @01KGQY72 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    session-reflect-loop runs too aggressively in ralph loops — 72 runs (40% of all runs), chains of up to 31 consecutive executions, vast majority capture zero new items. 113 min spent (19% of total workflow time) with questionable marginal value in consecutive chains. Should be conditional on whether preceding task-work-loop actually completed work.\n  [friction] @01KGQY70 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    task-work-loop has 69% abort rate (11/16 runs) — almost entirely because no eligible tasks existed when started. Workflow works fine when work exists (5/5 completed all 9 steps). Root cause: ralph orchestrator starts workflow without pre-checking kspec tasks ready --eligible.\n  [success] @01KGQY6Y (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Workflow run mining before prune: /reflect captured 99.4% of run content into durable artifacts. Out of 181 runs with ~100 meaningful notes, only 1 contained unique info not already tracked. Validates reflection-based knowledge capture approach.\n  [success] @01KGQVA9 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Subagent delegation pattern: using Explore agent for codebase survey + general-purpose agent for PR review kept main context clean and session efficient during daemon strict-mode fix\n  [friction] @01KGPHG8 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Committed directly to main forgetting branch protection requires PRs. Had to create branch after the fact and reset. Should always create feature branch first in this repo.\n  [success] @01KGPHG6 (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Review agent (opus subagent) caught a real validation bug - missing required 'id' field in plan document AC example. The schema check against actual parser code was the key insight. Always verify examples against actual schema definitions.\n  [success] @01KGP40Y (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Tracing output flow (console.log → stdout.write chain) quickly revealed the doubled prefix bug. When debugging output duplication, mapping the full write path through all wrappers/interceptors identifies where multiple layers add the same content.\n  [friction] @01KGP40S (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Vitest replaces console.log with its own implementation that doesn't flow through process.stdout.write. Tests that capture stdout.write won't see console.log output. When testing code that wraps both console.log and stdout.write, spy on console.log directly (vi.spyOn) and test stdout.write capture separately.\n  [success] @01KGP3JJ (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Test-first implementation with AC annotations: Writing tests with AC annotations (// AC: @spec-ref ac-N) before implementation ensures complete coverage and creates clear traceability. Pattern used in help.test.ts covered all 5 ACs systematically and caught the --json flag issue early.\n  [friction] @01KGP3JB (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Commander.js optional argument pattern: When a command has an optional argument like .command('help [topic]'), the action handler signature must use (topic: string | undefined, options) NOT (topic?: string, options?). The optional TypeScript syntax breaks Commander's argument passing - options becomes an empty object. Pattern seen in task.ts commands works correctly.\n  [success] @01KGP16K (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Plan with exact file paths, line numbers, and 'nature of change' descriptions translates directly to precise edits. The plan for ralph loop fixes had a table mapping files to changes — each row became one Edit tool call with no ambiguity.\n  [friction] @01KGP16H (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    kspec validate output is noisy with pre-existing reference errors and alignment warnings (22 ref errors, 152 alignment warnings). Makes it hard to verify whether a specific change introduced new issues. A --diff or --new-only flag that shows only issues not present in the previous commit would help.\n  [friction] @01KGP14G (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Acceptance criteria schema requires 'id' field but this isn't obvious when writing test plans or spec YAML. Parser validation fails silently - spec just doesn't appear in parsed output. Better error would be: 'AC missing required field: id' at parse time. Or make id optional and auto-generate if missing.\n  [success] @01KGP14D (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Using createdSpecsMap to track specs during import solved parent reference resolution elegantly. Specs created in topological order could reference earlier-created specs by slug lookup in the map, working in both dry-run and real modes. Pattern: track created entities in a map keyed by slug for multi-step operations with interdependencies.\n  [friction] @01KGP14A (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Test fixtures use @test-core module but many example commands in docs/tests assume @cli exists. Caused global find/replace in tests when module reference didn't match. Consider: (1) standardizing fixture module names to match common examples, (2) documenting fixture module structure in test helpers, or (3) adding @cli as an alias/additional module in fixtures.\n  [success] @01KGMX3K (3 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Test-driven regex debugging proved effective: wrote comprehensive tests for plan document parser's markdown section extraction (## Specs, ## Tasks, ## Implementation Notes), which immediately caught regex pattern bugs (^## anchors failed to match). Fixed patterns before integration, avoiding late-stage debugging. Pattern: write tests first for parsing/extraction logic, especially when dealing with complex patterns or unfamiliar APIs.\n  [success] @01KGM1RX (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Pattern-following implementation: When implementing new CLI commands, examining similar existing commands (e.g., derive.ts for task creation patterns, plan.ts for command structure) and reusing their patterns leads to consistent, correct implementations. This approach worked well for plan derive - imports, error handling, slug generation, and bidirectional updates all followed established patterns, resulting in clean first implementation with no structural issues.\n  [success] @01KGM07T (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Validation ordering for better errors - When validating references, check type mismatch (isTask/isSpecItem) before checking existence. Gives 'Reference X is not a plan' instead of 'Plan reference not found' when user passes wrong type. Applied in task add and task set --plan-ref validation.\n  [success] @01KGM07Q (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Schema-first implementation pattern - Defining schemas (Plan, PlansFile) first, then parser functions (loadPlans, findPlanByRef), then CLI commands (plan add/get/set) created clean separation. Each layer had focused tests. Pattern worked well for both plan CLI (23 tests) and plan_ref field (8 tests).\n  [friction] @01KGM07N (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Test reference ambiguity with @01 prefix - When writing integration tests that create plans/tasks, using generic references like @01 fails when test fixtures already contain items with ULIDs starting with 01. Must use explicit slugs (--slug test-plan-1) for test isolation. Affected cli-plan.test.ts and task-plan-ref.test.ts - all references needed slugs added.\n  [success] @01KGKYGF (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Spec split for automation eligibility: Splitting a large spec into focused child specs made task-to-AC mapping clear and enabled granular automation eligibility assessment.\n  [friction] @01KGKYGD (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Parent relationship not persisted: item add with under flag creates items but parent field is null. Also spec says parent flag but CLI requires under flag.\n  [success] @01KGKVNV (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Spawning a Plan agent to review completed spec/task work against the original plan catches gaps (like missing traits) that the primary agent overlooked. Useful pattern for quality assurance on spec creation work.\n  [success] @01KGK93J (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Task notes with root cause analysis and specific file list made implementation straightforward - this pattern should be replicated when creating reflection-based tasks\n  [success] @01KGG9M7 (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    kspec bootstraps greenfield projects well: Initialized kynamo (Rust rewrite project) with kspec to track its own development. Shadow branch, spec items for 6 implementation phases, and inbox items all created smoothly. Demonstrates kspec's value beyond self-hosting - can bootstrap any new project with structured planning.\n  [success] @01KGG9KZ (4 weeks ago by @claude)\n    Multi-codebase exploration via parallel subagents: Spawning 4-6 explore/research agents simultaneously to analyze kspec and kynetic-bot for gap analysis, architecture comparison, and Rust migration planning was highly effective. Each agent focused on one domain (CLI stack, bot ecosystem, ACP, context mgmt, migration strategy) and the results synthesized into comprehensive documentation.\n  [success] @01KG95K2 (1/30/2026 by @claude)\n    kspec search command exists and works well - found it during reflection when checking coverage. Searched 'multi-ref' and got comprehensive results across specs, tasks, inbox. Should use this more proactively during work.\n  [success] @01KG8RB4 (1/30/2026 by @claude)\n    User edge-case questions ('what about Ctrl+C?', 'does it work on Windows?') led to better implementation. Don't rush to close - invite scrutiny.\n  [success] @01KG8RAY (1/30/2026 by @claude)\n    Review feedback loop (Greptile + Claude) caught real bugs: async race condition in signal handler and no-op test. Worth keeping mandatory.\n  [success] @01KG8PFD (1/30/2026 by @claude)\n    Following existing patterns (task-limit marker) made implementing new feature (end-iteration) straightforward. Same file paths, detection mechanism, and cleanup lifecycle.\n  [success] @01KG7ARF (1/30/2026 by @claude)\n    Test pattern: when creating multiple items in quick succession for batch operation tests, use JSON mode to capture full ULIDs instead of parsing 8-char prefixes from human output. ULID prefixes can collide when items are created within the same millisecond, causing test deduplication to produce unexpected results. Full ULIDs are always unique.\n  [success] @01KG6WJZ (1/29/2026 by @claude)\n    Observation triage session efficiently resolved 12 friction observations by: (1) batch retrieving all observations, (2) cross-referencing AGENTS.md documentation, (3) categorizing into resolvable/keep-open/historical before acting, (4) verifying obsolete items (like deleted test files) before resolution\n  [success] @01KG64RZ (1/29/2026 by @claude)\n    CLI --force flag pattern works well for agent/script use. inbox delete --force skips confirmation. Same pattern could apply to other commands needing non-interactive mode.\n  [success] @01KG5SGK (1/29/2026 by @claude)\n    Explore+Plan parallel subagent pattern for implementation tasks: Before implementing a task with existing spec/notes, launch two subagents in parallel - (1) Explore agent to gather codebase context (file locations, interfaces, test patterns) and (2) Plan agent to design the implementation approach. Both complete before coding begins, providing thorough context AND a concrete plan. Used successfully for ref normalization task - all tests passed first try.\n  [friction] @01KG4M2Y (1/29/2026 by @claude)\n    Skill documentation can become stale without detection: /e2e skill claimed getByRole('option') doesn't work with bits-ui when it actually does (bits-ui added proper ARIA roles). No mechanism flags skill docs as potentially outdated when dependencies change. Task @01KG1H91 was created based on incorrect assumption from stale docs.\n  [success] @01KG4M2R (1/29/2026 by @claude)\n    Throwaway inspection tests: Writing quick, disposable tests to inspect actual DOM/state is effective debugging. DOM inspection test revealed bits-ui elements have both role='option' AND data-slot attributes - concrete evidence in minutes.\n  [success] @01KG4M2H (1/29/2026 by @claude)\n    Investigation-first debugging: When tests pass but docs say they shouldn't, investigate WHY before closing. In this session, discovered skill documentation was outdated rather than tests being wrong - prevented false task closure and led to fixing actual issue (stale docs).\n  [success] @01KG2FM2 (1/28/2026 by @claude)\n    isStaticMode() guard pattern worked well for conditionally disabling features without major refactoring - single check at API layer propagates through entire app\n  [friction] @01KG17RD (1/27/2026 by @claude)\n    Session context is loaded from .kspec-session file (not kynetic.meta.yaml). This caused debugging time when E2E tests couldn't find session focus. The API (fetchSessionContext) and file location should be documented in /e2e skill or AGENTS.md.\n  [success] @01KG17R6 (1/27/2026 by @claude)\n    Using /e2e skill patterns immediately caught ULID validation issue (Crockford base32 excludes I, L, O, U). Invalid ULIDs in fixtures fail silently - the skill's warning helped debug quickly.\n  [success] @01KG0ZHY (1/27/2026 by @claude)\n    Dedicated E2E fixtures pattern - creating packages/web-ui/tests/fixtures/ separate from shared tests/fixtures/ cleanly isolates E2E test data from unit tests, preventing cross-contamination\n  [friction] @01KG0N4V (1/27/2026 by @claude)\n    Schema validation (safeParse) failures in extractItemsFromRaw are silent - items just don't appear in results with no error or warning. This caused significant debugging time when trait had invalid enum values and module had nested items in traits field. Consider adding validation error logging or a strict mode that throws on parse failures.\n  [success] @01KG0J4X (1/27/2026 by @claude)\n    Documenting blocking issues with specific symptoms, likely causes ranked by probability, and debug commands - makes next session pickup much faster\n  [success] @01KG0J4H (1/27/2026 by @claude)\n    Reading reference tests (tasks.spec.ts) before writing new tests - understanding existing patterns informed structure and avoided reinventing conventions\n  [success] @01KG031Y (1/27/2026 by @claude)\n    Console log capture in E2E tests revealed exact bits-ui callback values, making the array quirk immediately visible. Adding console.log to component handlers during debugging is highly effective for understanding Svelte 5 reactivity issues.\n  [success] @01KFZ708 (1/26/2026 by @claude)\n    Svelte 5 reactivity solution: Use $state object pattern + flushSync() for reactive conditional rendering. Separate $state variables may not trigger {#if} block re-renders; wrapping state in object (panel = $state({ open: false, task: null })) and using flushSync(() => { panel.open = true }) ensures synchronous DOM updates. Also documented in /svelte-5 skill. Resolves friction @01KFYAH9.\n  [success] @01KFZ3YN (1/26/2026 by @claude)\n    Using Explore subagent for deep analysis before making changes provided comprehensive understanding (file locations, line numbers, root cause, impact analysis) that enabled confident parallel fixes via multiple subagents.\n  [friction] @01KFZ3YG (1/26/2026 by @claude)\n    Task notes from loop mode sessions can become noisy/unreliable, requiring manual cleanup or user warning to ignore recent notes. Example: @01KFP84N had 26 noise notes removed and user warned remaining notes might be incorrect.\n  [success] @01KFYAH1 (1/26/2026 by @claude)\n    Debug element technique for Svelte reactivity: Adding conditional debug elements like `{#if state}<div data-testid=\"debug-...\">{state}</div>{/if}` quickly verifies whether state is actually true at runtime vs expected. Useful for diagnosing reactivity issues where console logs show updates but UI doesn't reflect them.\n  [success] @01KFXVKJ (1/26/2026 by @claude)\n    Comparing dist/ vs source when debugging daemon issues - immediately revealed the stale build problem when runtime debugging was going in circles\n  [success] @01KFXTAK (1/26/2026 by @claude)\n    Subagent plan review caught CLI command syntax issues (missing --as and --id flags) before execution. Using a reviewer subagent after writing a plan prevents wasted iterations.\n  [friction] @01KFXTAD (1/26/2026 by @claude)\n    Tasks created via 'kspec derive' have auto-generated notes that just copy spec content. For standalone tasks, needed to manually add structured sections: Background (why this matters), Reproduction steps, Concrete examples, Open design questions. This structure makes tasks actionable without conversation context.\n  [success] @01KFVXSQ (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop exit condition working correctly: Session 28 demonstrated clean exit behavior after @01KFV6CZ fix. Loop checked 'kspec tasks ready --eligible', found empty array (all 3 in_progress tasks were automation:needs_review), and exited immediately with zero redundant iterations. This resolves the overnight session issue where loops ran 30+ iterations with no progress. Exit condition now filters both ready AND in_progress tasks by automation:eligible status.\n  [success] @01KFVVHG (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop mode escalation discipline working as designed: Session 01KFVN3D ran 18 iterations with 3 in_progress tasks. All tasks marked automation:needs_review in iterations 7-8 after assessment. Loop correctly exited when kspec tasks ready --eligible returned empty array. No thrashing, no shortcuts attempted, no improper completion claims. Tasks stayed in_progress with comprehensive checkpoint notes providing human handoff context. This validates the automation system recognizing its limits and delegating complex/blocked work appropriately.\n  [success] @01KFVVB2 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Session checkpoint notes (iterations 7-17) consistently documented: task state, what's complete, what's blocked, required resolution steps, investigation files, automation status. This pattern provides excellent continuity for next session and clear audit trail of escalation reasoning.\n  [friction] @01KFVVAT (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph automation loop ran 10+ iterations (7-17) with zero implementation work after all tasks marked needs_review. Loop exit logic only checks task existence, not progress stagnation. Should detect: N consecutive iterations with no file changes, commits, or task status transitions → early exit with diagnostic.\n  [success] @01KFVTYW (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Task escalation with comprehensive rationale prevents false automation work. Pattern observed: when tasks blocked or require human decisions, automation loop adds multi-paragraph notes documenting: (1) WHAT blocks the work, (2) WHY it's not automatable, (3) WHAT human needs to do next. Tags task automation:needs_review. Result: zero false work attempts across 15 iterations, clear handoff context for human sessions. Quality over speed: better to escalate with context than attempt inappropriate work.\n  [success] @01KFVTBK (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Session checkpoint hook enforcement prevented task abandonment: Stop hook detected 3 in_progress tasks without recent meaningful progress and blocked session exit until checkpoint notes were added. All tasks received documentation of current state, blockers, and next steps. This pattern ensures tasks don't silently stall - they either get documented or completed. Hook runs automatically on session end with no manual intervention needed. Particularly valuable for automation loops where task state must be explicit for next iteration.\n  [friction] @01KFVTBA (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop iteration without progress: Loop ran 12 consecutive iterations with zero code changes or git commits. All iterations exited with 'no automation-eligible tasks' message. Tasks remained in_progress state throughout despite being marked automation:needs_review. Each iteration added near-identical checkpoint notes documenting 'no work attempted'. Pattern indicates need for circuit breaker that detects repeated no-progress iterations and either auto-escalates tasks or stops the loop. Related to @01KF554T circuit breaker concept but captures actual observed behavior rather than theoretical design.\n  [friction] @01KFVSKK (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop mode automation tag disconnect: Across 8 consecutive loop iterations, task notes documented 'marked automation:needs_review' but the actual command 'kspec task set @ref --automation needs_review' was never executed. This suggests a pattern in autonomous loops where intent is documented in notes but the corresponding state change command is not run. Root cause: loop documenting WHAT should happen without executing HOW to make it happen.\n  [success] @01KFVSBZ (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    E2E test infrastructure debugging pattern: When E2E tests fail, work backward through the stack systematically. Session 01KFVN3D example: test timeout → Vite proxy missing → API client hardcoded URLs → daemon fixture loading issue. At each layer, verify the fix (tests connect successfully) before moving to next layer. This methodical approach identified root cause (daemon ContextManager path handling) after fixing intermediate issues (Vite proxy + relative URLs in commit c24fd694).\n  [success] @01KFVRK6 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop iteration 5: Accountability in action - instead of immediately marking tasks as needs_review, actually debugged E2E infrastructure issues. Fixed real problems (Vite proxy configuration, API client URL handling) that were blocking all E2E tests. Committed working code (c24fd694) that moves the project forward. Loop failures should document genuine investigation and progress, not just 'this is hard, skipping.'\n  [friction] @01KFVQYR (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop mode error handling AC-5 not working: Tasks @01KFP84N, @01KFP84S, @01KFQAGY all have [LOOP-FAIL:3] notes but remain automation:eligible instead of being auto-escalated to automation:needs_review as specified in @loop-mode-error-handling AC-5. Implementation in PR #243 claims to handle this but actual behavior shows automation status not being updated after 3 failures. Verified in session 01KFVN3DDTKEQF1DAF8V3KV48V iteration 4.\n  [friction] @01KFVQKF (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Task DoD conflict with project conventions: Task @01KFQAF9 DoD said 'write real HTTP tests' and 'no documentary/stub tests remain' but AGENTS.md documents that daemon API routes use documentary tests (static analysis). Had to resolve conflict by checking existing patterns in daemon-api-*.test.ts files. Resolution: Created E2E tests in packages/web-ui/tests/e2e/ and updated documentary test to reference them. Friction point: When DoD contradicts documented patterns, unclear which is authoritative.\n  [success] @01KFVQKD (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    E2E tests via Playwright for daemon API endpoints: Using packages/web-ui/tests/e2e/*.spec.ts for real HTTP integration tests (not vitest mocks) aligns with project architecture documented in AGENTS.md. This session: created api-projects.spec.ts with 5 passing tests making real fetch() calls to running daemon. Pattern: Start daemon via test fixture, make HTTP requests, verify responses. More reliable than mocking and tests actual behavior.\n  [success] @01KFVPYB (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Reading actual source code to verify task claims proved more reliable than trusting notes. When @01KFP84N notes said 'UI incomplete', reading TaskList.svelte revealed all required test IDs were present. Verification approach: Read components -> check test IDs -> run tests (if possible) -> only then accept completion claims. This prevented false conclusions about missing implementation.\n  [friction] @01KFVPY3 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop mode task verification gap: Multiple iterations accepted task notes claiming 'already implemented' or 'tests exist' without independent verification (running tests, checking code). Iteration 1 concluded UI was incomplete based on test failures; iteration 2 found UI components actually exist with proper test IDs. Real issue was E2E infrastructure, not missing implementation. Task notes are context, not proof of completion.\n  [success] @01KFTTA3 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Structured loop iteration note template pattern: Used consistently across 30 automation iterations to document why progress wasn't possible. Template format: '## Loop Iteration N - [Status]' followed by Exit Reason, Task Status (with ✅/❌ checkboxes for completed/incomplete work), specific blockers, and clear next actions. Creates audit trail showing automation decision-making and prevents false 'why is nothing happening' concerns. Makes it obvious when automation is correctly identifying blockers vs stuck in broken logic.\n  [friction] @01KFTSWY (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop ran 28 iterations with identical outcome (no eligible tasks, same 3 tasks documented each time). After 2-3 consecutive iterations returning no eligible tasks, the loop should recognize the pattern and exit early. Current behavior wastes API calls and adds redundant notes. Need early termination logic: if last N iterations (N=2 or 3) all exited with 'no eligible tasks' and same task set, stop the loop.\n  [friction] @01KFTSR2 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Automation loop adds repetitive status notes to stalled tasks. After 27 iterations of ralph automation, same 3 in-progress tasks (@01KFP84N, @01KFP84S, @01KFQAGY) accumulated 27+ nearly-identical notes documenting 'Loop Iteration N - No Eligible Tasks'. While notes provide audit trail, they create significant noise obscuring substantive progress notes. Consider: batch note update mechanism, dedicated status/stall-reason field, or suppress iteration notes when status unchanged.\n  [friction] @01KFTSQQ (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Workflow system lacks early exit mechanism. When workflow determines no work is possible (e.g., no eligible tasks), must still step through all remaining workflow steps providing skip reasons. Attempted 'kspec workflow complete @ref' which does not exist. Would benefit from 'workflow exit' or 'workflow skip-remaining' command to cleanly terminate when continuation is impossible.\n  [success] @01KFTR8Z (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop iteration 21 efficiently completed pending_review task @01KFP85M in first action by checking task status, validating work was already merged, and immediately completing it. Demonstrates value of prioritizing pending_review tasks over in_progress tasks - they're usually closest to done and just need verification/completion. Pattern: pending_review > in_progress > ready.\n  [friction] @01KFTR8F (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Repetitive checkpoint notes across automation loops: Tasks stuck in automation:needs_review state get identical checkpoint notes added every iteration. Example: @01KFP84N received 36 notes, with notes 27-36 being nearly identical 'Loop Iteration N - Session End Checkpoint' messages. Creates noise without adding value. Possible solutions: (1) Skip checkpoint notes if last note is recent and task state unchanged, (2) Update most recent checkpoint note instead of appending, (3) Add checkpoint command that manages session-end state separately from progress notes.\n  [success] @01KFTQJ6 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop iteration 19 assessment: Correctly identified that all 4 eligible GitHub Pages export tasks (@01KFMMJ4, @01KFMMXK, @01KFMMXR, @01KFMMXW) exceed automation loop capacity. Marked all as automation:needs_review with clear reasoning. Tasks form a coordinated feature set requiring multi-hour focused implementation.\n  [friction] @01KFTPYV (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Zero-completion automation sessions: Loop iteration 17 completed with 0 tasks finished, all 3 in_progress tasks escalated to automation:needs_review. Distinct from prior successful Ralph loops. Root cause: Tasks marked automation:eligible but require daemon E2E testing (2 tasks) or multi-file coordinated implementation (1 task). Gap between 'eligible' tag and actual automation feasibility in single iteration. Affects task selection heuristics.\n  [success] @01KFTPYK (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Automation loop graceful exit: When tasks require daemon/multi-file coordination exceeding loop capacity, system correctly escalated 3 tasks to automation:needs_review with comprehensive handoff notes. Clean exit without attempting incomplete implementations. Session 01KFTDBQB3Z0PA5Y0GQD8T1RW3 iteration 17.\n  [friction] @01KFTPNH (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Task automation eligibility assessment happens too late in workflow. Tasks get marked automation:eligible without scope assessment. Automation loops then waste iterations doing [LOOP-FAIL:1], [LOOP-FAIL:2], [LOOP-FAIL:3] assessments before marking automation:needs_review. Example: task @01KFMMXF assessed in iterations 6, 7, 14 as too complex (~300-500 lines) but stayed in eligible pool causing repeated evaluation. Assessment should occur BEFORE tasks marked eligible, not after automation attempts them. Related to @task-automation-eligibility but focuses on pre-eligibility workflow gap.\n  [friction] @01KFTPN9 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Task-work-loop workflow lacks exit condition for 'eligible but unsuitable' scenario. When eligible tasks exist but all exceed single-iteration capacity (e.g., 5 P3 tasks requiring 300-500 lines each), workflow continues to step 4 (start task) with no clean exit path. Had to manually cancel workflow in iteration 16. Exit conditions defined (no eligible tasks, already done, work complete) but not 'all eligible tasks too complex'. Affects ralph when only complex work remains in eligible pool.\n  [friction] @01KFTP0G (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Automation loop cannot handle daemon-dependent E2E test verification. Pattern: Tasks get to ~80-85% complete (implementation done, test IDs added, tests enabled) but then block on daemon startup + iterative test debugging. Three tasks hit this in session 01KFTDBQB3Z0PA5Y0GQD8T1RW3 (iteration 14): @01KFP84N, @01KFP84S, @01KFQAGY. Root cause: Daemon requires manual 'npm run serve', E2E failures unpredictable, debugging cycle exceeds single-iteration capacity. Workaround: Mark automation:needs_review and exit loop. Impact: Web UI E2E testing tasks systematically blocked from automation completion. Potential solutions: (1) Automate daemon lifecycle in loop, (2) Split tasks into 'implementation' + 'E2E verification' phases, (3) Add automation:daemon-required tag for better categorization.\n  [success] @01KFTMKW (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Batching related E2E test tasks into a single PR branch: Completed 3 tasks (@01KFP85D observations panel, @01KFP85G error handling, @01KFP85M navigation) on feature branch feat/observations-panel-e2e-tests. All tasks involved E2E tests and data-testid attributes for web UI. Benefits: (1) Avoided multiple PR context switches and branch overhead, (2) Shared commits across tasks (e.g., error-message testid used by all 3), (3) Coherent scope for review. Pattern works when tasks are tightly related, share common files, and can be reviewed together. Session completed 4 tasks total efficiently using this approach.\n  [success] @01KFTKCK (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Task verification pattern: Before starting test-only task (01KFP854 - E2E tests), verified WebSocketManager implementation existed. Read manager.ts to confirm AC coverage (ac-28 to ac-32) before committing to E2E test creation. This prevented wasting iteration on tests for non-existent features. Pattern: For tasks scoped as 'write tests for X', verify X implementation exists first. Especially valuable in automation loops with limited iteration budget.\n  [success] @01KFTJNJ (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Task-work workflow (@task-work-loop) successfully enforced complete task lifecycle in automation loop: task selection from eligible list, verification before start, implementation with notes during work, DoD validation, commit with trailers, submit to pending_review, PR creation. Session 01KFTDBQB3Z0PA5Y0GQD8T1RW3 iteration 9 completed @01KFP850 (command palette E2E tests) from start to PR without friction. Workflow prevented common mistakes: starting without verification, skipping notes, missing DoD checks.\n  [friction] @01KFTJNA (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Automation loop cannot verify E2E tests that require daemon running. Pattern: Tasks add data-testid attributes and create E2E tests, but verification requires manual session to start daemon (npm run serve -w packages/web-ui) and run tests. Repeated across @01KFP84N (tasks view), @01KFP84S (items view), @01KFP84X (inbox view) in session 01KFTDBQB3Z0PA5Y0GQD8T1RW3. Tasks marked automation:needs_review and stall at ~70-80% complete. Potential solutions: headless daemon fixture for E2E tests, mock API layer, or split tasks (implementation + verification).\n  [success] @01KFTHKM (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Task-work-loop workflow provided effective structure for automation. Clear steps (filter eligible → select → verify → start → work → commit → submit → PR → exit) prevented skipped steps. Workflow state tracking enabled clean handoffs. Successfully completed inbox view implementation (01KFP84X) following all steps systematically.\n  [friction] @01KFTHKC (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Session checkpoint hook blocks exit when in_progress tasks have automation:needs_review status. Hook expects all in_progress tasks to either complete or have handoff notes. However, tasks marked automation:needs_review are intentionally paused for human review. Workaround: manually add comprehensive handoff notes before exit. Pattern suggests automation:needs_review should either auto-add handoff notes or be recognized as valid exit state.\n  [friction] @01KFTHK6 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    E2E test tasks hit systematic blocker in automation loops: Cannot run daemon (npm run serve) + E2E tests (npm run test:e2e) within loop constraints. Pattern affects all web UI E2E test tasks. Results in [LOOP-FAIL] notes and automation:needs_review escalation. Encountered on tasks 01KFP84N, 01KFP84S, 01KFP84X in iteration 8. Implementation completes successfully (test IDs + test files), but verification step requires human-driven session with long-running daemon.\n  [success] @01KFTH5G (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop error handling protocol (PR #243) executed correctly in iteration 7: [LOOP-FAIL:N] notes tracked failures per task, failure count visible across iterations, auto-escalation to automation:needs_review triggered at threshold 3. Pattern enables clear visibility into which tasks are struggling in automation and systematic escalation when human intervention needed. Tasks @01KFP84N and @01KFP84S hit 3rd failure (daemon requirement blocker) and were properly escalated.\n  [friction] @01KFTGRP (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop mode failure tracking not queryable: [LOOP-FAIL:N] notes exist but no CLI command to check task failure count. Had to manually grep task notes to determine if @01KFP84N and @01KFP84S hit 3-failure threshold (both at count 2). Needed for automation decision: should loop retry task or escalate to automation:needs_review? Suggest: kspec task get @ref --loop-failures or similar to return failure count.\n  [friction] @01KFTGKV (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Loop iteration 6 encountered systematic blockers: All eligible P2 tasks are web UI E2E tests requiring daemon startup + test verification, exceeding single iteration capacity. Both in-progress tasks (@01KFP84N, @01KFP84S) hit same blocker (daemon requirement) and now at failure count 2. Attempted @01KFMMXF (export command) but ~300-500 line implementation scope also exceeds capacity. Pattern suggests: (1) Web UI E2E tasks incorrectly marked automation:eligible - should be automation:needs_review until daemon automation exists, OR (2) Tasks should be split: 'Add test IDs' (automatable) + 'Verify E2E tests pass' (manual), OR (3) DoD should be relaxed to 'Tests enabled, pending verification'.\n  [success] @01KFTFX9 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Comprehensive session-end notes enable clean task handoffs. Final note on @01KFP84N documented: what's done, what remains, estimated effort, specific next steps, blockers, recommendations. This level of detail makes continuing work in next iteration frictionless - no context rebuilding needed. Pattern: when leaving task in_progress, include (1) completed work summary, (2) remaining work list, (3) effort estimate, (4) next concrete steps, (5) blockers/considerations. Especially valuable in automation loops where next iteration may be hours/days later.\n  [success] @01KFTFX1 (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Partial progress commits valuable in automation loops. Iteration 4 on @01KFP84N: added test IDs to components (fd70b86d) even though DoD required fully enabled tests. Committing partial work provided value: (1) next iteration can continue from checkpoint, (2) incremental progress visible in git history, (3) work not lost if task abandoned. Better than all-or-nothing approach. Pattern: commit meaningful increments even if task incomplete, document remaining work clearly in notes and commit message.\n  [friction] @01KFTFWS (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Task Definition of Done (DoD) not visible during task selection. Task @01KFP84N had DoD requirement 'All E2E tests pass - no skips' in notes but wasn't surfaced during ready task selection. Workflow enforced DoD strictly at completion but constraint wasn't visible upfront. Created situation where task appeared eligible but became non-completable mid-work. DoD criteria are critical constraints that should influence task selection, not just exit validation. Suggest: extract DoD to structured field, show in task list, filter tasks by DoD compatibility.\n  [friction] @01KFTFWF (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Automation-eligible tasks lack time/scope estimates. All eligible tasks in iteration 4 were large-scope (10+ ACs, 30-60+ min work). Automation loops work best with 10-15 min tasks but selection mechanism doesn't filter by estimated effort. Existing eligibility system addresses complexity/clarity but not time dimension. Result: loops start work that can't complete in single iteration. Suggest: add estimated_effort field (small/medium/large) to tasks, --max-effort flag for automation.\n  [success] @01KFTESK (1/25/2026 by @claude)\n    Test-first development pattern: Writing comprehensive tests before integration implementation catches edge cases early and provides clear specification. Pattern used in loop-mode-error-handling task: (1) Created test file with 23 tests covering all 9 ACs, (2) Implemented utilities to make tests pass, (3) Integrated into ralph.ts. Benefits: TypeScript compilation errors caught immediately, edge cases identified before integration, tests serve as executable documentation. This approach is especially valuable for complex logic with multiple edge cases (failure tracking, progress detection, escalation thresholds).\n  [friction] @01KFT1QE (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Shadow branch divergence lacks guided resolution. When local and remote kspec-meta diverge significantly (61 vs 362 commits), kspec shadow sync just fails with no guidance. Manual git merge-tree diagnosis and conflict-by-conflict resolution required. @yaml-merge-driver would help but was cancelled.\n  [success] @01KFT1Q5 (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Subagent delegation for git history recovery: Pattern of spawn agent → search git log -p -S → extract lost content → report structured findings worked well for recovering 12+ lost notes from merge. Effective when content exists in history but was overwritten.\n  [friction] @01KFST52 (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Documentary test anti-pattern: Agents write test.skip() tests that pass CI but don't verify anything. Task framing as 'write tests' enables this. Root cause: tests treated as goal rather than validation mechanism.\n  [success] @01KFST50 (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Task reframing pattern: Change 'Write tests for X' to 'Implement X (AC-N to AC-M)' with implementation steps, required testIds, and 'No test.skip()' in Definition of Done. Prevents documentary test anti-pattern.\n  [success] @01KFST4Y (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Subagent audit pattern: Spawn agent to analyze multiple tasks → get structured findings → act on findings. Effective for task/test audits where parallel exploration needed.\n  [friction] @01KFS6P9 (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Workflow runs accumulate without lifecycle management. 34+ active runs exist with no clear way to complete/cancel old runs. Need: kspec workflow complete/cancel/cleanup commands to manage run lifecycle and prevent stale run accumulation.\n  [success] @01KFS6AX (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    E2E test creation workflow worked smoothly. Pattern established in project-selector.spec.ts provided clear template for documentary tests. Using test.skip() for pending implementation is effective for capturing expected behavior before UI exists. All 17 tests passed structure validation on first run.\n  [success] @01KFRCQH (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Using testUlid() helper for test fixtures prevents silent failures from invalid Crockford base32 chars (L, I, O, U). The helper auto-replaces these with valid chars. PR #210 adds documentation for this pattern.\n  [success] @01KFRAKV (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Incremental question-based requirements via AskUserQuestion: Asking one focused question at a time (task selection order, subagent model, skill invocation, error tracking) kept decisions clear and avoided ambiguity in spec design.\n  [success] @01KFRAKC (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Plan-Review-Revise cycle with subagents: Having a Plan agent design specs, then a second Plan agent review for gaps with full user constraint context, then fixing issues before presenting to user. Caught critical issues like ambiguous subagent spawn trigger.\n  [success] @01KFR0MB (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Subagent plan review before spec finalization - having a Plan subagent review the spec plan caught gaps (missing error cases, race conditions, path validation) that would have been harder to fix after writing the spec. Worth making standard practice for architecture specs.\n  [success] @01KFR0M0 (1/24/2026 by @claude)\n    Iterative question-based design with AskUserQuestion tool - asking one design question at a time during spec work (X-Kspec-Dir header choice, PID location, etc.) kept decisions focused and got clear answers. Better than presenting all options at once.\n  [success] @01KFQ8W7 (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    Capturing scope expansion immediately: When debugging revealed architectural issues beyond task scope, captured to inbox then promoted to proper task with full research context. Prevented context loss.\n  [success] @01KFQ8VZ (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    Subagent research pattern: Delegating architecture research to a subagent produced comprehensive findings with code examples, references, and concrete recommendations - more efficient than inline research\n  [success] @01KFQ59K (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    Same-origin architecture: daemon serving both web UI and API on single port simplifies CORS, WebSocket setup, and eliminates cross-origin complexity\n  [success] @01KFQ59D (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    E2E debugging: Playwright error-context.md files with page snapshots are invaluable for understanding what browser actually sees vs what code expects\n  [success] @01KFQ3AD (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    Detailed task implementation notes made E2E infrastructure setup straightforward - step-by-step guidance with verification checklist reduced ambiguity\n  [success] @01KFPH3Z (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    Documenting findings in task notes before implementing - created clear audit trail of decisions (daemon bundling approach) that future sessions can reference.\n  [success] @01KFPH3X (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    Investigation-first approach for bug fixes - reading task context, exploring code to understand root cause before proposing solutions. Found test failures were separate bug from the actual task issue.\n  [success] @01KFP9AN (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    AC-driven test design - Reading spec file directly and mapping tests to acceptance criteria ensured 100% coverage. Table format (AC | Test) made gaps immediately visible.\n  [success] @01KFP9AJ (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    Parallel subagent research for planning - Launching multiple explore agents simultaneously (one for best practices research, one for existing codebase patterns) provided comprehensive context quickly. Combined with Plan agent review, caught issues early before implementation.\n  [friction] @01KFNKFR (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    Interactive CLI tools block automation loops. Encountered with 'sv add tailwindcss' and 'shadcn-svelte init' - both require user input even with --yes flag. This forces either: (1) finding alternative non-interactive installation methods, (2) manual configuration, or (3) pre-scripted answers via expect/stdin. For automation-friendly workflows, prefer tools with true --yes/--no-interaction flags or configuration files over interactive prompts.\n  [success] @01KFNAT4 (1/23/2026 by @claude)\n    PR merge workflow with review resolution: Successfully merged PR #189 using @pr-review-merge workflow. All CI checks passed (tests, claude-review). Greptile feedback was addressed in code but threads weren't programmatically resolvable - documented in PR comments instead. Workflow enforced systematic review before merge.\n  [success] @01KFMP73 (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Detailed task notes with code snippets, file paths, and AC mappings. Makes tasks self-contained for automation and future sessions without needing original context.\n  [success] @01KFMP6T (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Plan mode with subagent exploration before spec creation. Using Explore agent to gather context from related projects (kynetic) and Plan agent to review/refine plans caught gaps early.\n  [success] @01KFMH5S (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Trait-based approach for cross-cutting concerns - creating @trait-shadow-commit made implicit shadow commit behavior explicit, testable, and documented. Subagent exploration before planning caught 6 additional missing commits beyond initial 4. Spec-first implementation ensures AC annotations in code reference back to spec.\n  [success] @01KFM8HP (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Plan mode + subagent review workflow - Having a Plan agent review the plan before presenting to user caught several issues (exit code semantics, missing AC coverage, CLI signature mismatch) that would have caused rework. Worth replicating for complex specs.\n  [success] @01KFKSGW (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Hybrid skill-workflow model works well: skill as entrypoint providing context, workflow for structure and tracking. Reduced reflect skill from 215 to 152 lines while adding progress tracking.\n  [idea] @01KFKRBG (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Add step editing to workflow add command\n  [friction] @01KFKA4S (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Branch deletion without content verification: compared commit messages but didn't diff actual file contents. Should always run git diff <branch>:<file> <main>:<file> before concluding content is merged.\n  [success] @01KFKA4R (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop analysis: 5 parallel subagents (iterations, PR compliance, reflections, commits, review feedback) provided comprehensive audit coverage efficiently. Pattern: split analysis by concern, run in parallel, synthesize results.\n  [success] @01KFJHA3 (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Pattern for adding new validation types: Created separate validation module (validate-skills.ts), integrated into validate command with dedicated flag (--skills), added comprehensive tests in matching test file. This modular approach makes validation extensible without bloating the main validate.ts file.\n  [success] @01KFJGQE (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    E2E test pattern for multi-entity search worked well: tested each entity type individually first (inbox, observation, agent, workflow, convention), then attempted combined test. When combined test had issues, individual passing tests proved core functionality works. This approach provides both comprehensive coverage and clear failure isolation.\n  [success] @01KFJFZ1 (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop iteration completed two tasks efficiently: First reviewed and merged PR #164 (validation warning), then picked up next ready task (@01KFJ571 ACP type safety) and completed it with PR #165. The loop workflow (check PRs → merge if ready → pick next task) worked smoothly. Key pattern: Complete one task fully (including PR creation) before loop ends.\n  [success] @01KFJFVX (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Type safety improvements catch bugs at compile-time: Adding typed response methods (respondReadTextFile, respondWriteTextFile) to ACPClient prevents runtime bugs by enforcing correct response structures. The pattern of replacing 'unknown' with SDK types (ReadTextFileRequest, etc.) provides IDE autocomplete and catches errors early. This approach worked well - build caught all type mismatches immediately.\n  [success] @01KFJF6V (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    PR workflow from main branch with unpushed commits: create feature branch, reset main to origin/main, push feature branch. This cleanly separates completed work from main without losing commits.\n  [success] @01KFJF6N (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Implementing validation warnings follows a clear pattern: 1) Add warning type to interface, 2) Add check logic in validation function, 3) Add formatting in display function, 4) Add E2E test. The existing staleness checks provided a good template to follow.\n  [success] @01KFJENS (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Successfully executed ralph loop PR review workflow: spawned review subagent → identified MUST-FIX issue (missing tests) → added test coverage → pushed to PR branch → waited for CI → verified all checks pass → merged with --merge flag to preserve trailers. Review agent correctly identified the gap between stated goal (catch format bugs) and reality (no tests exercising the code).\n  [success] @01KFJENJ (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review workflow caught missing test coverage - the automated review correctly identified that new mock functionality had zero tests, blocking merge until tests were added. Added 6 comprehensive tests by spawning the mock subprocess and using ACPClient to validate the full JSON-RPC protocol flow. The review requirement to test through the actual mock process (not just unit testing the logic) ensured we validated the complete integration.\n  [success] @01KFJE2D (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Task @01KFJ52W completed smoothly using proper workflow: read real implementation first (src/cli/commands/ralph.ts), understood the protocol format, implemented matching behavior in mock. Reading the production code before implementing the test mock ensured format compatibility.\n  [success] @01KFJDE8 (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop PR workflow is now well-established: create PR for pending_review tasks, wait for CI, review comments, merge if clean, then complete task. This iteration demonstrated the full cycle twice (PR #161 merged, PR #162 created).\n  [success] @01KFJDBD (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Biome initialization process worked smoothly - the auto-init command detected the repo structure and configured appropriate excludes (dist/) and VCS integration automatically. Only manual change needed was adjusting indentation style from tabs to spaces to match existing code.\n  [success] @01KFJCZB (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Test-first verification for infrastructure changes: after making the helper change, ran full test suite to verify no regressions. The failing tests revealed a subtle stdin handling bug (empty string vs undefined) that would have been hard to catch otherwise. Full test coverage paid off immediately.\n  [success] @01KFJCZ4 (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Using spawnSync instead of execSync for test helpers provides better control over stdio streams. Key insight: execSync only returns stdout on success - stderr is lost. spawnSync captures both stdout/stderr in separate properties regardless of exit code. This pattern is more predictable for testing CLI tools that write warnings to stderr.\n  [success] @01KFJCFN (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Review subagent pattern from RALPH_LOOP_INSTRUCTIONS.md worked exceptionally well. Single Task tool invocation with focused prompt ('check code quality, test coverage, alignment with spec, be strict about AC coverage') produced comprehensive review in table format covering: AC coverage (100%), code quality (excellent), test quality (E2E, no fluff), CI status (passing), files changed summary, and clear merge decision. The strict AC coverage requirement caught that all 4 direct ACs + 6 inherited trait ACs were properly tested. This automated review quality rivals human PR reviews.\n  [success] @01KFJCFD (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Complete iteration 12 workflow executed successfully: Found pending_review task → Created PR #160 with /pr skill → Spawned review subagent → Review approved with 100% AC coverage → Merged PR with --merge strategy → Pulled merged changes to local main. The automated review subagent provided excellent quality checks (AC coverage, test quality, code patterns). Total time from pending_review to merged: ~8 minutes. Key learning: Task should stay in pending_review until AFTER merge (I completed it prematurely after PR creation).\n  [success] @01KFJC2A (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Task state transition worked well: pending_review status held the completed work until PR was created, then completing the task after PR creation provides clean separation. This matches the RALPH_LOOP_INSTRUCTIONS.md pattern: task submit → PR creation → task complete after merge. However, I completed the task immediately after PR creation rather than after merge - should verify if this is the intended workflow or if completion should wait for merge.\n  [success] @01KFJC23 (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    The /pr skill workflow (auto-generate branch name from context, move commits from main to feature branch, create PR) worked seamlessly for shipping completed work. The skill correctly derived 'feat/workflow-advanced-features' from the task reference, handled the branch creation and main reset cleanly, and created a comprehensive PR. This pattern is efficient for the ralph loop where work completes on main and needs to be shipped via PR.\n  [success] @01KFJBZJ (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Workflow advanced features implementation completed smoothly. Key success factors: (1) Read existing workflow implementation first to understand patterns, (2) Extended schema before implementing CLI commands, (3) Followed existing test patterns for comprehensive coverage, (4) All 13 new tests passed on first run without fixes needed. The incremental approach (schema → commands → tests) worked well for this feature.\n  [success] @01KFJBNX (1/22/2026 by @claude)\n    Automated PR review (Greptile) caught critical bug where entry_confirmed was never recorded - only exit_confirmed was working. The review identified that entry_confirmed remained undefined in all cases. This demonstrates the value of automated code review in catching semantic bugs that pass tests. The fix involved creating stub step results when advancing to the next step to capture entry_confirmed, then updating those stubs when the step completes.\n  [success] @01KFJAVE (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Test-driven workflow implementation pattern worked well: wrote comprehensive E2E tests covering all ACs before refining implementation. The tests caught a subtle ordering issue (exit criteria must be checked before entry criteria) and a logical issue (skipping should bypass entry criteria for next step). Having 15 tests covering edge cases (strict/advisory modes, skip/confirm/force combinations) gave confidence that the implementation was correct. All 915 tests passing confirms no regressions.\n  [success] @01KFJA3P (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Iteration 8 completed: Merged PR #157 (workflow step navigation), then completed task @01KFJ4VT (shadow hook tests). Created PR #158. Workflow: reviewed and merged open PR first (per RALPH_LOOP_INSTRUCTIONS), then picked highest priority ready task. Task had clear 3-case scope from notes. Added tests to existing shadow.test.ts file, all 61 tests pass. PR created with /pr skill - auto-generated branch name test/shadow-hook-tests from commit message and task context.\n  [success] @01KFJA21 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Added comprehensive E2E tests for shadow hook functionality. Tests verify: 1) Hook installation during init copies from hooks/pre-commit to .git/hooks/pre-commit with executable permissions, 2) Hook blocks unauthorized commits (verifies commit count stays at 1), 3) Hook allows commits with KSPEC_SHADOW_COMMIT=1 env var (verifies commit count increases and message is correct). Used real hook file for tests 2 and 3 to ensure accurate behavior. All tests run in isolated temp directories. Task notes captured implementation approach before coding.\n  [success] @01KFJ9YY (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Workflow step navigation implementation demonstrates excellent code-to-spec traceability. AC annotations used throughout both implementation and tests. Run reference inference (auto-detect single active run) provides great UX. Error messages include actionable guidance with exact commands. Summary output on completion (duration, step counts) matches spec requirements exactly. This is a model implementation for future workflow features.\n  [success] @01KFJ9YR (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review and merge workflow executed successfully. Used Task agent for comprehensive review (AC coverage, test quality, E2E strategy, test isolation all verified). All CI checks passed, automated reviews found no issues. Task completed cleanly with proper trailers and spec sync. Total time from PR creation to merge: ~4 minutes. The structured review process caught no issues because implementation quality was excellent from the start - comprehensive E2E test coverage, proper AC annotations, perfect test isolation.\n  [success] @01KFJ9NF (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Workflow step navigation implementation went smoothly. The existing workflow foundation (AC tests, error patterns, helper functions) made adding the next command straightforward. Test-first approach caught edge cases early (e.g., inferring run when exactly one active). Pattern of checking spec ACs against test coverage helped ensure nothing was missed.\n  [success] @01KFJ97V (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review workflow is effective: spawned review subagent found 3 incorrect spec references that would have been missed. The strict review caught that annotations referenced @shadow-init instead of @shadow-init-remote for remote detection code. Fixed before merge, maintaining proper traceability.\n  [success] @01KFJ8JB (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review-merge flow worked smoothly: spawned subagent for comprehensive review, verified CI checks passed, checked for review comments, merged with --merge to preserve trailers, then completed task. The strict review requirements (AC coverage, test quality, isolation) caught in advance prevent merge issues. Subagent review before PR creation + automated CI review + manual merge checks = robust quality gate.\n  [success] @01KFJ8J5 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Systematic approach to code cleanup - created comprehensive mapping document before making changes, built automated sed script to handle bulk replacements, then manually verified and fixed edge cases. This pattern of 'map-script-verify-fix' worked well for 72 annotations across 9 files with zero regressions (all 874 tests pass). Script-first approach with manual verification is effective for large-scale mechanical refactoring.\n  [success] @01KFJ7SA (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    When fixing bugs with AC coverage: First read the code to understand the implementation, then identify the exact function/logic that needs updating, then add tests that validate the AC before implementing. This workflow ensures the fix is correct and properly validated. For AC-15, updating getParentTaskRef() to filter cancelled tasks was straightforward once the problem was identified.\n  [success] @01KFJ7AP (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Tests should use non-shadow mode setup (files in project root) unless specifically testing shadow functionality. The .kspec/ directory is ONLY used when shadow branch is properly configured. This keeps test setup simple and matches how kspec actually discovers files in non-shadow environments. Pattern: create kynetic.yaml, kynetic.meta.yaml, project.tasks.yaml directly in temp root directory.\n  [success] @01KFJ7AG (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Test fixture ULID validation issue was quickly identified through systematic debugging. The root cause (invalid ULID format in test fixtures) was found by examining validation failures and understanding that ULIDs must match proper regex format. Fix was straightforward: import ulid() and generate valid ULIDs. This pattern of using valid test data that matches production validation rules is important for reliable tests.\n  [success] @01KFJ636 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Workflow run foundation implementation demonstrates good schema-first development pattern. Defined all Zod schemas first, then storage operations, then CLI commands, then tests. This ordering prevented type errors and made the implementation flow smoothly. The type safety from Zod schemas caught issues early in compilation rather than at runtime.\n  [success] @01KFHYJ9 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    When debugging opaque CI/auth errors, search GitHub issues early. A quick search for 'npm trusted publishers OIDC' would have found the npm version bug immediately, saving significant iteration time.\n  [success] @01KFHJP6 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    PR automated review catching AC test coverage gaps - the Claude reviewer on PR #141 identified two missing test assertions (--clear-deps and --automation author validation) that I'd missed. The strict AC coverage requirement in CLAUDE.md combined with automated review provides good safety net.\n  [success] @01KFGRVJ (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Spec-first with traits: Creating spec, adding traits, then deriving task immediately surfaced what tests were needed (trait ACs). Applied to --clear-deps feature.\n  [success] @01KFG1JN (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Task @extract-test-helpers was already completed before being picked up. The workflow correctly handled this by verifying implementation (tests/helpers/cli.ts exists with all helpers, no duplicates in test files, 841 tests pass) and documenting findings in task notes before marking complete. This demonstrates good verification practices when tasks may have been completed out of band.\n  [success] @01KFG1CX (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Task reset command workflow: Reset cancelled task to pending, then start/submit/complete with proper documentation. Fixed @task-yaml-formatting status efficiently using task reset flow.\n  [success] @01KFG13F (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review and merge workflow is efficient - spawned parallel review subagents for both PRs, got clear recommendations, merged both immediately, completed tasks, pulled changes, and started new work. The review agents provided comprehensive analysis including local test verification and CI status context.\n  [success] @01KFG0GW (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Vitest v1 to v4 major version upgrade required only minimal changes: single async/await fix in test file. Breaking changes were well-contained. The vitest v4 warning message ('Promise returned by expect(actual).rejects.toThrow(expected) was not awaited') was clear and pointed directly to the issue, making the fix trivial. Major version upgrades don't always require extensive refactoring if the codebase follows best practices.\n  [success] @01KFFZSM (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Tech debt cleanup pattern: When finding duplicate logic, check if the duplication serves a purpose (e.g., specialized behavior) or is truly redundant. In this case, inbox.ts had a local shortUlid() helper that was functionally identical to .slice(0,8) used elsewhere. The 'smart' ReferenceIndex.shortUlid() method wasn't needed for ephemeral inbox items without slugs. Solution: remove helper, use direct string slicing consistently. This makes code more maintainable and easier to understand.\n  [success] @01KFFZG3 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop PR review workflow successfully prevented corrupted code from being merged. The iteration followed RALPH_LOOP_INSTRUCTIONS.md: (1) Checked for open PRs, (2) Spawned review subagent, (3) Fixed blocking issues found, (4) Re-verified tests locally, (5) Merged with --merge to preserve trailers, (6) Completed task with proper documentation. This systematic approach caught and fixed a critical issue that manual review might have missed.\n  [success] @01KFFZFH (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Interactive rebase with automated editor worked well for fixing historical commits. Using GIT_EDITOR with sed to automatically mark commits for editing eliminated manual intervention. Pattern: GIT_EDITOR='sed -i \"s/^pick <hash>/edit <hash>/\"' git rebase -i <base>. This allowed scripted fixes to commit history while maintaining full control.\n  [success] @01KFFZFC (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review subagent workflow is effective. Spawning a dedicated subagent with strict review criteria caught the critical file corruption issue before merge. The subagent provided detailed analysis including: blocking issues prioritized, test coverage gaps identified, code quality assessment, and specific fix commands. This caught a problem that would have been difficult to spot in manual review.\n  [friction] @01KFFZF7 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Git handling of files with newlines in filenames is extremely difficult. Standard commands like 'git rm' with shell quoting don't work. Even null-terminated operations struggle with escaping. Solution: Python script with subprocess.run() successfully handled the special characters where shell commands failed. This type of git corruption should be prevented at commit time if possible.\n  [success] @01KFFZ0F (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Test helper API documentation was critical for fixing tests. The kspec() helper signature (string args, not array) wasn't obvious from test file alone. Reading tests/helpers/cli.ts revealed correct usage pattern. Future: consider adding JSDoc examples in helper file showing common patterns.\n  [success] @01KFFYC3 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    findChildItems already existed in items.ts with correct implementation - avoided duplicate code by discovering existing helper. Good pattern: search for existing helpers before implementing new ones.\n  [success] @01KFFXV3 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Test error message checking pattern: When commands fail, error messages can be in either stdout or stderr. Using 'const output = result.stdout + result.stderr' and checking that combined output is more robust than checking just stdout. This worked for all 4 initially failing tests.\n  [success] @01KFFXTY (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    KSPEC_TEST_TTY pattern works well for testing interactive prompts. By adding an env var check alongside process.stdin.isTTY, we can simulate TTY in tests while preserving real TTY detection in production. This pattern can be reused for other interactive commands.\n  [success] @01KFFX1B (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Iteration 16: Successfully completed full PR workflow - reviewed and merged PR #122 (task set batch), blocked unclear task @01KFBDR7 (Result types), completed and shipped @01KFBJFE (dedupe formatRelativeTime) via PR #123. Pattern: PR review -> merge -> pick new task -> implement -> commit -> PR. Task selection improved: skipped manual_only task, blocked ambiguous task, picked clear tech-debt task. Total: 2 PRs processed (1 merged, 1 created).\n  [friction] @01KFFWV2 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Task @01KFBDR7 'Improve Result type enforcement' has unclear scope. Initial investigation shows current Result pattern (discriminated unions with 'ok' field) is well-implemented and consistently used. Most code properly checks .ok before accessing success fields. Task note suggests multiple improvements (branded types, lint rules, never throw library) but unclear which is actually needed or would provide value. Without concrete examples of bugs or near-misses from this pattern, hard to prioritize improvements. Recommendation: Block task pending concrete evidence of problems, or pivot to documentation task.\n  [success] @01KFFWR2 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop PR review workflow successful: spawned review subagent for pending_review task, agent performed comprehensive AC coverage check (13 ACs validated), test quality assessment (E2E strategy, isolation), and edge case verification. Review found no issues. Waited for CI, then merged with --merge flag to preserve trailers. Pattern: pending_review task -> spawn review agent -> wait for CI green -> merge -> complete task. This workflow ensures systematic quality checks without human intervention.\n  [success] @01KFFWAJ (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Test-first implementation worked well for task set batch. Writing all 15 tests first (based on spec ACs) provided clear targets and caught issues early. The 'write tests → implement → fix → repeat' cycle was faster than trying to implement and test simultaneously.\n  [success] @01KFFW9Q (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Batch operation pattern in batch.ts worked well for task set implementation. executeBatchOperation() handled all the complexity of partial failures, ref deduplication, and output formatting. Helper function extraction (setTaskFields) allowed clean code reuse between single and batch modes.\n  [success] @01KFFVG0 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Stop hook successfully caught incomplete session cleanup. Ralph loop should always checkpoint in-progress tasks before ending iteration, even if not completing them. The hook reminder ensures continuity for next session.\n  [success] @01KFFVEK (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop iteration 14: Successfully merged PR #121 (JSON output consistency), researched drift detection task (found it under-specified), and started well-defined task-set-batch task. Pattern: When a ready task lacks concrete examples or clear scope, checkpoint it with observations and move to a better-defined task rather than spinning on requirements gathering.\n  [friction] @01KFFVC7 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Task @01KFBDQE 'Add spec-schema drift detection' needs clearer scoping. The original note mentions three issues but #2 appears to be a misunderstanding (already handled by --staleness check), and #3 (fixture conflicts) lacks concrete examples. Tasks derived from retrospective observations should include specific examples or reproduction steps, not just abstract descriptions of problems.\n  [success] @01KFFTSZ (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop iteration 13 completed successfully: Reviewed and merged PR #120, then completed task @01KFBDM8 (JSON output consistency audit). The PR review workflow worked smoothly with automated CI checks. The audit task was well-scoped with clear notes about what to investigate. Created PR #121 for the fix. Key efficiency: Using TodoWrite to track sub-tasks within the larger audit task helped maintain focus.\n  [success] @01KFFTRJ (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    JSON output audit revealed mostly good patterns - only one inconsistency found (clone-for-testing had its own --json flag). The systematic approach of searching for JSON.stringify, checking stderr handling, and documenting envelope format conventions was effective. Key insight: envelope format variation is intentional and serves different use cases (raw data vs success confirmations vs errors vs hook-specific formats).\n  [success] @01KFFT48 (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    PR creation workflow smooth: auto-generated branch name from task slug, created branch, reset main, pushed, created PR with comprehensive summary including AC coverage checklist. The /pr skill handled the complexity well.\n  [success] @01KFFT1J (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Test helper pattern (kspecWithStatus vs kspecError) worked well once I found the right function. The cli.ts helper exports are well-structured for different test needs.\n  [success] @01KFFT1E (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    State enforcement implementation went smoothly - clear spec with detailed ACs made implementation straightforward. Breaking existing tests was expected and fixing them reinforced the new workflow.\n  [success] @01KFFSGA (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Spec status audit revealed that implementation often outpaces spec updates. ULID shortening, cascade updates, and YAML conventions were all implemented but marked not_started. The cascade feature in item set updated 18 child items automatically, demonstrating the value of hierarchical status propagation. Shadow branch separation meant no main branch PR was needed for metadata updates.\n  [success] @01KFFS6V (1/21/2026 by @claude)\n    Successfully completed full PR review cycle in single iteration: reviewed 2 PRs in parallel, merged 1 immediately (PR #118), fixed failing test in other (PR #119), waited for CI to confirm fix, then merged. Both tasks (@01KFBPQA, @01KFC3AR) completed. The --watch flag on gh pr checks was useful for waiting on CI completion.\n  [success] @01KFFS1P (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    RALPH_LOOP_INSTRUCTIONS.md PR review workflow is well-designed: check for PRs first, spawn review subagents, use decision tree (pass→merge, fail→fix). Following this process caught issues before merge and ensured quality. The --merge flag (not --squash) preserves kspec trailers which is critical for kspec log @ref functionality.\n  [friction] @01KFFS1G (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Shell escaping in tests is tricky across environments. The $'...' syntax worked locally but failed in CI. Using printf with double-escaped newlines ($(printf 'Line 1\\\\nLine 2\\\\nLine 3')) is more portable and explicit. Tests should prefer printf over $'...' for multiline string handling in bash commands.\n  [success] @01KFFS1A (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review subagents worked excellently in parallel. Spawned two review agents simultaneously - both produced comprehensive, strict reviews identifying issues (PR #119 had failing test, PR #118 was clean). The parallel approach saved time and both reviews were thorough with proper AC coverage validation.\n  [success] @01KFFRDE (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Iteration 8 complete - accomplished multiple tasks:\n\n1. Reviewed PR #118 (log empty repo fix) - comprehensive review passed\n2. Implemented @01KFC3AR (--description flag) with full AC coverage\n3. Created PR #119 for --description implementation\n4. Fixed failing CI test in PR #118 (missing git config)\n5. Captured observations (success + friction patterns)\n\nKey patterns:\n- PR review workflow with subagent worked well\n- Implementation -> tests -> PR flow is smooth\n- CI failures often reveal env config issues (git user.name/email)\n- Test patterns: always configure git after init in temp dirs\n\nBoth PRs now ready for merge once CI completes.\n  [friction] @01KFFRD1 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    CI test failures can occur when tests don't configure git user.name/email before running git commit in temp dirs. Pattern to follow: Always add 'git config user.email/user.name' commands after 'git init' in tests that create fresh repos and commit. Found in PR #118 - test passed locally but failed in CI.\n  [success] @01KFFR8H (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Iteration 8: Successfully implemented @01KFC3AR (--description flag for task add) from start to PR creation. Pattern that worked well:\n1. Read spec to understand all ACs\n2. Check existing schema (description field already existed)\n3. Add CLI option and wire to TaskInput\n4. Write comprehensive tests FIRST with AC annotations\n5. Fix shell escaping issues in tests (not implementation bugs)\n6. All 792 tests pass on first implementation try\n7. Created PR with full context\n\nTotal time: ~15 minutes for feature + tests + PR. Clean handoff to next iteration.\n  [success] @01KFFQSB (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    PR creation workflow smooth - auto-generated branch name from task slug, moved commit from main to feature branch, created comprehensive PR body from task/spec context. Task notes provided all context needed for PR summary.\n  [success] @01KFFQQA (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Test fixture management pattern: Using setupTempFixtures() + removing .git + git init creates clean test environment with kspec structure but no commits. More reliable than manually constructing YAML. Pattern used successfully in AC-2 and AC-5 tests.\n  [success] @01KFFPY6 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Skipped test was actually ready to run - validation infrastructure improvements had resolved the original issue. Worth periodically reviewing skipped tests to see if they can be unskipped. Test passed immediately when .skip was removed.\n  [success] @01KFFPY1 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review workflow working well: Claude review posted actionable feedback (null check for comment.author), fix was straightforward (optional chaining), all checks passed after fix, and merge proceeded smoothly. The automated review catch prevented a potential production bug.\n  [success] @01KFFPWF (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop PR workflow is working well: started iteration by checking for open PRs, reviewed and merged PR #115 (fixing spec trailers), then picked next priority task. PR review → merge → task selection creates natural workflow cadence.\n  [success] @01KFFPWA (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    CI workflow implementation was already partially complete from previous session - checking git status and existing files before starting work prevented duplicate effort. The workflow uses GitHub GraphQL API to query review thread resolution status, which is more robust than REST API approach for checking unresolved comments.\n  [success] @01KFFP5D (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    CI workflow implementation pattern: GitHub Actions with github-script action provides clean GraphQL API integration without custom tooling. For review thread resolution checks, the isResolved and isOutdated fields are exactly what's needed - filtering for \\!isResolved && \\!isOutdated gives accurate results. Excluding outdated threads prevents false positives from force-pushed commits.\n  [success] @01KFFN83 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Test improvement strategy: AC-5 test was fluff (admitted in comments it didn't test the scenario). Rewrote to actually set up dependency scenario and validate via tasks ready command. Understanding how dependency checking works (at query time, not start time) was key to writing correct test.\n  [success] @01KFFN7T (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review workflow validated: spawned subagent for strict review before merge, identified blocking issues (spec field name mismatch, fluff test), fixed them, then merged. Review agent correctly flagged AC-5 test as not validating its criterion.\n  [success] @01KFFMS3 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    PR CI waiting pattern - when CI is pending on a PR, better to move to next task rather than blocking iteration. The PR will be picked up in a future iteration once CI completes.\n  [success] @01KFFMRZ (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Task scoping from retrospective notes works well - the task notes from the ralph loop retrospective provided clear, actionable improvements that translated directly into implementation steps.\n  [success] @01KFFM7N (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Task reset implementation went smoothly. Following existing patterns from task.ts (cancel, complete, block) made implementation straightforward. Comprehensive test coverage (15 tests for 6 ACs) caught no issues. Shadow commit verification simplified by trusting commitIfShadow success rather than parsing git internals. All 783 tests pass on first try.\n  [success] @01KFEZ86 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Spec-plan session validated guided workflow design - the manual process of creating the guided-workflow-execution spec followed exactly the pattern the feature would automate: requirements → exploration → design → review → spec creation → derive. Dogfooding opportunity once implemented.\n  [success] @01KFEYT8 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Breaking monolithic feature into requirements before deriving tasks produces self-contained tasks with proper dependencies. One feature with 14 ACs became 4 requirements with 4-6 ACs each.\n  [success] @01KFEYT3 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Subagent review of specs before implementation caught 4 missing ACs and 2 missing flag behaviors. Worth doing for non-trivial specs.\n  [success] @01KFE1BE (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Fixed spec-implementation mismatch by updating the spec to match working implementation. Key insight: When spec and implementation diverge, evaluate which is more useful before blindly 'fixing' code to match spec. In this case, task dependency checking was more actionable than spec hierarchy checking, so updating the spec was the right choice. The tests validated that the implementation was correct for what it does.\n  [success] @01KFE13K (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Spec review task series completed efficiently. Breaking down large spec updates into 7 focused tasks (trait creation, AC addition, trait application by command category) enabled systematic progress tracking and validation at each step. TodoWrite tool helped track progress within each task.\n  [success] @01KFE0RJ (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Shadow branch auto-commit workflow: File changes made via Edit tool are automatically committed when kspec commands (like task note) run. No manual git add/commit needed for shadow-tracked files. This is the expected behavior and works well.\n  [success] @01KFE0EM (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Trait application pattern for CLI commands: (1) All commands get json-output, semantic-exit-codes, error-guidance. (2) Batch operations add multi-ref-batch. (3) Destructive operations add confirmation-prompt. Applied consistently to 7 task lifecycle commands. Pattern is clear and repeatable for remaining commands.\n  [success] @01KFE0E7 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Shadow branch auto-commit pattern works seamlessly for spec changes. Modified cli.yaml (spec file) and all changes were automatically committed to shadow branch via kspec commands (task note, task complete). No manual git operations needed. This confirms the design - spec changes live in shadow branch, auto-committed by CLI.\n  [success] @01KFE03D (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Systematic AC addition workflow worked efficiently: grouped items by category (schema types, task types, relationships, YAML), added 2 AC per item following given/when/then format. Pattern: each AC should test a specific aspect of the item's behavior. For type definitions, one AC validates structure and one validates usage/purpose.\n  [success] @01KFDZVT (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Debugging approach for YAML loading issues:\n1. Used node scripts to test individual components (initContext, extractItemsFromRaw, SpecItemSchema)\n2. Isolated the failure point (ULID validation regex)\n3. This incremental debugging approach was much faster than adding console.logs to source code\n\nPattern to remember: when kspec commands don't work as expected, test the parser/schema layers directly with minimal scripts.\n  [friction] @01KFDZVK (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Manually editing .kspec/kynetic.yaml to add traits requires:\n1. Understanding ULID format constraints (no I, L, O, U chars)\n2. Manually generating valid ULIDs\n3. Working around shadow branch auto-commit restrictions\n\nThe kspec trait add command would have handled all this, but for bulk creation of 8 traits with detailed AC, manual YAML editing was more practical. Future improvement: support adding AC during trait creation, or bulk trait import from YAML file.\n  [success] @01KFDZGT (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    E2E validation workflow effective for confirming system readiness. Created test data, verified behavior, cleaned up - all within single iteration. Trait system confirmed production-ready without needing to create permanent test fixtures.\n  [success] @01KFDZBX (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Completed first task in spec review sequence (@01KFDYAW). Simple cleanup task (removing non-existent file reference) was straightforward. Shadow branch auto-commit worked correctly - no manual commit needed for .kspec/ changes.\n  [success] @01KFDPM7 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph Loop iteration completed successfully: (1) Checked for open PRs per instructions (2) Found PR #106 with failing tests (7/8) (3) Launched review subagent to identify issues (4) Fixed all 3 blocking issues (worktree metadata cleanup, test setup, helper usage) (5) Verified locally (8/8 tests + full suite 707 passing) (6) Pushed and waited for CI (all green) (7) Merged PR and completed task @01KFBBTT (8) Captured 4 observations. Full workflow from PR review to merge completion in single iteration.\n  [friction] @01KFDPKZ (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Shadow branch worktree gets removed when checking out branches. After merging PR and checking out main, .kspec/ worktree was removed. Attempting to recreate with 'git worktree add .kspec kspec-meta' created nested .kspec/.kspec/ worktree breaking kspec. Fix required absolute paths and manual cleanup. Root cause: git worktree add interprets paths relative to CWD which may be inside .kspec. Consider: (1) kspec commands that detect missing worktree and auto-repair (2) fix spawn error in 'kspec shadow repair' (3) prevent nested worktree creation.\n  [success] @01KFD2YY (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Systematic debugging approach worked well: (1) Verified git clone works standalone, (2) Added debug output to trace actual parameters, (3) Discovered output was empty not error, (4) Checked test helper return types, (5) Found pattern in working tests. Each step narrowed the problem space until root cause was clear.\n  [friction] @01KFD2YS (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Optional positional parameter order in CLI commands can create usability issues. [source] [dest] signature made it impossible to provide dest and default source. Changed to [dest] [source] which better matches common usage patterns. Consider: use named options (--source, --dest) for commands where both parameters are truly optional and order-independent.\n  [success] @01KFD2YK (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Test helper pattern clarity - integration.test.ts showed the correct pattern of importing kspecOutput as kspec for string output. When tests fail due to type mismatches (expecting string, getting object), check imports against working test files.\n  [success] @01KFD1TC (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Bug investigation pattern: When validation passes unexpectedly, check item counts in output. 'Items checked: 3' vs expected 5 revealed traits weren't being loaded. Count mismatches are powerful debugging signals for data loading issues.\n  [success] @01KFD0VX (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Merged PR #104 (trait CLI commands) successfully. Review subagent found 0 blocking issues, 100% AC coverage (8/8), all tests passing. Production-quality implementation with proper isolation and AC annotations.\n  [success] @01KFD0DW (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review feedback loop worked well: (1) Subagent identified test quality issues with clear MUST-FIX verdict, (2) Systematic debugging traced root causes (YAML structure, slug conflicts, missing deps), (3) Fixed all 12 test failures, (4) All 695 tests pass locally, (5) PR updated and CI re-running. The strict review criteria (E2E tests, temp dirs, AC coverage, no fluff) ensured high quality implementation.\n  [success] @01KFD0A8 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Systematic test debugging approach worked well: (1) Run tests to see failures, (2) Isolate root cause through manual reproduction, (3) Fix fixtures to match actual kspec requirements, (4) Verify all tests pass. Using manual CLI invocations in temp dirs helped identify structural issues that weren't obvious from test code alone.\n  [success] @01KFCZ4S (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Used dedicated review subagent to identify critical schema bug in PR #104. The subagent's strict review caught that ManifestSchema was missing the traits field, causing Zod to strip traits during parsing. This was a blocking bug that made all functionality fail. Fixed the issue and verified through extensive manual testing. Pattern: Always use review subagents before merge, they catch bugs that aren't obvious from code inspection alone.\n  [success] @01KFCYH1 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Trait CLI implementation followed clean separation: parser infrastructure (TYPE_TO_CHILD_FIELD, NESTED_ITEM_FIELDS) before CLI commands. This pattern worked well - infrastructure changes enabled CLI to use existing patterns (addChildItem, buildIndexes) without special cases. E2E tests caught integration issues early (traits not loading from manifest).\n  [friction] @01KFCXYZ (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    CI checks can take 3-4 minutes to complete, which can consume a significant portion of an iteration when waiting for merge approval. Consider: (1) Starting CI early if possible, (2) Using wait time productively for reflection/documentation, (3) Maybe implementing a timeout strategy in Ralph Loop for when CI takes too long.\n  [success] @01KFCXY2 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Full PR review → fix → merge cycle completed successfully in one iteration. The Ralph Loop workflow of dedicating review subagent, fixing issues, waiting for CI, and merging is working smoothly. Key: strict review criteria and blocking on CI checks before merge.\n  [success] @01KFCXQH (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review workflow caught test quality issues before merge - the automated subagent review identified fluff tests and missing documentation that would have degraded test quality patterns in the codebase. The strict review criteria (MUST-FIX vs suggestions) worked well.\n  [success] @01KFCXGB (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Trait AC coverage validation implementation went smoothly by building on existing infrastructure. The TraitIndex and checkCompleteness() architecture made it straightforward to add inherited AC checking - just scan trait ACs the same way we scan direct ACs. The existing test coverage scanning and ref validation already handled most of the heavy lifting. Clean separation of concerns paid off.\n  [success] @01KFCX5D (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Systematic debugging approach: When implementation code looks correct but tests fail, investigate test infrastructure first. Created isolated debug scripts to replicate exact test scenario, which revealed YAML parser only reading first document. This pattern of creating minimal reproduction scripts in scratchpad is valuable for debugging test failures.\n  [success] @01KFCVFM (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Trait display implementation completed efficiently by reusing TraitIndex foundation. Integrating into buildIndexes() made trait data available throughout CLI. Text and JSON output handled cleanly with separate formatting logic. Test fixtures required valid ULIDs - good validation caught this early.\n  [success] @01KFCTR3 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Following established code patterns (ReferenceIndex, AlignmentIndex) made TraitIndex implementation straightforward. The bidirectional mapping pattern and constructor-based indexing approach worked well for trait-spec relationships. Test structure with AC annotations provides clear traceability.\n  [success] @01KFCTE1 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop PR review workflow proved effective: dedicated review subagent correctly identified missing E2E tests as BLOCKING issue, prompted fix, re-review confirmed resolution, and merge completed successfully. The strict review criteria (AC coverage, E2E priority, test isolation) caught important gaps before merge.\n  [success] @01KFCSQF (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Traits field validation implementation followed clean pattern from existing ref validation. Adding to REF_FIELDS array and extending validateRefs() was straightforward. The test pattern from parser.test.ts made writing comprehensive tests easy. All 5 ACs covered in 6 tests.\n  [success] @01KFCS35 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Test-first debugging workflow effective: Added debug output to failing tests to identify root cause (invalid ULID format), then fixed and removed debug code. Faster than guessing.\n  [success] @01KFCR87 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    YAML migration task was straightforward - clear scope, no dependencies, simple find-replace pattern. This type of refactoring task is ideal for automation: well-defined changes, easy to verify with tests, low risk.\n  [success] @01KFCQZK (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review workflow with dedicated subagent worked well - caught missing test coverage before merge. Strict review guidelines (AC coverage required, no fluff tests, E2E preferred) ensure quality standards are maintained across iterations.\n  [success] @01KFCQ0S (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    PR workflow (#96 → #97) demonstrates smooth iteration loop: review existing PR → merge → pick next task → implement → submit → create PR. The pending_review state effectively gates work and ensures each PR gets proper review before merge. Task @01KFC1GKG scope was well-defined (foundation only) which made it easy to complete in one iteration.\n  [success] @01KFCPF1 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Exit code fixes completed efficiently using TodoWrite to track all 6 individual changes. The todo list helped ensure no fixes were missed and provided clear progress visibility through the task.\n  [success] @01KFC3C2 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Task notes template for standalone implementation context worked well: Scope (what this task covers), Prerequisites (dependencies), Files (to modify/create), Patterns (code examples), Tests (where to add). Prevents 'see parent for context' band-aids.\n  [success] @01KFC3BV (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Subagent review of spec caught 3 blocking issues (AC collision, undefined JSON structure, display contradiction) that would have caused implementation problems. Pattern: review specs with critical eye before finalizing, not just for completeness but for internal consistency and edge cases.\n  [friction] @01KFC3BM (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Plan mode required multiple back-and-forth cycles. Missing upfront: output type (spec vs code), standalone requirements check, display/UX needs. Review subagent ran AFTER trying to exit plan mode instead of as a step before finalizing. Suggests plan mode needs structured phases or checklist.\n\n--- Session Metadata ---\n  Last updated: just now\n\n--- Quick Commands ---\n  kspec task start @01KHF2BK  # begin work\n  kspec triage inbox  # triage untriaged inbox items\n\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":4,"nanos":110031053},"formatted_output":"Total output lines: 1058\n\nℹ Synced shadow branch from remote\n\n=== Session Context ===\nBranch: main | Generated: just now\nTasks: 0 active, 35 ready, 0 blocked, 738/797 completed | Inbox: 154\n\n--- Ready to Pick Up ---\n  P2 @01KHF2BK Revisit ralph wrap-up agent design and test coverage\n  P2 @01KHF2BN Create spec for validate --drift feature\n  P2 @01KHF34K Add code-level reflect gating in ralph based on iteration delta\n  P2 @01KHVMWP Write getting-started tutorial and README for external users\n  P2 @task-redesign-web-ui Redesign web UI holistically — interactive spec/design session #web-ui #design #spike\n  P2 @task-reconcile-validate-alignment-warning-baseline Reconcile validate alignment warning baseline (status mismatches + orphans) #validation #alignment #meta\n  P2 @task-triage-automation-eligible-missing-spec-ref Triage automation-eligible task