ralph to agent runtime #docs #migration\n  P3 @01KJS8BD Replace 06-ralph-loop.md template with agent dispatch documentation #docs #agent #templates\n  P3 @01KJS8BE Migrate ralph tests to agent runtime equivalents #testing #agent #ralph\n  P3 @01KJS8BF Update workflows to remove ralph references and use agent dispatch concepts #docs #workflow #ralph\n  P3 @01KJS8BG Update skills to reference agent runtime instead of ralph #docs #skills #ralph\n  P3 @01KJTHA4 Fix AC-10 test flakiness in cli-agent-commands #cli #testing #bug\n  P3 @01KJTHAB Fix core-skill-install test flakiness in shard 3 #testing #bug\n  P3 @01KJTHAC Fix cli-serve test flakiness in shard 3 #testing #bug #cli\n  P3 @01KJTP68 Add isolated HOME helper for daemon-sensitive CLI tests #test #daemon #reflection\n  P3 @01KJTP69 Create wait-for-startup helper for process/daemon tests #test #daemon #reflection\n\n--- Recent Activity ---\n  ✓ @01KJS8B9 Add dispatch rule flags to kspec meta add agent / meta set (just now)\n    ├─ be840eb feat(meta): add agent dispatch-rule CLI flags (11 minutes ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ 7d94099 feat(meta): add agent dispatch-rule CLI flags (#669) (just now, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS7PD Add --text flag to kspec session log show to replay agent output (26 minutes ago)\n    ├─ b20e5ad feat(session-log): add --text replay for session log show (1 hour ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ 74b5e76 feat: add --text replay mode to session log show (#668) (26 minutes ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS702 CLI DX: accept tags as alias for tag in batch commands (1 hour ago)\n    ├─ c56b6f8 feat(batch): accept tags alias in batch args (1 hour ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ bfb55d1 feat(batch): accept tags alias for tag in batch commands (#667) (1 hour ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS6ZZ CLI DX: accept P1/P2/P3 format as alias for numeric priority in batch commands (1 hour ago)\n    ├─ 14af23c feat(batch): WIP accept P* priority aliases in batch (3 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    ├─ 1af5278 test(batch): assert priority alias results via task list (2 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ d561c38 feat(batch): accept P1/P2/P3 priority aliases in batch (#666) (1 hour ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS6ZP Handle EPIPE in ACP framing layer — src/acp/framing.ts send() throws uncaught EPIPE when child proce (2 hours ago)\n    ├─ f05177c fix(acp): checkpoint EPIPE handling work (5 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ 6607641 fix(acp): handle EPIPE in JSON-RPC framing output path (#665) (2 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS6ZX CLI DX: add --description flag to task set command (currently requires task patch --data) (2 hours ago)\n    ├─ 00fed9c feat(task): add --description support to task set (3 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ d0a95d0 feat(task): add --description support to task set (#661) (2 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS6ZW kspec batch --commands '' hangs instead of erroring — empty/missing args should fail fast with a cle (2 hours ago)\n    └─ bbce145 fix(batch): fail fast when batch stdin input is missing (#660) (2 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS701 CLI DX: accept spec_ref as alias for spec-ref in batch commands (2 hours ago) - No implementation delta required. Verified on 2026-03-03 that batch args accept spec_ref alias for --spec-ref in task ad...\n  ✓ @task-dispatch-in-progress-priority Implement in_progress-first dispatch scheduling (3 hours ago)\n    ├─ cb67865 feat(dispatch): prioritize in-progress task dispatch (3 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    ├─ f1ad9c3 fix(dispatch): keep needs_work ahead of pending (3 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n    └─ d80157c Implement in_progress-first dispatch scheduling (#663) (3 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ○ eab566a fix(agent): harden dispatch autonomy and prompt timeout handling (#662) (3 hours ago, Jacob Chapel)\n  ✓ @01KJS6ZT kspec validate --json appends human-readable summary text after JSON payload, causing invalid JSON f (3 hours ago) - Merged in PR #659. Implemented structured-output purity fix for validate/lint so --json/--yaml no longer appends human-r...\n\n--- Inbox (oldest first) ---\n  57 untriaged | 67 deferred | 154 total\n\n  @01KJDZBA (5 days ago by @claude) [testing, ralph, infra]\n    Add second mock adapter registration for ralph tests. Currently --adapter-cmd registers a single 'custom' adapter, making it impossible to test different adapter IDs in real (non-dry-run) runs. Options: (1) --worker-adapter-cmd / --reviewer-adapter-cmd flags, (2) env-based adapter registration (e.g. KSPEC_TEST_ADAPTERS JSON), (3) allow --adapter-cmd to accept an optional name prefix like --adapter-cmd 'worker:node mock.js'. This would enable proper integration tests for ac-2 (reviewer subagent uses reviewer adapter), ac-7 (env injection for both adapters with different harness targets), and ac-12 (different adapter IDs in session metadata). Currently these are covered by code-level AC annotations and dry-run output tests, but lack end-to-end verification with the mock ACP.\n  @01KJF69V (5 days ago by @claude) [cli, validation]\n    kspec validate should warn about items with identical titles in the same module — two items both named 'Generation Progress UX' with different ULIDs caused confusion during a fix session in imgen project\n  @01KJG5A7 (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [bug, ralph, codex, sessions]\n    Task budget false-positive outside ralph loop when stale KSPEC_SESSION_ID is present (observed in codex session). Repro: with KSPEC_SESSION_ID set to an old session that has budget.json started_this_cycle=max_per_cycle, 'kspec task start @ref' fails with 'Task budget exhausted ... let iteration end naturally' even when not running ralph and no in-progress tasks. Expected: budget guard should apply only during active ralph loop iteration context, or stale/non-ralph session IDs should be ignored.\n  @01KJGA18 (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [bug, infra, skills, setup]\n    Diagnose skill verify drift/no-hash for plugin-provided core skills.\n\nObserved behavior:\n- kspec skill verify reports codex statuses as drifted/no-hash for help, observations, triage, reflect, triage-inbox, writing-specs.\n- kspec skill diff reports plugin-provided (no local render to diff).\n- Rendered skill files exist under .agents/skills.\n\nInvestigate:\n- render-hash semantics in .kspec/skills for plugin-provided skills\n- plugin-provided status handling in skill verify/status/diff paths\n- whether this is expected behavior or a bug in drift detection\n  @01KJGAE2 (4 days ago by @claude) [plan-import, automation, friction]\n    derive_from_specs produces sparse tasks: derived tasks get null description, no tags, automation unassessed. Agents picking up these tasks lack context to work independently. Consider: auto-populate description from spec description, copy plan tags, or allow plan document to specify per-task fields.\n  @01KJGEEQ0 (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, cli, spec]\n    Reflection follow-up: add explicit parent/ancestor context to kspec item get output (human and --json) so hierarchy is unambiguous without separate tree queries.\n  @01KJGEEQ21 (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, cli, ux]\n    Reflection follow-up: add optional ancestor-chain display for kspec item list --tree --search so matched descendants show parent context and avoid false orphan interpretation.\n  @01KJGEEQ2Y (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, validation, traits]\n    Reflection follow-up: add validation check for trait/command-contract contradictions (e.g., command AC prohibits --refs while applied traits require batch refs semantics).\n  @01KJGFKJ (4 days ago by @claude) [cli, ux]\n    Unified kspec get @ref dispatch: currently plans require 'kspec plan get' while specs/tasks use 'kspec item get' / 'kspec task get'. A single 'kspec get @ref' that auto-dispatches to the right entity type would reduce friction for agents and humans who don't know the entity type upfront.\n  @01KJGW8Z (4 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, testing, cli]\n    Add stale-dist guard for CLI-based tests: detect outdated dist/cli build in tests/helpers/cli.ts and fail fast with actionable guidance (or optional auto-build) to avoid misleading failures.\n  @01KJHAFV (4 days ago by @claude) [reflection, cli, workflow]\n    Meta workflow editing gap: kspec meta set cannot update workflow steps. Agents must delete+recreate workflow to edit a step, which can churn workflow ULIDs and temporarily break references. Add safe step-level update support (e.g., --set-step <index> --content/--on-fail) or preserve ULID on replace to avoid reference drift.\n  @01KJJHAR (3 days ago by @claude) [infra, cli, reflection]\n    Worktree guard escape hatch: add env var KSPEC_GUARD_OVERRIDE_DONT_USE that bypasses the guard hook for intentional recovery operations (git reset, rebase in .kspec). Name deliberately long to discourage agent use — humans-only for recovery scenarios.\n  @01KJK3YZ (3 days ago by @claude) [reflection, git, automation]\n    Add commit-scope guard for automated task work: before committing, detect pre-existing staged files unrelated to the current task and prevent accidental inclusion (or require explicit acknowledgment). In this iteration, unrelated staged files were included because they were already in the index before git add on task files.\n  @01KJK5XG (3 days ago by @claude) [reflection, testing, ralph]\n    tests/ralph.test.ts helper runRalph splits args on whitespace, so quoted values (e.g., --focus \"keep scope narrow\") break and cause misleading unknown-command/option failures. Improve helper to accept argv arrays or robust shell-like parsing for test args.\n  @01KJK84Y (3 days ago by @claude) [reflection, cli, tasks]\n    Reflection: fix-cycle ergonomics mismatch — task-work guidance says 're-submit' needs_work tasks, but CLI rejects kspec task submit when status is needs_work and requires explicit kspec task start first. Consider allowing direct submit from needs_work (auto-transition to in_progress) or improving error/help text to show required two-step command sequence.\n  @01KJKDBK (3 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, testing, sessions]\n    tests/cli/session-note-limit.test.ts has reproducible timeout instability in current baseline. During this iteration: full npm test timed out at lines 34/70/104, and isolated run timed out at lines 34/104 despite rebuilding. This appears separate from ralph prompt changes and indicates session-start note-limit path/perf regression or test flake that should be stabilized.\n  @01KJKKCB (3 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, cli, tasks]\n    Reflection: add file-based payload input to task notes (e.g., kspec task note @ref --file <path> and/or --stdin). Current command only accepts positional message, which makes multiline/special-character notes brittle and conflicts with the convention to use file-based payloads for safe shell handling.\n  @01KJKY1H (3 days ago by Jacob Chapel) [reflection, bug, batch, shadow]\n    Reflection follow-up: kspec batch can fail during copy-back with ENOTEMPTY on .kspec/sessions/<session-id> (observed while batching task start+note). The batch reports all subcommands succeeded, but exits non-zero and leaves shadow worktree in a temporarily broken/deleted-file state until manual recovery (e.g., git -C .kspec restore .). Improve batch copy-back robustness for session blob directories and avoid failing the batch when payload writes succeeded.\n  @01KJN378 (2 days ago by @claude) [bug, testing]\n    enhanced-setup.test.ts: 'should render core skills to codex output when codex is detected' fails — .codex/config.toml not created. Pre-existing failure (confirmed by stash test). Likely related to completed task @01KJEWKG — may need test update to match new codex config behavior.\n  @01KJN5MB (2 days ago by @claude) [reflection]\n    Fix pre-existing test failure: tests/enhanced-setup.test.ts 'should render core skills to codex output when codex is detected' fails with ENOENT on .codex/config.toml — test expects setup to create codex config but it is not being created. Confirmed pre-existing (not introduced by recent changes). Investigate whether codex config generation is missing or test fixture setup is incomplete.\n  Use: kspec triage inbox to triage untriaged items\n\n--- Observations (unresolved) ---\n  [friction] @01KJW0NZ (2 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Creating PRs with gh --body inline markdown containing backticks triggers shell interpolation; use --body-file for reliable automation.\n  [friction] @01KJVT2Q (3 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    CLI integration tests run against dist binary, so editing command options requires a full build before vitest; failures initially look like unknown-option regressions.\n  [success] @01KJVP3S (5 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Context-switch hygiene worked: checkpointing dirty work to a dedicated branch plus task-note handoff preserved progress and prevented losing work when switching priority tasks.\n  [friction] @01KJVB7XH (8 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Behavior-level AC intent can be missed when tests only verify internals. Add endpoint/log effect assertions earlier in task implementation to reduce fix-cycle churn.\n  [success] @01KJVB7XD (8 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Worker/reviewer loop with explicit MUST-FIX feedback improved quality: first review caught behavior-level AC gaps, second pass merged cleanly with clear audit trail.\n  [success] @01KJV1PFJ (11 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Task lifecycle loop (task-work + PR + spawned pr-review) provided clean end-to-end closure with merged PR and task completion in one pass.\n  [friction] @01KJV1PFE (11 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    PR review automation attempted APPROVE on self-authored PR and GitHub rejected it; review event selection should adapt to author/reviewer identity.\n  [success] @01KJTZYE (11 hours ago by Jacob Chapel)\n    Concurrency fix pattern: isolate race to a narrow atomic helper, migrate only hot mutation paths first, verify with targeted race regressions before full-suite run, then close via worker+review-agent loop.\n  [friction] @01KJTFVY (16 hours ago by @claude)\n    Testing CLI-layer decisions (e.g. isJsonMode() → onUpdate suppression) requires knowing which abstraction level to test at. Direct runInvocation unit tests give false positives; CLI subprocess tests hit adapter limitations; ESM spy approach is unreliable cross-module. The reliable pattern: use setJsonMode(true) + isJsonMode() from output.ts to replicate the exact CLI decision in-process, then assert on the result. Document this as the preferred pattern for testing CLI mode-gating behavior.\n  [success] @01KJSA6A (1 day ago by @claude)\n    Session log analysis for agent failure diagnosis: Python one-liner over events.jsonl extracted the exact tool call sequence and identified the ACP permission request gap in 2 minutes. The structured event log format made root cause analysis fast — tool_call status:pending -> 5min gap -> agent.failed was immediately diagnostic.\n  [friction] @01KJSA69X (1 day ago by @claude)\n    meta set has --add-skill but no --remove-skill. To fix a wrong skill slug on an agent, had to edit .kspec/kynetic.meta.yaml directly and trigger commit via task note. This is the second time a missing remove/clear flag forced a YAML edit (dispatch rules also have no CLI). These gaps accumulate quickly when wiring up new agents.\n  [friction] @01KJSA69R (1 day ago by @claude)\n    Skill slug namespace confusion: .agents/skills/ uses kspec- prefix (kspec-task-work) for rendered output, but .kspec/skills/ uses bare slug (task-work) for runtime injection. Agent definitions must use the bare slug — but nothing in the CLI or docs makes this distinction clear. Easy to wire up agents with wrong slug and get silent skill injection failure (skills silently skipped when file not found).\n  [friction] @01KJS6ESZ (1 day ago by @claude)\n    check-unresolved-comments CI check goes stale when review threads are resolved after the check already ran for that push. Fix: gh run rerun <run-id>. Reminder: resolve threads BEFORE or immediately after pushing to avoid this.\n  [friction] @01KJS6ESV (1 day ago by @claude)\n    Daemon packages/daemon/src/server.ts cannot be imported directly in vitest — transitive Bun/Elysia imports break module resolution. Pattern: recreate the logic as a pure function in the test file (see daemon-server.test.ts and daemon-web-ui-bundle.test.ts). Worth documenting as a testing convention for daemon code.\n  [friction] @01KJS6ESQ (1 day ago by @claude)\n    shx cp -r <src> <dest> is non-idempotent when dest already exists — creates nested subdirectory on repeated runs. Build scripts must always shx rm -rf <dest> before copying. Add to build/ci convention.\n  [success] @01KJRSMRE (1 day ago by @claude)\n    GraphQL thread resolution + empty commit pattern worked for stale CI: (1) kspec task submit → (2) resolve threads via GraphQL mutation → (3) push code or empty commit → (4) CI re-runs with threads resolved. Resolving threads before push avoids needing the empty commit.\n  [friction] @01KJRSMRA (1 day ago by @claude)\n    check-unresolved-comments CI check is triggered by push. If review threads are resolved AFTER a push (…33407 tokens truncated…est in PR #118 (missing git config)\n5. Captured observations (success + friction patterns)\n\nKey patterns:\n- PR review workflow with subagent worked well\n- Implementation -> tests -> PR flow is smooth\n- CI failures often reveal env config issues (git user.name/email)\n- Test patterns: always configure git after init in temp dirs\n\nBoth PRs now ready for merge once CI completes.\n  [friction] @01KFFRD1 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    CI test failures can occur when tests don't configure git user.name/email before running git commit in temp dirs. Pattern to follow: Always add 'git config user.email/user.name' commands after 'git init' in tests that create fresh repos and commit. Found in PR #118 - test passed locally but failed in CI.\n  [success] @01KFFR8H (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Iteration 8: Successfully implemented @01KFC3AR (--description flag for task add) from start to PR creation. Pattern that worked well:\n1. Read spec to understand all ACs\n2. Check existing schema (description field already existed)\n3. Add CLI option and wire to TaskInput\n4. Write comprehensive tests FIRST with AC annotations\n5. Fix shell escaping issues in tests (not implementation bugs)\n6. All 792 tests pass on first implementation try\n7. Created PR with full context\n\nTotal time: ~15 minutes for feature + tests + PR. Clean handoff to next iteration.\n  [success] @01KFFQSB (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    PR creation workflow smooth - auto-generated branch name from task slug, moved commit from main to feature branch, created comprehensive PR body from task/spec context. Task notes provided all context needed for PR summary.\n  [success] @01KFFQQA (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Test fixture management pattern: Using setupTempFixtures() + removing .git + git init creates clean test environment with kspec structure but no commits. More reliable than manually constructing YAML. Pattern used successfully in AC-2 and AC-5 tests.\n  [success] @01KFFPY6 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Skipped test was actually ready to run - validation infrastructure improvements had resolved the original issue. Worth periodically reviewing skipped tests to see if they can be unskipped. Test passed immediately when .skip was removed.\n  [success] @01KFFPY1 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review workflow working well: Claude review posted actionable feedback (null check for comment.author), fix was straightforward (optional chaining), all checks passed after fix, and merge proceeded smoothly. The automated review catch prevented a potential production bug.\n  [success] @01KFFPWF (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop PR workflow is working well: started iteration by checking for open PRs, reviewed and merged PR #115 (fixing spec trailers), then picked next priority task. PR review → merge → task selection creates natural workflow cadence.\n  [success] @01KFFPWA (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    CI workflow implementation was already partially complete from previous session - checking git status and existing files before starting work prevented duplicate effort. The workflow uses GitHub GraphQL API to query review thread resolution status, which is more robust than REST API approach for checking unresolved comments.\n  [success] @01KFFP5D (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    CI workflow implementation pattern: GitHub Actions with github-script action provides clean GraphQL API integration without custom tooling. For review thread resolution checks, the isResolved and isOutdated fields are exactly what's needed - filtering for \\!isResolved && \\!isOutdated gives accurate results. Excluding outdated threads prevents false positives from force-pushed commits.\n  [success] @01KFFN83 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Test improvement strategy: AC-5 test was fluff (admitted in comments it didn't test the scenario). Rewrote to actually set up dependency scenario and validate via tasks ready command. Understanding how dependency checking works (at query time, not start time) was key to writing correct test.\n  [success] @01KFFN7T (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review workflow validated: spawned subagent for strict review before merge, identified blocking issues (spec field name mismatch, fluff test), fixed them, then merged. Review agent correctly flagged AC-5 test as not validating its criterion.\n  [success] @01KFFMS3 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    PR CI waiting pattern - when CI is pending on a PR, better to move to next task rather than blocking iteration. The PR will be picked up in a future iteration once CI completes.\n  [success] @01KFFMRZ (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Task scoping from retrospective notes works well - the task notes from the ralph loop retrospective provided clear, actionable improvements that translated directly into implementation steps.\n  [success] @01KFFM7N (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Task reset implementation went smoothly. Following existing patterns from task.ts (cancel, complete, block) made implementation straightforward. Comprehensive test coverage (15 tests for 6 ACs) caught no issues. Shadow commit verification simplified by trusting commitIfShadow success rather than parsing git internals. All 783 tests pass on first try.\n  [success] @01KFEZ86 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Spec-plan session validated guided workflow design - the manual process of creating the guided-workflow-execution spec followed exactly the pattern the feature would automate: requirements → exploration → design → review → spec creation → derive. Dogfooding opportunity once implemented.\n  [success] @01KFEYT8 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Breaking monolithic feature into requirements before deriving tasks produces self-contained tasks with proper dependencies. One feature with 14 ACs became 4 requirements with 4-6 ACs each.\n  [success] @01KFEYT3 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Subagent review of specs before implementation caught 4 missing ACs and 2 missing flag behaviors. Worth doing for non-trivial specs.\n  [success] @01KFE1BE (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Fixed spec-implementation mismatch by updating the spec to match working implementation. Key insight: When spec and implementation diverge, evaluate which is more useful before blindly 'fixing' code to match spec. In this case, task dependency checking was more actionable than spec hierarchy checking, so updating the spec was the right choice. The tests validated that the implementation was correct for what it does.\n  [success] @01KFE13K (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Spec review task series completed efficiently. Breaking down large spec updates into 7 focused tasks (trait creation, AC addition, trait application by command category) enabled systematic progress tracking and validation at each step. TodoWrite tool helped track progress within each task.\n  [success] @01KFE0RJ (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Shadow branch auto-commit workflow: File changes made via Edit tool are automatically committed when kspec commands (like task note) run. No manual git add/commit needed for shadow-tracked files. This is the expected behavior and works well.\n  [success] @01KFE0EM (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Trait application pattern for CLI commands: (1) All commands get json-output, semantic-exit-codes, error-guidance. (2) Batch operations add multi-ref-batch. (3) Destructive operations add confirmation-prompt. Applied consistently to 7 task lifecycle commands. Pattern is clear and repeatable for remaining commands.\n  [success] @01KFE0E7 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Shadow branch auto-commit pattern works seamlessly for spec changes. Modified cli.yaml (spec file) and all changes were automatically committed to shadow branch via kspec commands (task note, task complete). No manual git operations needed. This confirms the design - spec changes live in shadow branch, auto-committed by CLI.\n  [success] @01KFE03D (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Systematic AC addition workflow worked efficiently: grouped items by category (schema types, task types, relationships, YAML), added 2 AC per item following given/when/then format. Pattern: each AC should test a specific aspect of the item's behavior. For type definitions, one AC validates structure and one validates usage/purpose.\n  [success] @01KFDZVT (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Debugging approach for YAML loading issues:\n1. Used node scripts to test individual components (initContext, extractItemsFromRaw, SpecItemSchema)\n2. Isolated the failure point (ULID validation regex)\n3. This incremental debugging approach was much faster than adding console.logs to source code\n\nPattern to remember: when kspec commands don't work as expected, test the parser/schema layers directly with minimal scripts.\n  [friction] @01KFDZVK (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Manually editing .kspec/kynetic.yaml to add traits requires:\n1. Understanding ULID format constraints (no I, L, O, U chars)\n2. Manually generating valid ULIDs\n3. Working around shadow branch auto-commit restrictions\n\nThe kspec trait add command would have handled all this, but for bulk creation of 8 traits with detailed AC, manual YAML editing was more practical. Future improvement: support adding AC during trait creation, or bulk trait import from YAML file.\n  [success] @01KFDZGT (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    E2E validation workflow effective for confirming system readiness. Created test data, verified behavior, cleaned up - all within single iteration. Trait system confirmed production-ready without needing to create permanent test fixtures.\n  [success] @01KFDZBX (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Completed first task in spec review sequence (@01KFDYAW). Simple cleanup task (removing non-existent file reference) was straightforward. Shadow branch auto-commit worked correctly - no manual commit needed for .kspec/ changes.\n  [success] @01KFDPM7 (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph Loop iteration completed successfully: (1) Checked for open PRs per instructions (2) Found PR #106 with failing tests (7/8) (3) Launched review subagent to identify issues (4) Fixed all 3 blocking issues (worktree metadata cleanup, test setup, helper usage) (5) Verified locally (8/8 tests + full suite 707 passing) (6) Pushed and waited for CI (all green) (7) Merged PR and completed task @01KFBBTT (8) Captured 4 observations. Full workflow from PR review to merge completion in single iteration.\n  [friction] @01KFDPKZ (1/20/2026 by @claude)\n    Shadow branch worktree gets removed when checking out branches. After merging PR and checking out main, .kspec/ worktree was removed. Attempting to recreate with 'git worktree add .kspec kspec-meta' created nested .kspec/.kspec/ worktree breaking kspec. Fix required absolute paths and manual cleanup. Root cause: git worktree add interprets paths relative to CWD which may be inside .kspec. Consider: (1) kspec commands that detect missing worktree and auto-repair (2) fix spawn error in 'kspec shadow repair' (3) prevent nested worktree creation.\n  [success] @01KFD2YY (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Systematic debugging approach worked well: (1) Verified git clone works standalone, (2) Added debug output to trace actual parameters, (3) Discovered output was empty not error, (4) Checked test helper return types, (5) Found pattern in working tests. Each step narrowed the problem space until root cause was clear.\n  [friction] @01KFD2YS (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Optional positional parameter order in CLI commands can create usability issues. [source] [dest] signature made it impossible to provide dest and default source. Changed to [dest] [source] which better matches common usage patterns. Consider: use named options (--source, --dest) for commands where both parameters are truly optional and order-independent.\n  [success] @01KFD2YK (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Test helper pattern clarity - integration.test.ts showed the correct pattern of importing kspecOutput as kspec for string output. When tests fail due to type mismatches (expecting string, getting object), check imports against working test files.\n  [success] @01KFD1TC (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Bug investigation pattern: When validation passes unexpectedly, check item counts in output. 'Items checked: 3' vs expected 5 revealed traits weren't being loaded. Count mismatches are powerful debugging signals for data loading issues.\n  [success] @01KFD0VX (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Merged PR #104 (trait CLI commands) successfully. Review subagent found 0 blocking issues, 100% AC coverage (8/8), all tests passing. Production-quality implementation with proper isolation and AC annotations.\n  [success] @01KFD0DW (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review feedback loop worked well: (1) Subagent identified test quality issues with clear MUST-FIX verdict, (2) Systematic debugging traced root causes (YAML structure, slug conflicts, missing deps), (3) Fixed all 12 test failures, (4) All 695 tests pass locally, (5) PR updated and CI re-running. The strict review criteria (E2E tests, temp dirs, AC coverage, no fluff) ensured high quality implementation.\n  [success] @01KFD0A8 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Systematic test debugging approach worked well: (1) Run tests to see failures, (2) Isolate root cause through manual reproduction, (3) Fix fixtures to match actual kspec requirements, (4) Verify all tests pass. Using manual CLI invocations in temp dirs helped identify structural issues that weren't obvious from test code alone.\n  [success] @01KFCZ4S (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Used dedicated review subagent to identify critical schema bug in PR #104. The subagent's strict review caught that ManifestSchema was missing the traits field, causing Zod to strip traits during parsing. This was a blocking bug that made all functionality fail. Fixed the issue and verified through extensive manual testing. Pattern: Always use review subagents before merge, they catch bugs that aren't obvious from code inspection alone.\n  [success] @01KFCYH1 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Trait CLI implementation followed clean separation: parser infrastructure (TYPE_TO_CHILD_FIELD, NESTED_ITEM_FIELDS) before CLI commands. This pattern worked well - infrastructure changes enabled CLI to use existing patterns (addChildItem, buildIndexes) without special cases. E2E tests caught integration issues early (traits not loading from manifest).\n  [friction] @01KFCXYZ (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    CI checks can take 3-4 minutes to complete, which can consume a significant portion of an iteration when waiting for merge approval. Consider: (1) Starting CI early if possible, (2) Using wait time productively for reflection/documentation, (3) Maybe implementing a timeout strategy in Ralph Loop for when CI takes too long.\n  [success] @01KFCXY2 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Full PR review → fix → merge cycle completed successfully in one iteration. The Ralph Loop workflow of dedicating review subagent, fixing issues, waiting for CI, and merging is working smoothly. Key: strict review criteria and blocking on CI checks before merge.\n  [success] @01KFCXQH (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review workflow caught test quality issues before merge - the automated subagent review identified fluff tests and missing documentation that would have degraded test quality patterns in the codebase. The strict review criteria (MUST-FIX vs suggestions) worked well.\n  [success] @01KFCXGB (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Trait AC coverage validation implementation went smoothly by building on existing infrastructure. The TraitIndex and checkCompleteness() architecture made it straightforward to add inherited AC checking - just scan trait ACs the same way we scan direct ACs. The existing test coverage scanning and ref validation already handled most of the heavy lifting. Clean separation of concerns paid off.\n  [success] @01KFCX5D (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Systematic debugging approach: When implementation code looks correct but tests fail, investigate test infrastructure first. Created isolated debug scripts to replicate exact test scenario, which revealed YAML parser only reading first document. This pattern of creating minimal reproduction scripts in scratchpad is valuable for debugging test failures.\n  [success] @01KFCVFM (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Trait display implementation completed efficiently by reusing TraitIndex foundation. Integrating into buildIndexes() made trait data available throughout CLI. Text and JSON output handled cleanly with separate formatting logic. Test fixtures required valid ULIDs - good validation caught this early.\n  [success] @01KFCTR3 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Following established code patterns (ReferenceIndex, AlignmentIndex) made TraitIndex implementation straightforward. The bidirectional mapping pattern and constructor-based indexing approach worked well for trait-spec relationships. Test structure with AC annotations provides clear traceability.\n  [success] @01KFCTE1 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Ralph loop PR review workflow proved effective: dedicated review subagent correctly identified missing E2E tests as BLOCKING issue, prompted fix, re-review confirmed resolution, and merge completed successfully. The strict review criteria (AC coverage, E2E priority, test isolation) caught important gaps before merge.\n  [success] @01KFCSQF (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Traits field validation implementation followed clean pattern from existing ref validation. Adding to REF_FIELDS array and extending validateRefs() was straightforward. The test pattern from parser.test.ts made writing comprehensive tests easy. All 5 ACs covered in 6 tests.\n  [success] @01KFCS35 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Test-first debugging workflow effective: Added debug output to failing tests to identify root cause (invalid ULID format), then fixed and removed debug code. Faster than guessing.\n  [success] @01KFCR87 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    YAML migration task was straightforward - clear scope, no dependencies, simple find-replace pattern. This type of refactoring task is ideal for automation: well-defined changes, easy to verify with tests, low risk.\n  [success] @01KFCQZK (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    PR review workflow with dedicated subagent worked well - caught missing test coverage before merge. Strict review guidelines (AC coverage required, no fluff tests, E2E preferred) ensure quality standards are maintained across iterations.\n  [success] @01KFCQ0S (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    PR workflow (#96 → #97) demonstrates smooth iteration loop: review existing PR → merge → pick next task → implement → submit → create PR. The pending_review state effectively gates work and ensures each PR gets proper review before merge. Task @01KFC1GKG scope was well-defined (foundation only) which made it easy to complete in one iteration.\n  [success] @01KFCPF1 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Exit code fixes completed efficiently using TodoWrite to track all 6 individual changes. The todo list helped ensure no fixes were missed and provided clear progress visibility through the task.\n  [success] @01KFC3C2 (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Task notes template for standalone implementation context worked well: Scope (what this task covers), Prerequisites (dependencies), Files (to modify/create), Patterns (code examples), Tests (where to add). Prevents 'see parent for context' band-aids.\n  [success] @01KFC3BV (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Subagent review of spec caught 3 blocking issues (AC collision, undefined JSON structure, display contradiction) that would have caused implementation problems. Pattern: review specs with critical eye before finalizing, not just for completeness but for internal consistency and edge cases.\n  [friction] @01KFC3BM (1/19/2026 by @claude)\n    Plan mode required multiple back-and-forth cycles. Missing upfront: output type (spec vs code), standalone requirements check, display/UX needs. Review subagent ran AFTER trying to exit plan mode instead of as a step before finalizing. Suggests plan mode needs structured phases or checklist.\n\n--- Session Metadata ---\n  Last updated: just now\n\n--- Quick Commands ---\n  kspec task start @01KHF2BK  # begin work\n  kspec triage inbox  # triage untriaged inbox items\n\n","status":"completed"}}